window.GUIDE={"slug":"classical-myth","name":"Classical Myth in Art","base":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto","img":"/study/art-history/classical-myth/img/","parts":[{"h":1,"title":"The Nature of Myth","wk":1,"section":"Myth and Its World"},{"h":2,"title":"The Cultural Context","wk":1,"section":"Myth and Its World"},{"h":3,"title":"Creation and the Rise of Zeus","wk":2,"section":"The Beginning of the World"},{"h":4,"title":"The Origins of Mortals","wk":2,"section":"The Beginning of the World"},{"h":5,"title":"Zeus, Hera, Poseidon, and Apollo","wk":3,"section":"The Olympians"},{"h":6,"title":"Hermes, Hephaestus, Ares, and the Goddesses","wk":3,"section":"The Olympians"},{"h":9,"title":"Demeter and Dionysus","wk":4,"section":"Fertility and Death"},{"h":10,"title":"Myths of Death","wk":4,"section":"Fertility and Death"},{"h":11,"title":"Perseus and the Heroes of Argos","wk":5,"section":"Heroes of Argos"},{"h":12,"title":"Heracles","wk":5,"section":"Heroes of Argos"},{"h":13,"title":"Theseus and Crete","wk":6,"section":"Heroes of Athens and Thebes"},{"h":14,"title":"Thebes, Oedipus, and Jason","wk":6,"section":"Heroes of Athens and Thebes"},{"h":15,"title":"The Trojan War","wk":7,"section":"Troy and the Journey Home"},{"h":16,"title":"The Odyssey","wk":7,"section":"Troy and the Journey Home"},{"h":17,"title":"Aeneas and the Founding of Rome","wk":8,"section":"Rome and the Meaning of Myth"},{"h":18,"title":"Interpreting Myth","wk":8,"section":"Rome and the Meaning of Myth"}],"items":[{"id":"start","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/start.html","h":0,"kind":"intro","label":"About this site","title":"About this site","artist":"","thumb":null,"text":"About this site"},{"id":"h1-0","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/part-1.html","h":1,"kind":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"The Nature of Myth","artist":"","thumb":"h1_council","text":"The Nature of Myth · What myth is, the kinds of myth, and the Olympian gods · A myth is a traditional story that a whole community treats as important. It has a plot, characters in conflict, and a setting in the distant past, and it was passed along by word of mouth long before anyone wrote it down. Because every teller changed the details, Greek myths never had one official version. This section introduces the main kinds of myth and the twelve Olympian gods who drive most of the stories that later artists painted and carved. · Myth = story with a plot, conflict, and a far-off past · Three kinds: divine myth, legend, folktale · Greek gods kept by Rome under Latin names · Myth · A traditional story with importance for a whole community, not just one teller; the subject of every work on this site. · Mythos · The Greek word behind “myth,” first meaning an authoritative spoken account rather than a made-up tale. · Pantheon · The full set of a culture’s gods taken as a group, such as the Greek Olympians. · Divine myth · A story whose main actors are gods and other supernatural beings, set before human time (for example, the war of gods and Giants). · Legend (saga) · A story about human heroes, often nobles, set in real places such as Troy or Athens; it works like ancient history. · Folktale · A story about ordinary people or talking animals told to entertain and teach social rules, like Aesop’s fables. · Oral tradition · Passing stories along by memory and performance; it explains why the same myth survives in different versions. · Giovanni Jacopo Caraglio after Raphael · Assembly of the Gods · engraving, 16th century"},{"id":"h1-1","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h1-1.html","h":1,"kind":"theme","label":"Myths explain the world","title":"Myths explain the world","artist":"","thumb":"h1_hades","text":"Myths explain the world · Many myths were the ancient answer to questions that science answers today. · Why do volcanoes rumble? Because Zeus pinned the monster Typhon under Mount Etna. Why does winter come? Because Hades carried Persephone to the underworld and her mother Demeter grieves while she is gone. Stories like these, which explain how something came to be, are called etiological myths, and they run through almost every story in this guide. · Etiology = explanation of a cause or origin · Seasons: Persephone’s stay with Hades · Volcanoes: Typhon trapped under Etna · Etiological tale · A myth that explains the origin of a natural event, custom, or ritual; many divine myths do this."},{"id":"h1-2","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h1-2.html","h":1,"kind":"theme","label":"No single true version","title":"No single true version","artist":"","thumb":"h1_hermes","text":"No single true version · Every poet, playwright, and vase painter retold a myth his own way. · Greek myths circulated orally for centuries before Homer and Hesiod wrote versions down around 700 BCE. Later poets, playwrights, sculptors, and painters changed names, motives, and endings to suit their audiences. There was no sacred book and no church to decide which version was correct, so comparing versions is part of studying myth, and images are often our only source for a version no text preserves. · Written down first in the Archaic period (700s BCE) · No authoritative text or priesthood · Vase paintings preserve their own versions"},{"id":"h1_zeus","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h1_zeus.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"Jupiter of Smyrna","title":"Jupiter of Smyrna","artist":"","thumb":"h1_zeus","text":"Jupiter of Smyrna · Roman, 2nd century CE · Marble · Louvre, Paris · A Roman statue of the chief god, found at Smyrna (modern İzmir) in 1680 and sent to Louis XIV; the arm with the thunderbolt and the eagle are later restorations. · Zeus, called Jupiter by the Romans, is the king of the gods and the god of the sky and thunder. He is the youngest son of the Titans Cronus and Rhea and became ruler after overthrowing his father. As judge of oaths and guest-friendship he stands for order and justice, yet the myths also give him more lovers than any other god, which explains the many demigod heroes. The sculptor shows him the standard way: a mature bearded man, bare-chested, with a scepter and thunderbolt. · King of the gods; sky, weather, thunder · Attributes: thunderbolt, eagle, scepter, oak · Brother-husband of Hera"},{"id":"h1_hera","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h1_hera.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"Juno and Argus","title":"Juno and Argus","artist":"Peter Paul Rubens","thumb":"h1_hera","text":"Juno and Argus · Peter Paul Rubens · ca. 1610–11 · Oil on canvas · Wallraf-Richartz-Museum, Cologne · A large showpiece painted in Antwerp soon after Rubens returned from eight years in Italy, where he had studied ancient sculpture and Venetian color. · Hera, the Roman Juno, is queen of the gods and goddess of marriage and the family. She is Zeus’s sister and wife, and in myth she punishes his lovers and their children, most famously Heracles, whose name means “glory of Hera.” Here she and her messenger Iris take the hundred eyes from the severed head of her watchman Argus to set in the tails of her peacocks, the bird that became her emblem. His body lies in the foreground. · Queen of the gods; marriage and childbirth · Attributes: diadem, scepter, peacock, pomegranate · Pursues Zeus’s lovers and children"},{"id":"h1_poseidon","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h1_poseidon.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"Bronze god from Cape Artemision","title":"Bronze god from Cape Artemision","artist":"","thumb":"h1_poseidon","text":"Bronze god from Cape Artemision · Greek, ca. 460 BCE · Bronze · National Archaeological Museum, Athens · A large sanctuary statue lost in an ancient shipwreck and recovered from the sea in 1926–28; it may have been made as an offering to the god. · Poseidon, the Roman Neptune, rules the sea, earthquakes, and horses, and he is a brother of Zeus and Hades. This bronze once held a weapon that is now lost, so scholars still debate whether it showed Poseidon with his trident or Zeus with a thunderbolt. Either way it shows the Early Classical ideal: a perfectly balanced, mature body caught at the moment before a throw. Bronzes this size rarely survive, because most were melted down for their metal. · God of sea, earthquakes, horses · Attributes: trident, dolphin, horse, bull · Identity debated: Poseidon or Zeus"},{"id":"h1_demeter","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h1_demeter.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"Ceres and Two Nymphs","title":"Ceres and Two Nymphs","artist":"Peter Paul Rubens and Frans Snyders","thumb":"h1_demeter","text":"Ceres and Two Nymphs · Peter Paul Rubens and Frans Snyders · ca. 1620–28 · Oil on canvas · Museo del Prado, Madrid · A workshop collaboration: Rubens painted the figures and his Antwerp colleague Frans Snyders, a specialist in still life, painted the fruit and the cornucopia. · Demeter, the Roman Ceres, is the goddess of grain, farming, and the seasons; the word “cereal” comes from her Latin name. Rubens shows her as a calm, full-bodied goddess of plenty, standing with two nymphs who hold a horn of plenty overflowing with fruit. Her daughter Persephone’s yearly return from the underworld brings back the growing season, and Demeter’s rites at Eleusis offered worshippers hope for a better afterlife. · Goddess of grain and harvest · Attributes: sheaf of wheat, torch, cornucopia · Mother of Persephone; center of Eleusinian Mysteries"},{"id":"h1_persephone","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h1_persephone.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"The Return of Persephone","title":"The Return of Persephone","artist":"Frederic Leighton","thumb":"h1_persephone","text":"The Return of Persephone · Frederic Leighton · 1891 · Oil on canvas · Leeds Art Gallery · An exhibition painting by the president of the Royal Academy, made for a Victorian audience that loved classical subjects. · Persephone, the Roman Proserpina, is Demeter’s daughter and became queen of the underworld after Hades carried her off. Because she had eaten pomegranate seeds there, she must return to him for part of each year, and her absence explains winter. Leighton paints the moment Hermes lifts her out of the dark toward her mother, who reaches up in the sunlight. The story turns a family’s grief into the cycle of the seasons. · Queen of the underworld; goddess of spring · Pomegranate seeds bind her to Hades · Her yearly return explains the seasons"},{"id":"h1_athena","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h1_athena.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"Minerva Expelling the Vices from the Garden of Virtue","title":"Minerva Expelling the Vices from the Garden of Virtue","artist":"Andrea Mantegna","thumb":"h1_athena","text":"Minerva Expelling the Vices from the Garden of Virtue · Andrea Mantegna · 1499–1502 · Tempera on canvas · Louvre, Paris · Painted for the studiolo, the private study, of Isabella d’Este in Mantua, one of a set of mythological paintings that turned ancient gods into lessons about virtue. · Athena, the Roman Minerva, is the virgin goddess of wisdom, crafts such as weaving, and the planning side of war. She was born fully armed from Zeus’s head after he swallowed her mother, the Titaness Metis (“cleverness”). Mantegna shows her in helmet and armor, charging into a garden to drive out a crowd of vices, among them Venus standing on a centaur. Athens, named for her, saw her as its protector. · Wisdom, crafts, strategic war · Attributes: helmet, spear, aegis with Gorgon head, owl, olive · Patron goddess of Athens"},{"id":"h1_hestia","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h1_hestia.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"Hestia Giustiniani","title":"Hestia Giustiniani","artist":"","thumb":"h1_hestia","text":"Hestia Giustiniani · Roman copy after a Greek original of ca. 470–460 BCE · Marble · Torlonia Collection, Rome · photographed on loan to the Louvre, 2024 · A Roman copy of an Early Classical statue of a standing goddess; the identification as Hestia is traditional rather than certain. · Hestia, the Roman Vesta, is the goddess of the hearth, the fire at the center of every home and city. She has almost no stories of her own, because her role was simply to be present wherever a fire was kept. In Rome the Vestal Virgins tended the city’s sacred flame. Some later lists of the Twelve Olympians drop Hestia and include Dionysus instead. · Goddess of the hearth and household order · Sacred flame; Vestal Virgins in Rome · Sometimes replaced by Dionysus among the Twelve · Twelve Olympians · The main gods who live on Mount Olympus; the lists vary slightly, and Hades, who lives below, is usually left out."},{"id":"h1_dionysus","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h1_dionysus.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"Bacchus and Ariadne","title":"Bacchus and Ariadne","artist":"Titian","thumb":"h1_dionysus","text":"Bacchus and Ariadne · Titian · 1520–23 · Oil on canvas · National Gallery, London · Painted for the private study of Alfonso d’Este, Duke of Ferrara, as part of a series of mythological scenes. · Dionysus, the Roman Bacchus, is the god of wine, ecstasy, and theater, and the only Olympian with a mortal mother, the Theban princess Semele. Titian shows him leaping from his leopard-drawn chariot toward Ariadne, the Cretan princess abandoned by Theseus, whom he marries and later places in the sky as a constellation. His noisy followers, the satyrs and maenads, fill the right side. · Wine, revelry, theater · Attributes: grapevine, ivy, wine cup, leopard · Mother Semele was mortal"},{"id":"h1_apollo","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h1_apollo.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"Apollo Belvedere","title":"Apollo Belvedere","artist":"","thumb":"h1_apollo","text":"Apollo Belvedere · Roman copy, ca. 120–140 CE, after a Greek bronze of ca. 330 BCE · Marble · Vatican Museums · A Roman copy of a famous Greek statue; rediscovered in the late 1400s, it became a model of ideal male beauty for Renaissance and later artists. · Apollo, whose name stayed the same in Latin, is the god of light, music, poetry, prophecy, healing, and archery. He is the son of Zeus and Leto and the twin of Artemis. His oracle at Delphi was the most important in Greece. The statue shows him having just shot an arrow, his bow once in the outstretched left hand. · Light, music, prophecy, archery · Attributes: bow, lyre, laurel · Oracle at Delphi; twin of Artemis"},{"id":"h1_artemis","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h1_artemis.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"Diana and Her Companions","title":"Diana and Her Companions","artist":"Johannes Vermeer","thumb":"h1_artemis","text":"Diana and Her Companions · Johannes Vermeer · ca. 1653–56 · Oil on canvas · Mauritshuis, The Hague · One of Vermeer’s earliest surviving works, painted when he still hoped to be a history painter, before he turned to the quiet interiors he is known for. · Artemis, the Roman Diana, is the virgin goddess of the hunt, wild animals, the moon, and childbirth. Twin sister of Apollo, she lives in the wilderness among nymphs. Vermeer shows her without her bow, resting at dusk while a nymph washes her feet; only the small crescent moon on her brow and the hunting dog identify her. Stories warn of what happens to men who see her bathing, like the hunter Actaeon. · Hunt, wilderness, moon, childbirth · Attributes: bow and quiver, crescent moon, deer, hunting dog · Protector of girls before marriage"},{"id":"h1_ares","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h1_ares.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"Mars","title":"Mars","artist":"Diego Velázquez","thumb":"h1_ares","text":"Mars · Diego Velázquez · ca. 1638 · Oil on canvas · Museo del Prado, Madrid · Painted for the Torre de la Parada, a royal hunting lodge outside Madrid, for King Philip IV of Spain. · Ares, the Roman Mars, is the god of war in its violent, bloody form, unlike Athena, who stands for planning and strategy. The Greeks disliked him, but the Romans honored Mars as the father of Romulus, and the month of March is named for him. Velázquez borrows the seated pose of the ancient Ludovisi Ares but makes him a tired, middle-aged soldier with his armor dropped at his feet, a god of war with no war to fight. · Violent, physical side of war · Attributes: helmet, spear, shield · Lover of Aphrodite; honored by Rome as Mars"},{"id":"h1_aphrodite","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h1_aphrodite.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"The Birth of Venus","title":"The Birth of Venus","artist":"Sandro Botticelli","thumb":"h1_aphrodite","text":"The Birth of Venus · Sandro Botticelli · ca. 1484–86 · Tempera on canvas · Uffizi Gallery, Florence · Probably made for a member of the Medici circle in Florence, as a large painting for a private residence. · Aphrodite, the Roman Venus, is the goddess of love, beauty, and desire. In Hesiod she rises from sea foam after Cronus throws his father Uranus’s severed genitals into the sea, while Homer calls her a daughter of Zeus and Dione. Botticelli shows her arriving on a shell, blown ashore by the winds and met by a goddess of the seasons. She is married to Hephaestus but loves Ares. · Love, beauty, desire · Attributes: dove, rose, myrtle, shell · Born from sea foam (Hesiod)"},{"id":"h1_hephaestus","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h1_hephaestus.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"Apollo in the Forge of Vulcan","title":"Apollo in the Forge of Vulcan","artist":"Diego Velázquez","thumb":"h1_hephaestus","text":"Apollo in the Forge of Vulcan · Diego Velázquez · 1630 · Oil on canvas · Museo del Prado, Madrid · Painted during Velázquez’s first stay in Italy and later acquired for the Spanish royal collection. · Hephaestus, the Roman Vulcan, is the smith and craftsman of the gods, the maker of their weapons and palaces, and the only Olympian with a physical disability. Velázquez shows Apollo arriving in his forge to tell him that his wife, Aphrodite, is having an affair with Ares, and the workers freeze in shock. The word “volcano” comes from Vulcan, whose forge was imagined beneath the earth. · Fire, forge, metalwork · Attributes: hammer, anvil, tongs · Husband of Aphrodite"},{"id":"h1_hermes","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h1_hermes.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"Hermes and the Infant Dionysus","title":"Hermes and the Infant Dionysus","artist":"Attributed to Praxiteles","thumb":"h1_hermes","text":"Hermes and the Infant Dionysus · Attributed to Praxiteles · ca. 340–330 BCE (some scholars date it later) · Marble · Archaeological Museum of Olympia · shown in a 19th-century photograph · A statue dedicated in the Temple of Hera at Olympia, where the traveler Pausanias saw it in the 2nd century CE; excavators found it there in 1877. · Hermes, the Roman Mercury, is the messenger of the gods, the god of travelers, trade, and thieves, and the guide who leads souls to the underworld. He is the son of Zeus and the nymph Maia. Here he carries his baby half-brother Dionysus to the nymphs who will raise him, safe from Hera’s anger, and once dangled a bunch of grapes in his missing right hand. The relaxed S-curve pose and soft, polished surfaces made Praxiteles the most admired sculptor of the 4th century BCE. · Messenger; travelers, trade, thieves · Attributes: caduceus, petasos (traveler’s hat), winged sandals · Leads souls to Hades; protects the infant Dionysus"},{"id":"h1_hadesB","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h1_hadesB.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"Pluto (as Serapis) and Persephone (as Isis) with Cerberus","title":"Pluto (as Serapis) and Persephone (as Isis) with Cerberus","artist":"","thumb":"h1_hadesB","text":"Pluto (as Serapis) and Persephone (as Isis) with Cerberus · Roman, 2nd century CE · Marble · Heraklion Archaeological Museum, Crete · Cult statues from a sanctuary of Egyptian gods at Gortyn on Crete, where Romans worshipped the rulers of the underworld in Egyptian form. · Hades, the Roman Pluto (“the rich one”), rules the underworld and the dead. He is the oldest brother of Zeus and Poseidon but lives below the earth, so he is usually not counted among the Olympians. Here he stands with a scepter beside his three-headed dog Cerberus, next to his queen Persephone. The pair are dressed as the Egyptian gods Serapis and Isis, showing how Roman religion blended Greek and Egyptian gods. · God of the underworld and the dead · Attributes: scepter, Cerberus, helmet of invisibility · Not an Olympian: lives below"},{"id":"h1-18","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h1-18.html","h":1,"kind":"theme","label":"Reading a god by attributes","title":"Reading a god by attributes","artist":"","thumb":"h1_artemis","text":"Reading a god by attributes · Artists name a god by objects and animals, not by a face. · Greek and Roman gods look like ideal humans, so a statue of Apollo and one of an athlete can be almost the same body. What tells viewers who is who is the attribute: a thunderbolt for Zeus, a trident for Poseidon, a bow and deer for Artemis, a caduceus for Hermes. Renaissance and later painters kept the same system, so the code still works across two thousand years of art. · Gods = ideal human bodies · Identity carried by objects and animals · Same code used in Renaissance and later art"},{"id":"h1-19","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h1-19.html","h":1,"kind":"theme","label":"Why Rome kept the Greek gods","title":"Why Rome kept the Greek gods","artist":"","thumb":"h1_zeus","text":"Why Rome kept the Greek gods · Rome matched its own gods to the Greek ones and gave them Roman jobs. · Rome came into contact with Greek culture through trade and the Greek cities of southern Italy, and it matched its own deities to the Greek ones: Jupiter to Zeus, Juno to Hera, Minerva to Athena. The Romans kept Greek stories and copied Greek statues, as many works on this site show, but reorganized the gods around the needs of the Roman state. The three most important became the Capitoline Triad, worshipped together on the Capitoline Hill. · Roman gods matched to Greek counterparts · Greek statues copied for Roman settings · State religion centered on the Capitoline Triad · Capitoline Triad · Jupiter, Juno, and Minerva, the three chief gods of Roman state religion, honored together in the great temple on Rome’s Capitoline Hill."},{"id":"h2-0","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/part-2.html","h":2,"kind":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"The Cultural Context","artist":"","thumb":"h2_throne","text":"The Cultural Context · The Greek world that produced the myths · Greek myths took shape over two thousand years of history, from the Bronze Age palaces of Crete and Mycenae to the Hellenistic kingdoms that followed Alexander the Great. A rocky, mountainous land pushed the Greeks toward the sea, trade, and independent city-states. The myths also carry older ideas from Mesopotamia, and they were first written down in the Archaic period by poets who looked back on a lost heroic age. · Historical periods: Minoan → Mycenaean → Dark Age → Archaic → Classical → Hellenistic · Mountains and sea → trade, colonies, separate city-states · Near Eastern roots; written down ca. 700 BCE · Greek historical periods · Early/Middle Bronze Age (Minoan) 3000–1600 BCE; Late Bronze Age (Mycenaean) 1600–1150; Dark Age 1150–800; Archaic 800–480; Classical 480–323; Hellenistic 323–31 BCE. · Greek art periods · Geometric 900–700 BCE; Orientalizing 700–600; Archaic 600–480; Early Classical 480–450; High Classical 450–400; Late Classical 400–323; Hellenistic 323–31 BCE. · Indo-Europeans · Peoples speaking related languages, Greek among them, who spread from Central Asia across Europe; their societies were ranked into rulers and priests, warriors, and food producers. · Throne Room, Palace of Knossos, Crete · Minoan"},{"id":"h2_knossos_b","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h2_knossos_b.html","h":2,"kind":"work","label":"Palace of Knossos, North Entrance","title":"Palace of Knossos, North Entrance","artist":"","thumb":"h2_knossos_b","text":"Palace of Knossos, North Entrance · Minoan, ca. 1700–1450 BCE · Knossos, Crete · columns and bull fresco reconstructed by Arthur Evans · The center of a Bronze Age state: royal residence, storehouse, workshop, and ritual space, built without defensive walls. · The Minoans of Crete, named by the archaeologist Arthur Evans after the mythical King Minos, built huge palace complexes with no fortification walls. The maze of rooms at Knossos may lie behind the myth of the Labyrinth, and the bull images that decorated it echo the story of the Minotaur. The Minoans wrote in a script called Linear A that is still undeciphered. · Minoan Crete: palaces without walls · Possible source of Minos, Labyrinth, Minotaur myths · Bull imagery; Linear A script"},{"id":"h2_atreus","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h2_atreus.html","h":2,"kind":"work","label":"“Treasury of Atreus”","title":"“Treasury of Atreus”","artist":"","thumb":"h2_atreus","text":"“Treasury of Atreus” · Mycenaean, ca. 1250 BCE · Stone tholos tomb · Mycenae, Greece · A royal tomb: a long open passage (dromos) leads to a domed, beehive-shaped burial chamber (tholos). · The Mycenaeans were the Greek-speaking Bronze Age culture of the mainland, ruling from walled citadels such as Mycenae and Tiryns. Heinrich Schliemann, who excavated here in the 1870s, believed he had found the world of Homer’s king Agamemnon, and this tomb still carries the name of Agamemnon’s father. The Mycenaeans kept palace accounts in Linear B, an early form of Greek, but did not use writing for stories. · Mycenaean Greece: fortified citadels · Linked by tradition to Agamemnon’s family · Linear B used for records, not myth · Tholos · A beehive-shaped stone tomb for Mycenaean rulers. · Dromos · The long entrance passage leading into a tholos tomb."},{"id":"h2-3","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h2-3.html","h":2,"kind":"theme","label":"Bronze Age ruins behind the myths","title":"Bronze Age ruins behind the myths","artist":"","thumb":"h2_throne","text":"Bronze Age ruins behind the myths · Heroic myths remember, loosely, a real world that had vanished. · Around 1150 BCE the Mycenaean palaces collapsed, writing disappeared, and Greece entered a Dark Age. When poets later told stories of Minos, Agamemnon, and Troy, they were describing a grand past they knew only from ruins and inherited songs. Archaeology has since shown that places like Knossos, Mycenae, and Troy were real, even if the stories told about them are not history. · Palace collapse ca. 1150 BCE · Dark Age: loss of writing · Myths set in real, ruined places · Dark Age · The period after the Mycenaean collapse (ca. 1150–800 BCE) marked by depopulation and the loss of writing."},{"id":"h2_krater","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h2_krater.html","h":2,"kind":"work","label":"Funerary krater","title":"Funerary krater","artist":"Attributed to the Hirschfeld Workshop","thumb":"h2_krater","text":"Funerary krater · Attributed to the Hirschfeld Workshop · Late Geometric, ca. 750–735 BCE · Terracotta · Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A grave marker for an Athenian aristocrat: liquid offerings could be poured through its base, and its scenes show the funeral itself. · In the Geometric period Greek potters covered vases with bands of zigzags and meanders, and then added stick-like human figures. This huge krater shows the dead man laid out on a bier surrounded by mourners, with a procession of chariots and warriors below. Scenes like these appear just as the Greek alphabet was being adopted, around the time Homer’s poems took shape. · Geometric style: bands of pattern, stick figures · Funeral scene and chariot procession · Era of the new Greek alphabet · Krater · A large bowl for mixing wine with water; giant ones served as grave markers in the Geometric period."},{"id":"h2_kouros","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h2_kouros.html","h":2,"kind":"work","label":"Marble statue of a kouros (youth)","title":"Marble statue of a kouros (youth)","artist":"","thumb":"h2_kouros","text":"Marble statue of a kouros (youth) · Archaic, ca. 590–580 BCE · Marble · Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A grave marker for a young Athenian man, showing him as an ideal youth rather than a portrait. · Kouroi (singular kouros) are standing nude youths, left foot forward and arms at their sides, a pose borrowed from Egyptian sculpture. This is one of the earliest surviving Athenian marble statues. Over the next century Greek sculptors gradually loosened this stiff pose into the natural, balanced bodies of the Classical period, a change that happened unusually fast. · Egyptian-inspired pose, left foot forward · Ideal youth, not a portrait · Start of fast change toward Classical naturalism · Kouros · An Archaic Greek statue of a standing nude young man, used as a grave marker or an offering to a god."},{"id":"h2_hoplite","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h2_hoplite.html","h":2,"kind":"work","label":"Neck-amphora with a Greek warrior and a Persian","title":"Neck-amphora with a Greek warrior and a Persian","artist":"","thumb":"h2_hoplite","text":"Neck-amphora with a Greek warrior and a Persian · Athenian, early 5th century BCE · Red-figure terracotta · Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A storage jar for wine or oil; scenes of Greeks defeating Persians were popular after the Persian Wars. · A Greek hoplite with a round shield and spear attacks a Persian archer in trousers. Between 490 and 479 BCE the Greek city-states defeated two Persian invasions at battles including Marathon, Thermopylae, and Salamis. The victory gave Greeks a shared identity defined against “barbarian” Asia, an idea that runs through Classical art and its use of myth. · Persian Wars, 490–479 BCE · Greek vs. Persian = civilization vs. “other” · Hoplite armor: helmet, round shield, spear · Hoplite · A heavily armed Greek citizen-soldier who fought shoulder to shoulder in a phalanx, replacing the one-on-one duels of Homeric heroes. · Hellenes · The Greeks’ own name for themselves, from Hellas; “Greek” comes from the Romans."},{"id":"h2_leonidas","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h2_leonidas.html","h":2,"kind":"work","label":"Leonidas at Thermopylae","title":"Leonidas at Thermopylae","artist":"Jacques-Louis David","thumb":"h2_leonidas","text":"Leonidas at Thermopylae · Jacques-Louis David · 1814 · Oil on canvas · Louvre, Paris · A large history painting meant to show patriotic self-sacrifice, finished in the last months of Napoleon’s empire. · In 480 BCE King Leonidas of Sparta and a small force held the narrow pass of Thermopylae against the Persian army before being killed. David shows Leonidas calmly seated among his men just before the final battle. The painting shows how later Europe treated Greek history itself almost as myth, a story of heroic virtue. · Thermopylae, 480 BCE · Spartan sacrifice as heroic model · Greek history used as moral example"},{"id":"h2_pericles","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h2_pericles.html","h":2,"kind":"work","label":"Bust of Pericles","title":"Bust of Pericles","artist":"After Kresilas","thumb":"h2_pericles","text":"Bust of Pericles · After Kresilas · Roman copy of a Greek original of ca. 430 BCE · Marble · Vatican Museums (Museo Chiaramonti) · A Roman copy of the honorary portrait set up on the Athenian Acropolis. · Pericles led Athens at its height in the mid-400s BCE, when the Parthenon was built and drama, history, and philosophy flourished. He wears a general’s helmet, pushed up to show his face. Athens was a democracy, but only free adult male citizens could vote, and the city’s wealth rested partly on slaves and on an empire of allied cities. · Leader of Classical Athens · Helmet = office of general · Democracy limited to male citizens · Polis (pl. poleis) · An independent Greek city-state with its own laws, army, and gods, such as Athens or Corinth. · Demos · The citizen body of a polis: free men who owned property, spoke and voted in the assembly, and fought in its wars. · Tyrants · Rulers who took power outside the old aristocracy (ca. 650–500 BCE), often with commercial backing; the bad sense of the word comes from aristocratic writers."},{"id":"h2_alexmosaic","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h2_alexmosaic.html","h":2,"kind":"work","label":"Alexander Mosaic (Battle of Issus)","title":"Alexander Mosaic (Battle of Issus)","artist":"","thumb":"h2_alexmosaic","text":"Alexander Mosaic (Battle of Issus) · Roman, ca. 100 BCE, after a Greek painting of ca. 315 BCE · Floor mosaic from the House of the Faun, Pompeii · National Archaeological Museum, Naples · A floor mosaic of more than a million tiny stones made for one of the grandest houses in Pompeii, copying a lost Greek painting. · Alexander III of Macedon (356–323 BCE) conquered the Persian Empire and carried Greek culture to Egypt and India. At left he charges bareheaded on his horse Bucephalus, while the Persian king Darius turns in his chariot to flee. Alexander’s death in 323 BCE begins the Hellenistic period, when Greek kingdoms spread Greek art and myth across the eastern Mediterranean. · Conquered Persia, 334–323 BCE · Alexander vs. Darius at Issus (333 BCE) · Start of the Hellenistic period"},{"id":"h2_symposium","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h2_symposium.html","h":2,"kind":"work","label":"Symposium scene","title":"Symposium scene","artist":"Brygos Painter","thumb":"h2_symposium","text":"Symposium scene · Brygos Painter · Athenian, ca. 490–480 BCE · Red-figure cup (interior) · British Museum, London · A drinking cup used at a symposium; the picture inside was revealed as the drinker emptied it. · A reclining young man hands a pair of pipes to a female musician. The symposium was an all-male evening of drinking, poetry, song, and conversation, and it was one of the main places where myths were retold and where painted cups showing them were used. The women present were paid entertainers, not wives. · All-male drinking party · Setting for poetry, song, and myth · Cups decorated for this use · Symposium · An all-male drinking party with poetry, music, and talk; a key setting for retelling myths. · Hetairai · Educated female companions paid to entertain men with music and conversation at symposia. · Aoidoi, rhapsodes · Aoidoi were singer-poets who composed myths in performance; rhapsodes later recited memorized epic poetry. · Pedagogue · The enslaved attendant who supervised a boy’s schooling, roughly from age 6 to 13. · Pederasty · A socially recognized relationship between an adult citizen man and an adolescent boy, framed as mentorship, common in aristocratic Athens."},{"id":"h2_prothesis","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h2_prothesis.html","h":2,"kind":"work","label":"Krater fragment with a prothesis","title":"Krater fragment with a prothesis","artist":"Dipylon Master","thumb":"h2_prothesis","text":"Krater fragment with a prothesis · Dipylon Master · Late Geometric, ca. 760–750 BCE · Terracotta · Louvre, Paris · Part of a grave-marker vase from Athens’ Dipylon cemetery; the prothesis was the laying-out of the body for mourning. · Women lead the mourning around the dead, raising their hands to their heads. Greek women spent most of their lives in the household, but birth and death, the two events that brought ritual pollution, were their responsibility. The myths repeatedly define women by the stages of their lives, from unmarried girl to bride to mother. · Women lead the mourning · Birth and death handled by women · Life stages: girl → bride → mother · Gynaikeion · The women’s quarters of a Greek house, where women spun and wove and young children were raised. · Parthenos · An unmarried girl of marriageable age, linked with the wild goddess Artemis. · Nymphe · A bride or newly married woman. · Gyne · A grown, married woman, a status fully reached through childbirth. · Miasma · Ritual pollution caused by events such as birth, death, and bloodshed, which required purification."},{"id":"h2-12","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h2-12.html","h":2,"kind":"theme","label":"Myth in a man’s world","title":"Myth in a man’s world","artist":"","thumb":"h2_symposium","text":"Myth in a man’s world · Most of what survives was written by and for elite men. · Almost all of our sources come from aristocratic men in Athens in the 400s and 300s BCE. Myths were performed at their symposia, paid for by their families, and shaped by their views of war, honor, and women. Reading the myths with this in mind helps explain why heroes are male warriors and why women in the stories are so often dangerous, silent, or blamed. · Sources: elite Athenian men · Performed at symposia and festivals · Shapes how women appear in myth"},{"id":"h2_uruk","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h2_uruk.html","h":2,"kind":"work","label":"Female head from Uruk (possibly Inanna)","title":"Female head from Uruk (possibly Inanna)","artist":"","thumb":"h2_uruk","text":"Female head from Uruk (possibly Inanna) · Sumerian, ca. 3200–3000 BCE · Marble · Iraq Museum, Baghdad · Probably the face of a cult statue from the temple precinct of Inanna at Uruk; the eyes and brows once held colored inlays. · Each Sumerian city had its own protecting god, who lived in a temple at the city’s center. Inanna, the “Queen of Heaven,” was the goddess of love and war, and Greek writers’ Aphrodite shares some of her traits. Many of the patterns of Greek myth, such as generations of gods and a great flood, appear first in Mesopotamia. · Sumerian city gods · Inanna: love and war · Mesopotamian roots of Greek myth · Sumerian gods · An (sky, first ruler), Inanna (love and war), Enlil (storms and rule), Enki (fresh water and wisdom), Ki (earth), and Ereshkigal (queen of the underworld)."},{"id":"h2_naramsin","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h2_naramsin.html","h":2,"kind":"work","label":"Victory Stele of Naram-Sin","title":"Victory Stele of Naram-Sin","artist":"","thumb":"h2_naramsin","text":"Victory Stele of Naram-Sin · Akkadian, ca. 2254–2218 BCE · Sandstone · Louvre, Paris · A public monument celebrating a royal victory; it was carried off to Susa as war booty more than a thousand years later. · The Akkadian king Naram-Sin climbs a mountain over defeated enemies, wearing the horned crown that marks a god. When the Semitic-speaking Akkadians conquered Sumer around 2300 BCE, they took over the Sumerian gods under new names and passed them to the Babylonians. That chain of borrowing is how Near Eastern stories reached the Greeks. · King shown with a god’s horned crown · Akkadians adopted Sumerian gods · Ideas passed on to Babylon and beyond"},{"id":"h2-15","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h2-15.html","h":2,"kind":"theme","label":"Myth’s roots in the Near East","title":"Myth’s roots in the Near East","artist":"","thumb":"h2_uruk","text":"Myth’s roots in the Near East · Greek myth grew out of much older Mesopotamian traditions. · Sumerians, Akkadians, and Babylonians told of gods with human passions, generations of gods overthrowing one another, and a flood sent to destroy humankind. Greek stories borrowed and reshaped these patterns, just as Rome would later borrow from Greece. The Hebrew Bible draws on the same Mesopotamian stock, which is why Genesis and Greek myth sometimes look alike. · Sumer → Akkad → Babylon → Greece · Shared patterns: divine generations, flood · Genesis draws on the same sources"},{"id":"h2-16","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h2-16.html","h":2,"kind":"text","label":"Where the stories come from","title":"Where the stories come from","artist":"","thumb":null,"text":"Where the stories come from · Greek myths were told for centuries before Homer and Hesiod wrote versions down around 700 BCE. Athenian tragedy reworked them for the stage in the 400s BCE, and Roman poets gave them their most influential forms. The works below are the main sources later artists read. · The main written sources · Homer and Hesiod: earliest written versions (ca. 700 BCE) · Tragedy: myth staged in Classical Athens · Virgil and Ovid: the Roman retellings artists used most · Homer, Iliad and Odyssey · Epic poems; the Iliad covers weeks in the tenth year of the Trojan War, the Odyssey Odysseus’s ten-year voyage home. · Hesiod, Theogony and Works and Days · The Theogony tells the birth of the gods and Zeus’s rise; Works and Days gives advice on life and includes Prometheus, Pandora, and the Five Ages. · Tragedy: Aeschylus, Sophocles, Euripides · Athenian playwrights who staged myths at festivals of Dionysus; Aeschylus stresses the gods, Sophocles fate, Euripides human passion. · Virgil, Aeneid (29–19 BCE) · Roman epic written under Augustus, tracing Aeneas from fallen Troy to Italy and Rome’s founding. · Ovid, Metamorphoses (8 CE) · A long Roman poem retelling hundreds of myths of transformation; the single most-used source for later artists."},{"id":"h3-0","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/part-3.html","h":3,"kind":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"Creation and the Rise of Zeus","artist":"","thumb":"h3_titans","text":"Creation and the Rise of Zeus · From Chaos to the rule of the Olympians · Hesiod’s Theogony (“birth of the gods”) tells how the world began with Chaos and how three generations of gods fought for control of it. Sky-god Uranus is overthrown by his son Cronus, and Cronus by his son Zeus. Zeus then has to defeat the Titans, the monster Typhon, and the Giants before the Olympians rule for good and divide the world among themselves. · Chaos → Earth → Sky → Titans → Olympians · Three cosmic wars secure Zeus’s rule · World divided: sky, sea, underworld · Chaos · The first thing to exist in Hesiod: a yawning gap or void, not disorder. · Gaea (Gaia) · Earth, who comes from Chaos and gives birth to the Sky and the Sea, then mother of the Titans and many monsters. · Uranus · The Sky, son and husband of Gaea; overthrown by his son Cronus. · Tartarus · The deepest pit beneath the earth, where defeated gods and monsters are locked away. · Cornelis van Haarlem · The Fall of the Titans · 1588–90"},{"id":"h3_uranus","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h3_uranus.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"The Mutilation of Uranus by Saturn","title":"The Mutilation of Uranus by Saturn","artist":"Giorgio Vasari and Cristofano Gherardi","thumb":"h3_uranus","text":"The Mutilation of Uranus by Saturn · Giorgio Vasari and Cristofano Gherardi · ca. 1556 · Ceiling painting · Sala degli Elementi, Palazzo Vecchio, Florence · Part of a ceiling program for Duke Cosimo I de’ Medici’s palace apartments, using myth to celebrate the elements and, indirectly, the duke. · Uranus hated his children and kept them hidden inside Earth, so Gaea made a great sickle and her youngest Titan son, Cronus, ambushed and castrated his father. Blood falling on the earth produced the Furies and the Giants, and the severed parts thrown into the sea produced Aphrodite. Vasari’s ceiling turns this violent episode into the origin of the elements. · Gaea’s sickle; Cronus attacks Uranus · Blood → Furies, Giants · Sea foam → Aphrodite · Titans · The twelve children of Gaea and Uranus, including Oceanus, Hyperion, Cronus, and Rhea; the generation of gods before the Olympians. · Erinyes (Furies) · Female spirits of vengeance born from Uranus’s blood, who hunt down those who shed family blood. · Giants · Huge, violent sons of Earth born from Uranus’s blood, later defeated by the Olympians."},{"id":"h3_venus","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h3_venus.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"The Birth of Venus","title":"The Birth of Venus","artist":"Alexandre Cabanel","thumb":"h3_venus","text":"The Birth of Venus · Alexandre Cabanel · 1875 · Oil on canvas · Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A smaller version of Cabanel’s famous 1863 Paris Salon painting, made for an American collector. · In Hesiod’s account, Aphrodite is born from the foam that gathered around Uranus’s severed genitals, which is how ancient writers explained her name (aphros means “foam”). Cabanel turns the story into a dreamy academic nude floating on the waves with cupids overhead. The original version was bought by Napoleon III, showing how mythology gave respectable cover to the nude in 19th-century art. · Born from sea foam (aphros) · Academic Salon painting · Myth as cover for the nude"},{"id":"h3_phaeton","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h3_phaeton.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"The Fall of Phaeton","title":"The Fall of Phaeton","artist":"Peter Paul Rubens","thumb":"h3_phaeton","text":"The Fall of Phaeton · Peter Paul Rubens · ca. 1604–08 · Oil on canvas · National Gallery of Art, Washington · An early large mythological painting from Rubens’s years in Rome; its first owner is not recorded. · The Titan Hyperion fathered the sun (Helios), the moon (Selene), and the dawn (Eos). Helios’s son Phaeton begged to drive the sun’s chariot for one day, lost control, and scorched the earth until Zeus struck him down with a thunderbolt. Ancient writers used the story to explain deserts and why some peoples had dark skin. Rubens shows the moment the horses scatter and the hours of the day flee in panic. · Hyperion → Helios, Selene, Eos · Phaeton loses the sun chariot · Etiological story of scorched lands · Hyperion · A Titan, father of the sun Helios, the moon Selene, and the dawn Eos. · Phaeton · Son of Helios who wrecked the sun’s chariot and was killed by Zeus’s thunderbolt."},{"id":"h3_saturn","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h3_saturn.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"Saturn","title":"Saturn","artist":"Peter Paul Rubens","thumb":"h3_saturn","text":"Saturn · Peter Paul Rubens · 1636–38 · Oil on canvas · Museo del Prado, Madrid · One of dozens of mythological paintings commissioned by Philip IV of Spain for his hunting lodge, the Torre de la Parada. · Cronus, the Roman Saturn, learned that one of his children would overthrow him, so he swallowed each one at birth. His wife Rhea saved the youngest, Zeus, by hiding him on Crete and giving Cronus a stone wrapped in swaddling clothes. Rubens paints an old man biting into a child’s chest, with the stars of the planet Saturn above. Renaissance writers confused Cronus with Chronos, “Time,” which is why he often carries a scythe and appears as Father Time. · Cronus swallows his children · Rhea substitutes a stone for Zeus · Confused with Time (Chronos) · Cronus (Kronos) · Youngest Titan, who overthrew Uranus and swallowed his own children to keep power. · Rhea · Titaness, wife of Cronus and mother of Hestia, Demeter, Hera, Hades, Poseidon, and Zeus. · These great Cronos swallowed as each came forth from the womb to his mother’s knees"},{"id":"h3-5","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h3-5.html","h":3,"kind":"theme","label":"Sons overthrow fathers","title":"Sons overthrow fathers","artist":"","thumb":"h3_goya","text":"Sons overthrow fathers · Power passes by violence from one generation to the next. · Uranus suppresses his children and is overthrown by Cronus; Cronus swallows his children and is overthrown by Zeus. Zeus breaks the cycle by swallowing his first wife Metis before she can bear a son greater than he is, and Athena is born from his head instead. Goya’s later painting of Saturn, made on the wall of his own house, shows how this ancient story could become an image of pure terror. · Uranus → Cronus → Zeus · Each father fears a son · Zeus ends the cycle by swallowing Metis · Metis · Titaness of cleverness, Zeus’s first wife, swallowed by him while pregnant with Athena."},{"id":"h3_titans","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h3_titans.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"The Fall of the Titans","title":"The Fall of the Titans","artist":"Cornelis van Haarlem","thumb":"h3_titans","text":"The Fall of the Titans · Cornelis van Haarlem · 1588–90 · Oil on canvas · Statens Museum for Kunst, Copenhagen · A showpiece of Dutch Mannerism, made to display the painter’s skill with twisting, muscular nudes. · The Titanomachy was a ten-year war between the Titans and Zeus’s Olympians. Zeus won only after freeing the Cyclopes, who forged his thunderbolt, and the Hundred-Handers, who hurled boulders. The defeated Titans were locked in Tartarus, and Atlas was condemned to hold up the sky. Van Haarlem shows the Titans tumbling down in a tangle of bodies. · Titanomachy: ten-year war · Cyclopes and Hundred-Handers help Zeus · Losers imprisoned in Tartarus · Titanomachy · The war between the Titans and the Olympians that made Zeus king. · Cyclopes · Three one-eyed sons of Gaea and Uranus, smiths who forged Zeus’s thunderbolt. · Hecatonchires (Hundred-Handers) · Three giants with fifty heads and a hundred arms each who threw boulders at the Titans."},{"id":"h3_atlas","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h3_atlas.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"Atlas","title":"Atlas","artist":"Guercino","thumb":"h3_atlas","text":"Atlas · Guercino · 1646 · Oil on canvas · Museo Stefano Bardini, Florence · Painted for Lorenzo de’ Medici, of the family that ruled Florence; the Titan with the globe was a favorite subject of seventeenth-century painters. · Atlas, a Titan who fought against Zeus, was punished by being made to hold up the heavens at the western edge of the world. Guercino shows him as a powerful, aging man, straining under a celestial globe marked with the constellations, the form given to the sky on ancient statues like the Farnese Atlas. His name lives on in the word for a book of maps. · Titan punished after the Titanomachy · Holds up the sky · Globe marked with constellations · Atlas · Titan condemned by Zeus to hold the sky on his shoulders forever."},{"id":"h3_chimera","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h3_chimera.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"Bellerophon, Riding Pegasus, Slays the Chimera","title":"Bellerophon, Riding Pegasus, Slays the Chimera","artist":"Peter Paul Rubens","thumb":"h3_chimera","text":"Bellerophon, Riding Pegasus, Slays the Chimera · Peter Paul Rubens · 1635 · Oil on panel · Musée Bonnat-Helleu, Bayonne · A small oil sketch, the kind Rubens painted to work out a design before his workshop made the full-size picture. · Gaea also mated with Pontus, the sea, and her descendants include a line of monsters. The Chimera breathed fire and had a lion’s body, a goat’s head rising from its back, and a snake for a tail. The hero Bellerophon killed it while riding the winged horse Pegasus, which he had tamed with a golden bridle from Athena. Rubens drives the hero down from the sky onto the monster in one diagonal sweep. · Lion, goat, and serpent in one body · Killed by Bellerophon on Pegasus · Oil sketch: a design worked out quickly · Pontus · The sea, born from Gaea alone; through his children she becomes ancestor of many monsters. · Chimera · Fire-breathing monster, part lion, part goat, part serpent, killed by Bellerophon. · Gorgons · Three snake-haired sisters, including Medusa, whose gaze turned people to stone. · Geryon · A three-bodied giant whose cattle Heracles stole, guarded by Orthus. · Orthus · Geryon’s two-headed guard dog. · Cerberus · The many-headed dog guarding the gate of the underworld. · Hydra · Many-headed water serpent that grew new heads when one was cut off, killed by Heracles."},{"id":"h3-9","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h3-9.html","h":3,"kind":"theme","label":"Monsters with borrowed bodies","title":"Monsters with borrowed bodies","artist":"","thumb":"h3_typhon","text":"Monsters with borrowed bodies · Greek monsters mix animal and human parts, an idea borrowed from older art. · Egyptian and Near Eastern art had long combined human and animal parts, often for protective gods. Greek artists borrowed these hybrids, like the sphinx, the griffin, and the winged Harpies, but usually made them threats to be destroyed. Killing a monster became the defining act of a hero, and the monsters stand for the wild disorder that civilization must overcome. · Hybrids borrowed from Egypt and the Near East · Protective images become threats · Heroes prove themselves by killing them"},{"id":"h3_athena","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h3_athena.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"The Birth of Minerva","title":"The Birth of Minerva","artist":"René-Antoine Houasse","thumb":"h3_athena","text":"The Birth of Minerva · René-Antoine Houasse · 1688 · Oil on canvas · Palace of Versailles · One of a series on the story of Minerva that Houasse, a pupil of Charles Le Brun, painted for the royal palaces of Louis XIV. · Zeus swallowed his pregnant wife Metis, and later, suffering a terrible headache, had his head split open with an axe, usually by Hephaestus. Out sprang Athena, fully grown and armed. Houasse sets the scene on the clouds of Olympus, with the gods gathered as Hephaestus lowers his axe and the armored goddess flies up from her father’s head. The story makes her the child of Zeus alone, which is why she always takes his side. · Athena born from Zeus’s head · Hephaestus’s axe splits the skull · Baroque court painting · Black-figure · Greek vase technique (ca. 700–500 BCE) with figures in black slip and incised details on the red clay."},{"id":"h3_typhon","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h3_typhon.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"Hydria with Zeus fighting Typhon","title":"Hydria with Zeus fighting Typhon","artist":"","thumb":"h3_typhon","text":"Hydria with Zeus fighting Typhon · Chalcidian, ca. 540–530 BCE · Black-figure terracotta · Staatliche Antikensammlungen, Munich · A hydria, a three-handled jar used to carry water; this one, made by Greek potters in southern Italy, was found in an Etruscan grave. · Angry at the defeat of her Titan children, Gaea mated with Tartarus and gave birth to Typhon, a winged giant with a hundred snake heads. In the Typhonomachy Zeus fought him with thunderbolts and finally buried him under Mount Etna in Sicily, whose eruptions were said to be the monster’s fire. Here Zeus strides forward with a thunderbolt at the winged, snake-legged Typhon. · Typhon = son of Gaea and Tartarus · Typhonomachy: Zeus vs. Typhon · Buried under Etna: why volcanoes erupt · Typhonomachy · Zeus’s battle with the monster Typhon, the second fight for control of the world. · Typhon (Typhoeus) · A monstrous, winged, snake-legged son of Gaea and Tartarus, defeated by Zeus. · And in the bitterness of his anger Zeus cast him into wide Tartarus."},{"id":"h3_perg_c","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h3_perg_c.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"Pergamon Altar: Athena fighting the Giant Alcyoneus","title":"Pergamon Altar: Athena fighting the Giant Alcyoneus","artist":"","thumb":"h3_perg_c","text":"Pergamon Altar: Athena fighting the Giant Alcyoneus · Hellenistic, ca. 180–160 BCE · Marble relief · Pergamon Museum, Berlin · Part of the frieze wrapping a monumental altar built by the kings of Pergamon, who compared their victories over the Gauls to the gods’ victory over the Giants. · The Gigantomachy was the last great battle, in which the Olympians, with the help of the mortal hero Heracles, defeated the Giants. On this frieze Athena grabs the winged Giant Alcyoneus by the hair, while his mother Gaea rises from the ground pleading for him and Victory flies in to crown the goddess. The writhing, deeply carved bodies are typical of Hellenistic drama. · Gigantomachy: gods vs. Giants · Gaea pleads for her son · Political message for Pergamon’s kings · Gigantomachy · The war of the Olympians against the Giants, often used in art as a symbol of order defeating barbarism."},{"id":"h3_giulio","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h3_giulio.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"Sala dei Giganti (Room of the Giants)","title":"Sala dei Giganti (Room of the Giants)","artist":"Giulio Romano","thumb":"h3_giulio","text":"Sala dei Giganti (Room of the Giants) · Giulio Romano · 1532–35 · Fresco · Palazzo Te, Mantua · A room in the pleasure palace of Federico II Gonzaga, Duke of Mantua, painted on every wall and the ceiling to overwhelm visitors. · Giulio Romano painted the Giants crushed under collapsing columns and mountains as Jupiter hurls thunderbolts from the dome above. Because the painting covers the whole room, visitors feel the building falling around them. The theme flattered Federico’s patron, the Emperor Charles V, as a Jupiter who crushes rebels. · Whole room painted floor to ceiling · Jupiter crushes the Giants · Political flattery of Charles V"},{"id":"h3-14","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h3-14.html","h":3,"kind":"theme","label":"Order defeats chaos","title":"Order defeats chaos","artist":"","thumb":"h3_perg_c","text":"Order defeats chaos · Each cosmic war replaces raw force with Zeus’s order. · The Titans, Typhon, and the Giants are all children of Earth who stand for wild, unchecked power. Zeus wins by alliances and intelligence as much as by force, then shares out the world: he takes the sky, Poseidon the sea, and Hades the underworld. Later rulers, from the kings of Pergamon to Renaissance princes, used these battles to cast their own enemies as monsters. · Three wars: Titans, Typhon, Giants · Zeus shares the world by lot · Reused as political propaganda · Division of the world · After victory, Zeus, Poseidon, and Hades cast lots: Zeus took the sky, Poseidon the sea, Hades the underworld; earth and Olympus were shared."},{"id":"h3-15","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h3-15.html","h":3,"kind":"text","label":"Another way to begin","title":"Another way to begin","artist":"","thumb":null,"text":"Another way to begin · Hesiod’s was not the only Greek creation story. A version preserved in Aristophanes’ comedy Birds (414 BCE) and in later Orphic poems begins with Night (Nyx), pictured as a black-winged bird, who lays an egg in the darkness. From it hatches Eros, the force of love, who brings the other elements together; Homer, meanwhile, calls Oceanus the origin of the gods. · A creation story from a cosmic egg · Night lays a cosmic egg · Eros hatches first and joins things together · Several Greek origin stories existed side by side · Nyx · Night, one of the first beings from Chaos; in the egg story, the black-winged mother of Eros. · Eros · Desire, one of the oldest powers in Hesiod and the first-born in the egg story; later the boy Cupid. · Oceanus · Titan of the great river encircling the earth, called the origin of the gods in Homer’s Iliad."},{"id":"h4-0","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/part-4.html","h":4,"kind":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"The Origins of Mortals","artist":"","thumb":"h4_sarc","text":"The Origins of Mortals · Prometheus, Pandora, the Five Ages, and the Flood · The Greeks had no single story of how humans were made; Hesiod never says. Later writers credit the Titan Prometheus with shaping people from clay, and every version agrees that he became humankind’s champion against Zeus. His tricks cost humans dearly: Zeus answered with Pandora, the first woman, and the stories go on to explain toil, sickness, marriage, and a long decline from a golden age. · Prometheus shapes and protects humans · Zeus’s answer: Pandora and her jar · Decline through five ages; the flood · Prometheus · Titan whose name means “forethought”; trickster who sides with humans, steals fire, and is punished by Zeus. · Epimetheus · Prometheus’s brother, “afterthought,” who accepts Pandora despite being warned against gifts from Zeus. · Sarcophagus with Prometheus creating humankind · Roman, ca. 240 CE"},{"id":"h4_piero","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h4_piero.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"The Myth of Prometheus","title":"The Myth of Prometheus","artist":"Piero di Cosimo","thumb":"h4_piero","text":"The Myth of Prometheus · Piero di Cosimo · ca. 1510–15 · Oil on poplar panel · Alte Pinakothek, Munich · One of a pair of long panels (the other is in Strasbourg) made as part of the furnishings of a room in a Florentine home. · Piero tells the story as a sequence: Prometheus shapes a clay statue of a man, flies up with Athena to light a torch from the sun’s chariot, and brings the fire down to give the figure life. In this version, drawn from later writers, Prometheus is less a trickster than a creator, an idea Renaissance thinkers compared to God shaping Adam. · Continuous narrative: one panel, several moments · Prometheus as maker of humans · Fire from the sun animates the figure"},{"id":"h4_sarc","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h4_sarc.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"Sarcophagus with Prometheus creating humankind","title":"Sarcophagus with Prometheus creating humankind","artist":"","thumb":"h4_sarc","text":"Sarcophagus with Prometheus creating humankind · Roman, ca. 240 CE · Marble · Louvre, Paris; on deposit at the Musée départemental Arles antique since 2022 · A coffin carved for a wealthy Roman and found in the Alyscamps cemetery at Arles; the creation and life of humans framed the death of the person inside. · Prometheus sits modeling a small human figure while gods watch, among them Athena in her helmet and Hermes in his winged hat. In the Roman versions known from Ovid and later writers, Prometheus mixes earth and water to make people in the gods’ image, and Athena gives them reason. Christian writers later saw a pagan parallel to God forming Adam from earth in Genesis. · Prometheus models a human figure · Athena gives understanding · Parallel to Genesis"},{"id":"h4_kylix","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h4_kylix.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"Cup with Atlas and Prometheus","title":"Cup with Atlas and Prometheus","artist":"Arkesilas Painter","thumb":"h4_kylix","text":"Cup with Atlas and Prometheus · Arkesilas Painter · Laconian (Spartan), ca. 560–550 BCE · Black-figure terracotta · Vatican Museums (Museo Gregoriano Etrusco) · A drinking cup from Sparta, found in Etruria; the picture filled the inside of the bowl. · Two brothers suffer side by side: Atlas holds up the sky, and Prometheus, bound to a post, is attacked by Zeus’s eagle, which eats his liver every day while it grows back each night. This is one of the earliest images of the myth. In Hesiod the hero Heracles later kills the eagle and frees Prometheus, with Zeus’s consent. · Earliest images of the punishment · Eagle eats a regrowing liver · Atlas and Prometheus are brothers"},{"id":"h4_cossiers","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h4_cossiers.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"Prometheus Carrying Fire","title":"Prometheus Carrying Fire","artist":"Jan Cossiers, after a sketch by Rubens","thumb":"h4_cossiers","text":"Prometheus Carrying Fire · Jan Cossiers, after a sketch by Rubens · 1636–38 · Oil on canvas · Museo del Prado, Madrid · Painted for Philip IV’s hunting lodge, the Torre de la Parada, as part of Rubens’s workshop series on Ovid’s myths. · At a sacrifice at Mecone, Prometheus hid the good meat of an ox inside its unappetizing stomach and wrapped the bones in shining fat, and Zeus chose the fat. That trick explained why Greeks burned bones and fat for the gods and ate the meat themselves. Zeus retaliated by hiding fire, and Prometheus stole it back in a hollow fennel stalk, the burning torch he runs with here. · Trick at Mecone: bones in fat · Etiology of Greek sacrifice · Fire stolen in a fennel stalk · stole again for men from Zeus the counsellor in a hollow fennel-stalk"},{"id":"h4_baburen","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h4_baburen.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"Prometheus Being Chained by Vulcan","title":"Prometheus Being Chained by Vulcan","artist":"Dirck van Baburen","thumb":"h4_baburen","text":"Prometheus Being Chained by Vulcan · Dirck van Baburen · 1623 · Oil on canvas · Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam · A dramatic gallery painting by a Utrecht follower of Caravaggio; no patron or first owner is recorded. · Vulcan, the smith, hammers chains onto Prometheus while Mercury points to the punishment. Baburen borrows Caravaggio’s strong light and dark and his plain, physical bodies, so the scene feels like a real execution. By this time Vulcan stood for technology itself, and Mercury for cunning, the same qualities that got Prometheus into trouble. · Vulcan chains, Mercury oversees · Caravaggesque light and realism · Technology punishing its giver"},{"id":"h4_rubens","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h4_rubens.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"Prometheus Bound","title":"Prometheus Bound","artist":"Peter Paul Rubens, eagle by Frans Snyders","thumb":"h4_rubens","text":"Prometheus Bound · Peter Paul Rubens, eagle by Frans Snyders · 1611–18 · Oil on canvas · Philadelphia Museum of Art · A large showpiece Rubens kept for years and later sold; he chose the subject to show his mastery of the heroic nude. · Rubens shows Prometheus falling headfirst down the rocks as the eagle tears at him, a pose built from his study of ancient sculpture and Michelangelo. In Aeschylus’s play Prometheus Bound, the Titan defends himself by listing what he taught humans: building, counting, writing, medicine, and more. That made him a hero of human progress for later artists and writers. · Collaboration with Snyders (eagle) · Pose from ancient sculpture and Michelangelo · Aeschylus: benefactor of civilization"},{"id":"h4-7","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h4-7.html","h":4,"kind":"theme","label":"The trickster who made us human","title":"The trickster who made us human","artist":"","thumb":"h4_kylix","text":"The trickster who made us human · Every gift from Prometheus comes with a cost to humans. · Prometheus’s trick at the sacrifice gave humans the meat but left the gods the smoke, setting people halfway between gods and animals. Stealing fire gave them technology but brought on Zeus’s anger and Pandora. The story explains why human life mixes progress with suffering, and later ages turned Prometheus into a symbol of creativity, rebellion, and the price of knowledge. · Sacrifice places humans between gods and beasts · Fire = technology and punishment · Later symbol of creativity and rebellion"},{"id":"h4_epimetheus","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h4_epimetheus.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"Epimetheus Opening Pandora’s Vase","title":"Epimetheus Opening Pandora’s Vase","artist":"Giulio Bonasone","thumb":"h4_epimetheus","text":"Epimetheus Opening Pandora’s Vase · Giulio Bonasone · mid-16th century · Engraving · Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A print sold to collectors and artists; prints like this spread mythological imagery across Europe. · To punish humans for the stolen fire, Zeus had Hephaestus shape the first woman from earth and water; Athena taught her weaving, Aphrodite gave her desire, and Hermes gave her a deceitful mind. She was called Pandora, “all gifts,” and was presented to Epimetheus. Bonasone’s print is unusual in showing Epimetheus himself lifting the lid as the evils fly out. · Pandora made by Hephaestus, gifted by the gods · Presented to Epimetheus · Here Epimetheus opens the vessel · Pandora · The first woman in Hesiod, “all gifts,” made by the gods to punish humans for Prometheus’s theft. · the very famous Limping God formed of earth the likeness of a shy maiden"},{"id":"h4_cousin","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h4_cousin.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"Eva Prima Pandora","title":"Eva Prima Pandora","artist":"Jean Cousin the Elder","thumb":"h4_cousin","text":"Eva Prima Pandora · Jean Cousin the Elder · ca. 1550 · Oil on panel · Louvre, Paris · The Louvre acquired it in 1922 from Sens, Cousin’s home town; the title, “Eve the first Pandora,” spells out its argument. · Cousin merges two first women: Eve of Genesis and Pandora of Greek myth. She reclines with a skull under her arm, an apple branch, and a jar, while a snake curls around her arm. Both stories blame a woman’s curiosity for bringing suffering into the world, and Renaissance thinkers liked to treat pagan myths as echoes of the Bible. · Eve and Pandora combined · Skull, apple, jar, snake · Both blame a woman for human suffering"},{"id":"h4_waterhouse","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h4_waterhouse.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"Pandora","title":"Pandora","artist":"John William Waterhouse","thumb":"h4_waterhouse","text":"Pandora · John William Waterhouse · 1896 · Oil on canvas · private collection · A Victorian exhibition painting for a public that loved romantic, literary subjects. · Waterhouse shows Pandora kneeling in a wood, peering into a golden box as a wisp of smoke escapes. In Hesiod the container is a large storage jar (pithos); it became a “box” through a Renaissance mistranslation, and later versions add the rule that she must not open it. When she lifts the lid, sickness and toil escape, and only Hope stays inside. · Jar (pithos), not a box · Evils escape; Hope remains · Romantic Victorian version · Pithos · A large clay storage jar; the original vessel in Hesiod, later called a “box.” · Only Hope remained there in an unbreakable home within under the rim of the great jar"},{"id":"h4_danaides","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h4_danaides.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"The Danaïdes","title":"The Danaïdes","artist":"John William Waterhouse","thumb":"h4_danaides","text":"The Danaïdes · John William Waterhouse · 1903 · Oil on canvas · private collection · A Victorian exhibition painting of a punishment in the underworld. · The fifty daughters of Danaus were forced to marry their cousins, and all but one killed her husband on the wedding night. In the underworld they were sentenced to carry water forever in leaking jars that never fill. Waterhouse paints them pouring endlessly into a basin. The jar, like Pandora’s, links women with containers, and Greek ideas of marriage cast the bride as a vessel for children. · Killed their husbands on the wedding night · Endless water-carrying in Hades · Jar as symbol of the female body · Danaïds · The daughters of Danaus, punished in the underworld for murdering their husbands by carrying water in leaking jars. · Marriage = children = immortality · Greek belief that, with no happy afterlife for most people, a person lived on through children, which made marriage necessary."},{"id":"h4-12","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h4-12.html","h":4,"kind":"theme","label":"Woman as a container","title":"Woman as a container","artist":"","thumb":"h4_cousin","text":"Woman as a container · Greek myth repeatedly pictures women as vessels. · Pandora comes with a jar, the Danaïds carry broken jars, and Greek women in daily life carried water jars and wedding gifts. Writers compared a woman to a jar or a stomach: something that holds and consumes. Hesiod treats women as a costly necessity, since a man without children has no one to care for him in old age. These stories say more about Greek men’s anxieties than about real women. · Jar = womb, storage, and burial urn · Marriage resented but necessary · Myth reflects male anxieties"},{"id":"h4_cranach","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h4_cranach.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"The Golden Age","title":"The Golden Age","artist":"Lucas Cranach the Elder","thumb":"h4_cranach","text":"The Golden Age · Lucas Cranach the Elder · ca. 1530 · Oil on panel · Nasjonalmuseet, Oslo · An oak panel picturing a mythical paradise; its original owner is not recorded. · Hesiod describes five races of humans, each worse than the last. In the Golden Age under Cronus, people lived like gods, without work, illness, or old age, and died as if falling asleep. Cranach paints them dancing and bathing in a walled garden, an image close to the Christian Eden. The silver race was foolish, the bronze violent, and the heroes better, but Hesiod’s own Iron Age is the worst of all. · Gold → Silver → Bronze → Heroes → Iron · Golden Age: no toil, no old age · Hesiod lives in the Iron Age · Five Ages (Races) · Hesiod’s history of humankind: Golden, Silver, Bronze, the Age of Heroes, and the present Iron Age. · they lived like gods without sorrow of heart, remote and free from toil and grief"},{"id":"h4_flood","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h4_flood.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"The Flood Tablet (Epic of Gilgamesh, Tablet XI)","title":"The Flood Tablet (Epic of Gilgamesh, Tablet XI)","artist":"","thumb":"h4_flood","text":"The Flood Tablet (Epic of Gilgamesh, Tablet XI) · Neo-Assyrian, 7th century BCE · Clay · British Museum, London · A tablet from the library of the Assyrian king Ashurbanipal at Nineveh, a royal collection of literature and learning. · When George Smith of the British Museum translated this tablet in 1872, he found a flood story strikingly like Noah’s: the gods decide to destroy humanity, one god warns a good man to build a boat, and he releases birds to find land. Older Sumerian and Akkadian versions name the hero Ziusudra and Atrahasis. The Greek flood story belongs to the same family. · Translated by George Smith, 1872 · Boat, warning god, birds sent out · Mesopotamian flood stories predate Genesis · Ziusudra and Enki · In the Sumerian flood story, the god Enki warns the good king Ziusudra to build a boat. · Atrahasis and Ea · The Akkadian version of the same story; Ea is the Akkadian name of Enki. · Noah and Yahweh · In Genesis, God warns Noah to build the ark before a flood sent to punish human wickedness."},{"id":"h4_deluge","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h4_deluge.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"The Deluge","title":"The Deluge","artist":"Michelangelo","thumb":"h4_deluge","text":"The Deluge · Michelangelo · 1508–09 · Fresco · Sistine Chapel ceiling, Vatican · One of the nine Genesis scenes on the ceiling of the pope’s chapel, commissioned by Julius II. · Michelangelo shows desperate families climbing to high ground while Noah’s ark floats in the distance. Placed among Greek myths, the scene shows how widely the flood story spread. Each culture gives it a different reason: overpopulation in Mesopotamia, human wickedness in Genesis and in the Greek version. · Genesis flood on the Sistine ceiling · Refugees in the foreground, ark behind · Same story, different reasons"},{"id":"h4_deucalion","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h4_deucalion.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"Deucalion and Pyrrha","title":"Deucalion and Pyrrha","artist":"Peter Paul Rubens","thumb":"h4_deucalion","text":"Deucalion and Pyrrha · Peter Paul Rubens · 1636–37 · Oil on panel (sketch) · Museo del Prado, Madrid · An oil sketch for a lost canvas by Jan Cossiers for the Torre de la Parada series; assistants used sketches like this to paint the full-size canvases. · In the Greek version, told most fully by Ovid, Zeus floods the world after the king Lycaon serves him human flesh. Only the good couple Deucalion, son of Prometheus, and Pyrrha, daughter of Epimetheus and Pandora, survive in a chest or boat. Told by an oracle to throw “the bones of their mother” behind them, they toss stones, the bones of Mother Earth, which become new men and women. · Zeus’s flood punishes cannibalism (Lycaon) · Survivors: Deucalion and Pyrrha · Stones become a new humankind · Deucalion and Pyrrha · The pious couple who survive the Greek flood and repopulate the earth by throwing stones."},{"id":"h4-17","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h4-17.html","h":4,"kind":"theme","label":"A world in decline","title":"A world in decline","artist":"","thumb":"h4_deluge","text":"A world in decline · Greek myth tells human history as a fall, not as progress. · Hesiod’s ages run downhill from gold to iron, and the flood wipes the slate almost clean. Humans fall from a god-like life because of Prometheus’s tricks and Pandora’s jar, much as Adam and Eve are expelled from Eden. Later, the Roman poet Virgil imagined the ages repeating in a cycle, so that Augustus could announce the return of a Golden Age. · History as decline: gold to iron · Flood as divine reset · Rome promised a new Golden Age · For now truly is a race of iron, and men never rest from labour and sorrow by day, and from perishing by night"},{"id":"h5-0","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/part-5.html","h":5,"kind":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"Zeus, Hera, Poseidon, and Apollo","artist":"","thumb":"h5_gossaert","text":"Zeus, Hera, Poseidon, and Apollo · The divine family, the loves of Zeus, and the god of Delphi · The Greeks imagined their gods as a family that looks and behaves like a human one, only immortal and far more powerful. Zeus is the father who keeps order, Hera the mother who guards marriage, and their brothers and children each control part of the world. Most stories about Zeus are about his unions with goddesses and mortal women, which produce the forces of the universe and the heroes of legend. Apollo, his son by Leto, stands for prophecy, music, healing, and order. · Gods as an immortal human family · Zeus’s unions create cosmic powers and heroes · Apollo: prophecy, music, healing, plague · Anthropomorphism · Picturing gods in human form with human feelings; the basis of nearly all Greek religious art. · Xenia · Guest-friendship, the sacred duty between host and guest that Zeus protects; breaking it brings disaster. · Metis · Titaness of cleverness, Zeus’s first consort, mother of Athena. · Themis · Titaness of divine law and order; by Zeus mother of the Horae (Seasons) and the Moirae (Fates). · Moirae (Fates) · Clotho, who spins the thread of life; Lachesis, who measures it; and Atropos, who cuts it. · Eurynome · An Oceanid, by Zeus mother of the three Graces. · Mnemosyne · Titaness of memory, by Zeus mother of the nine Muses. · Jan Gossaert · Neptune and Amphitrite · 1516"},{"id":"h5_ingres","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h5_ingres.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"Jupiter and Thetis","title":"Jupiter and Thetis","artist":"Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres","thumb":"h5_ingres","text":"Jupiter and Thetis · Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres · 1811 · Oil on canvas · Musée Granet, Aix-en-Provence · A required showpiece Ingres sent from Rome to the French Academy as a prize student; it was bought by the French state. · The sea goddess Thetis kneels and touches Zeus’s chin, the ancient gesture of a suppliant, begging him to honor her son Achilles, as in the first book of the Iliad. Zeus sits enthroned like an ancient cult statue, with his eagle and scepter, while the jealous Hera watches from the upper left. Ingres turns the king of the gods into an image of absolute, almost frightening authority. · Scene from Iliad 1: Thetis pleads for Achilles · Zeus as enthroned cult image · Hera watches from the clouds"},{"id":"h5-2","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h5-2.html","h":5,"kind":"theme","label":"A family of gods","title":"A family of gods","artist":"","thumb":"h5_hesperides","text":"A family of gods · Greek gods quarrel, love, and scheme like a human family. · Each Olympian has a family role and a part of the world: Zeus the father and judge, Hera the wife and mother, Poseidon the sea, Hestia the hearth, Demeter the harvest, Hephaestus the crafts, Athena women’s skills and practical wisdom, Aphrodite desire, Apollo prophecy and healing, Hermes travel and messages. Because the gods act like people, their stories could model, or warn against, human behavior. · Each god has a domain and a family role · Human passions on a cosmic scale · Myths as models and warnings"},{"id":"h5_danae","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h5_danae.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"Danaë","title":"Danaë","artist":"Titian","thumb":"h5_danae","text":"Danaë · Titian · ca. 1560–65 · Oil on canvas · Museo del Prado, Madrid · A later version of the Danaë Titian sent to King Philip II of Spain in 1553 (now in the Wellington Collection, London); the first owner of this one is unknown. · King Acrisius of Argos locked his daughter Danaë in a bronze chamber because an oracle said her son would kill him. Zeus reached her as a shower of gold, and she gave birth to the hero Perseus. Titian contrasts the relaxed, glowing princess with her old nurse, who greedily catches the coins in her apron. · Zeus as a shower of gold · Mother of Perseus · Later version of Philip II’s Danaë · Danaë · Argive princess imprisoned by her father; by Zeus mother of Perseus."},{"id":"h5_leda","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h5_leda.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"Leda and the Swan","title":"Leda and the Swan","artist":"Attributed to Francesco Melzi, after Leonardo da Vinci","thumb":"h5_leda","text":"Leda and the Swan · Attributed to Francesco Melzi, after Leonardo da Vinci · ca. 1505–07 · Oil on panel · Uffizi Gallery, Florence · copy of Leonardo’s lost original · A copy by Leonardo’s pupil of a lost painting that was famous in its own time; copies spread the composition across Europe. · Zeus took the form of a swan to seduce Leda, queen of Sparta. From the eggs she laid came Helen, later of Troy, and in most versions her sister Clytemnestra and the twins Castor and Pollux. Leonardo’s original is lost, so copies like this one preserve his twisting standing figure and the children hatching at her feet. · Zeus as a swan · Leda, queen of Sparta · Eggs hatch Helen and her siblings · Leda · Queen of Sparta who, after Zeus visited her as a swan, bore Helen and the Dioscuri (Castor and Pollux)."},{"id":"h5_europa","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h5_europa.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"The Rape of Europa","title":"The Rape of Europa","artist":"Titian","thumb":"h5_europa","text":"The Rape of Europa · Titian · 1560–62 · Oil on canvas · Isabella Stewart Gardner Museum, Boston · The last of the poesie painted for Philip II of Spain. · Zeus turned himself into a beautiful white bull, coaxed the Phoenician princess Europa onto his back, and swam with her across the sea to Crete. There she bore Minos, the future king of Crete, whose palace and Labyrinth return in the story of Theseus. Titian shows her sprawled and terrified as the shore recedes, with cupids riding the waves. · Zeus as a white bull · Europa carried to Crete · Mother of King Minos · Europa · Phoenician princess carried off by Zeus in bull form; mother of Minos and namesake of Europe."},{"id":"h5_ganymede","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h5_ganymede.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"The Abduction of Ganymede","title":"The Abduction of Ganymede","artist":"Correggio","thumb":"h5_ganymede","text":"The Abduction of Ganymede · Correggio · ca. 1531–32 · Oil on canvas · Kunsthistorisches Museum, Vienna · Part of a series on the Loves of Jupiter, probably made for Federico II Gonzaga, Duke of Mantua. · Zeus, in the form of an eagle, snatched up the beautiful Trojan prince Ganymede and carried him to Olympus to be the gods’ cupbearer. Correggio paints the boy rising into the sky while his dog barks below. The Romans called him Catamitus, the source of the English word “catamite,” because the story was read as Zeus’s love for a youth. · Zeus as an eagle · Trojan prince made cupbearer of the gods · Series on the Loves of Jupiter · Ganymede (Catamitus) · Trojan prince taken to Olympus by Zeus’s eagle to serve as cupbearer; Catamitus is his Latin name."},{"id":"h5_io","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h5_io.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"Jupiter and Io","title":"Jupiter and Io","artist":"Correggio","thumb":"h5_io","text":"Jupiter and Io · Correggio · ca. 1531–32 · Oil on canvas · Kunsthistorisches Museum, Vienna · Companion to the Ganymede in the same Loves of Jupiter series for the Gonzaga court. · To hide his affair with the priestess Io from Hera, Zeus wrapped himself in a dark cloud; Correggio shows only a faint face and a hand emerging from the mist as Io melts into his embrace. When Hera grew suspicious, Zeus turned Io into a white cow, and Hera set the hundred-eyed giant Argus to guard her. · Zeus disguised as a cloud · Io turned into a cow · Hera sets Argus to guard her · Io · Priestess of Hera loved by Zeus and turned into a cow; she wandered as far as Egypt before regaining her form."},{"id":"h5_argus","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h5_argus.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"Mercury and Argus","title":"Mercury and Argus","artist":"Diego Velázquez","thumb":"h5_argus","text":"Mercury and Argus · Diego Velázquez · ca. 1659 · Oil on canvas · Museo del Prado, Madrid · Painted for the Hall of Mirrors in the royal palace (Alcázar) of Madrid, a long, high room that explains the painting’s wide format. · Zeus sent Hermes to free Io. Hermes lulled the watchman Argus Panoptes (“all-seeing”) to sleep with music and stories, then killed him. Hera placed Argus’s hundred eyes on the tail of her peacock. Velázquez paints a quiet, shadowy moment as Hermes creeps toward the dozing guard, with the cow behind them. · Hermes lulls Argus to sleep · Argus’s eyes end up on the peacock · Late work by Velázquez · Argus Panoptes · Hundred-eyed giant Hera set to guard Io, killed by Hermes; his eyes decorate Hera’s peacock."},{"id":"h5-9","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h5-9.html","h":5,"kind":"theme","label":"The loves of Zeus","title":"The loves of Zeus","artist":"","thumb":"h5_europa","text":"The loves of Zeus · Zeus’s many unions explain where heroes and ruling families came from. · Zeus takes shape after shape, a shower of gold, a swan, a bull, an eagle, a cloud, to reach the women and youths he desires. To ancient audiences these stories gave great families and cities a divine ancestor: Perseus, Helen, and Minos all descend from such unions. Renaissance rulers commissioned these scenes as elegant, erotic pictures, which is why so many survive. · Disguises: gold, swan, bull, eagle, cloud · Divine ancestors for heroes and kings · Favorite subject of Renaissance patrons"},{"id":"h5_hesperides","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h5_hesperides.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"The Garden of the Hesperides","title":"The Garden of the Hesperides","artist":"Frederic Leighton","thumb":"h5_hesperides","text":"The Garden of the Hesperides · Frederic Leighton · 1892 · Oil on canvas · Lady Lever Art Gallery, Port Sunlight · A round exhibition painting shown at the Royal Academy, made for wealthy Victorian collectors. · When Hera married Zeus, Earth gave her a tree of golden apples, which grew in a garden at the western edge of the world. The nymphs called the Hesperides tended it, guarded by the serpent Ladon, near where Atlas holds up the sky. Leighton’s drowsy nymphs and coiled serpent prepare for the later labor in which Heracles takes the apples. · Golden apples: Earth’s wedding gift to Hera · Guarded by the Hesperides and Ladon · Later stolen in a labor of Heracles · Hera’s children · With Zeus, Hebe (youth), Eileithyia (childbirth), and Ares; in Hesiod she bears Hephaestus on her own. · Ladon · The serpent that coils around the tree of golden apples."},{"id":"h5_gossaert","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h5_gossaert.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"Neptune and Amphitrite","title":"Neptune and Amphitrite","artist":"Jan Gossaert","thumb":"h5_gossaert","text":"Neptune and Amphitrite · Jan Gossaert · 1516 · Oil on panel · Gemäldegalerie, Berlin · Painted for Philip of Burgundy, an admiral and later bishop, for his castle at Souburg; one of the first large mythological nudes in Northern art. · Poseidon, the Roman Neptune, married the sea nymph Amphitrite, and their son was the merman Triton. Gossaert, who had just seen ancient sculpture in Rome, poses the couple as massive classical nudes inside an ornate temple. Poseidon also fathered monsters and marvels: the Cyclops Polyphemus with the nymph Thoosa, and, with Medusa, the winged horse Pegasus and the warrior Chrysaor. · Poseidon marries Amphitrite; son Triton · Northern artist responds to ancient Rome · Father of Polyphemus and Pegasus · Amphitrite · Sea nymph, wife of Poseidon and mother of Triton. · Pegasus and Chrysaor · Offspring of Poseidon and Medusa, born from her neck when Perseus beheaded her; Bellerophon later tamed Pegasus. · Hades · Brother of Zeus who rules the dead with Persephone; the Cyclopes gave him a helmet of invisibility."},{"id":"h5_redon","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h5_redon.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"The Cyclops","title":"The Cyclops","artist":"Odilon Redon","thumb":"h5_redon","text":"The Cyclops · Odilon Redon · ca. 1914 · Oil on cardboard mounted on panel · Kröller-Müller Museum, Otterlo · A late Symbolist painting, made for collectors of Redon’s dreamlike work. · Polyphemus, son of Poseidon and the nymph Thoosa, is the one-eyed giant Odysseus later blinds. In a story told by Theocritus and Ovid, he falls hopelessly in love with the sea nymph Galatea. Redon shows the giant peering shyly over a hill at the sleeping nymph, turning a monster into a lonely, almost gentle figure. · Polyphemus, son of Poseidon · In love with Galatea · Symbolist dream image · Polyphemus · One-eyed Cyclops, son of Poseidon, who traps and is blinded by Odysseus."},{"id":"h5_pythia","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h5_pythia.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"Cup with King Aegeus consulting the oracle","title":"Cup with King Aegeus consulting the oracle","artist":"Attributed to the Kodros Painter","thumb":"h5_pythia","text":"Cup with King Aegeus consulting the oracle · Attributed to the Kodros Painter · Athenian, ca. 440–430 BCE · Red-figure kylix · Altes Museum, Berlin · A drinking cup; it is the only surviving image from Classical Athens of the Delphic oracle at work. · The Athenian king Aegeus, childless, asks the oracle what to do; the answer will lead to the birth of Theseus. The prophetess, labeled Themis here, sits on Apollo’s tall tripod holding a laurel sprig and a dish. At Delphi the priestess called the Pythia spoke Apollo’s answers, and priests turned them into verse. · Only Classical image of the Delphic oracle · Prophetess on Apollo’s tripod with laurel · Aegeus asks about an heir · Pythia · The priestess of Apollo at Delphi who spoke his prophecies, seated on a tripod. · Dactylic hexameter · The six-beat meter of epic poetry, in which Delphic answers were often delivered."},{"id":"h5_delphi","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h5_delphi.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"Temple of Apollo at Delphi","title":"Temple of Apollo at Delphi","artist":"","thumb":"h5_delphi","text":"Temple of Apollo at Delphi · 4th century BCE (rebuilt after 373 BCE) · Limestone · Delphi, Greece, on the slopes of Mount Parnassus · The home of Apollo’s oracle, the most important in the Greek world, consulted by cities and individuals before major decisions. · Apollo was born on the island of Delos to the Titaness Leto, after Hera banned every land from receiving her and held back the birth goddess Eileithyia. As a young god he killed the serpent Python at Delphi and took over the site. His temple carried two famous mottoes, “Know yourself” and “Nothing in excess.” Anyone stained by bloodshed had to be purified before approaching him. · Apollo born on Delos · Kills the Python and claims Delphi · Mottoes: know yourself; nothing in excess · Delphi · Sanctuary of Apollo on Mount Parnassus, thought to be the center (navel) of the world. · Python · The serpent Apollo killed to take Delphi; the Pythia and the Pythian Games are named for it. · Telphusa · A spring nymph who tried to steer Apollo away from Delphi; he buried her spring in punishment. · Tityus · A giant who attacked Leto and was killed by Apollo and Artemis, then punished in the underworld. · Miasma · Ritual pollution, especially from bloodshed, that Apollo’s rites could cleanse."},{"id":"h5_bernini","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h5_bernini.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"Apollo and Daphne","title":"Apollo and Daphne","artist":"Piero del Pollaiuolo","thumb":"h5_bernini","text":"Apollo and Daphne · Piero del Pollaiuolo · ca. 1470–80 · Oil on wood · National Gallery, London · A small panel made for a private room in Florence, where mythological scenes decorated furniture and walls of wealthy homes. · Struck by one of Cupid’s arrows, Apollo chased the nymph Daphne, who prayed to escape and was turned into a laurel tree. Pollaiuolo dresses Apollo as a young Florentine and sets the chase in the Arno valley; he catches her just as her arms sprout into great branches of laurel. Apollo made the laurel his sacred plant, which is why winners and poets were crowned with laurel, and why we still say “laureate.” · Daphne becomes a laurel · Ancient myth in a Florentine landscape · Origin of “laureate” · Daphne · A nymph turned into a laurel (daphne in Greek) to escape Apollo."},{"id":"h5_hyacinth","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h5_hyacinth.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"The Death of Hyacinthus","title":"The Death of Hyacinthus","artist":"Giovanni Battista Tiepolo","thumb":"h5_hyacinth","text":"The Death of Hyacinthus · Giovanni Battista Tiepolo · 1752–53 · Oil on canvas · Thyssen-Bornemisza Museum, Madrid · Painted during Tiepolo’s stay in Würzburg; it belonged to Wilhelm Friedrich of Schaumburg-Lippe, who may have bought it directly from the artist. · Apollo loved the Spartan youth Hyacinthus and accidentally killed him with a thrown discus; from his blood grew the hyacinth flower. Tiepolo updates the story by replacing the discus with a tennis ball and racket at the youth’s feet. Apollo’s loves almost always end in loss: Cassandra rejects him, Coronis betrays him, Daphne flees. · Killed by Apollo’s discus · Blood becomes the hyacinth · Tiepolo swaps in a tennis racket · Cassandra · Trojan princess given prophecy by Apollo and then cursed never to be believed. · Cumaean Sibyl · Apollo’s prophetess at Cumae in Italy, who later guides Aeneas to the underworld. · Coronis and Asclepius · Coronis bore Apollo’s son Asclepius, god of medicine, who was raised by the centaur Chiron. · Hygieia · Daughter of Asclepius, goddess of health, source of the word “hygiene.”"},{"id":"h5_marsyas","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h5_marsyas.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"The Flaying of Marsyas","title":"The Flaying of Marsyas","artist":"Titian","thumb":"h5_marsyas","text":"The Flaying of Marsyas · Titian · ca. 1570–76 · Oil on canvas · Archdiocesan Museum, Kroměříž · One of Titian’s last paintings, apparently kept in his studio and not made for a known patron. · The satyr Marsyas dared to challenge Apollo to a music contest, his pipes against the god’s lyre. He lost, and Apollo had him hung upside down and skinned alive. Titian’s loose, smeared brushwork makes the scene even more disturbing, while King Midas sits in thought nearby. The story warns against hubris, the human or near-human pride that challenges a god. · Music contest: pipes vs. lyre · Punishment for challenging a god · Titian’s late, loose brushwork · Marsyas · A satyr who challenged Apollo to a music contest and was flayed for losing. · Hubris · Arrogant pride that oversteps human limits and provokes the gods."},{"id":"h5-18","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h5-18.html","h":5,"kind":"theme","label":"Apollo: order and its price","title":"Apollo: order and its price","artist":"","thumb":"h5_pythia","text":"Apollo: order and its price · The god of reason and harmony can also be the cruelest of the gods. · Apollo stands for light, music, prophecy, and the rule of measure, the values written on his temple. Yet his myths are full of violence: he kills the Python, flays Marsyas, and sends plague on those who offend him. For the Greeks, order and danger came from the same god, and respecting the limits he set was a matter of survival. · Light, music, prophecy, healing · Also plague and punishment · Know your limits: the Delphic lesson"},{"id":"h6-0","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/part-6.html","h":6,"kind":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"Hermes, Hephaestus, Ares, and the Goddesses","artist":"","thumb":"h6_milo","text":"Hermes, Hephaestus, Ares, and the Goddesses · Messengers, makers, lovers, and the powers of women · This part covers the remaining gods and the five great goddesses. Hermes moves between worlds as messenger, trickster, and guide of the dead; Hephaestus makes things; Ares brings violence. The goddesses each govern part of women’s lives and the fertility of the world: Demeter the crops, Hestia the house, Aphrodite attraction, Artemis wild animals and girls, Athena weaving and skill. · Hermes: boundaries, trade, the dead · Hephaestus and Ares: craft and violence · Goddesses mapped onto women’s lives · Female Olympians and fertility · Each goddess oversees one kind of growth or plenty: Demeter the grain harvest, Hestia the household, Aphrodite sexual desire, Artemis wild animals, and Athena weaving and other crafts. · Aphrodite of Melos (Venus de Milo) · ca. 150–125 BCE"},{"id":"h6_herm","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h6_herm.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"Miniature herm of Hermes","title":"Miniature herm of Hermes","artist":"After Alkamenes","thumb":"h6_herm","text":"Miniature herm of Hermes · After Alkamenes · Roman, 1st–2nd century CE, after a Greek original of ca. 430 BCE · Marble, 4.6 cm (1¾ in.) high · Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A tiny Roman copy of a herm, the square pillar with a god’s head and male genitals that Greeks set up at doorways, crossroads, and boundaries to protect them. · Hermes, son of Zeus and the nymph Maia, protects travelers, merchants, and thieves, and guides souls of the dead to the underworld. Greeks set up herms, pillars topped with his bearded head, outside houses and at crossroads to ward off harm. This old-fashioned, bearded style copies a famous herm by the sculptor Alkamenes that stood at the entrance to the Athenian Acropolis. · Herm = boundary pillar with Hermes’s head · Protects doors, roads, and crossings · Guide of souls (psychopompos) · Herm · A square stone pillar topped with a head of Hermes, set at boundaries and doorways. · Apotropaion · An object meant to turn away evil, such as a herm or a Gorgon’s face. · Psychopompos · “Guide of souls,” Hermes’s role leading the dead to Hades. · Caduceus · Hermes’s herald’s staff entwined with two snakes; one story links it to snakes the seer Tiresias struck apart. · Autolycus · Son of Hermes, master thief, and grandfather of Odysseus."},{"id":"h6_echo","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h6_echo.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"Echo and Narcissus","title":"Echo and Narcissus","artist":"John William Waterhouse","thumb":"h6_echo","text":"Echo and Narcissus · John William Waterhouse · 1903 · Oil on canvas · Walker Art Gallery, Liverpool · A Royal Academy exhibition painting drawn from Ovid’s Metamorphoses. · Hermes’s son Pan, half man and half goat, is the god of shepherds and wild places; sudden fear in lonely country was blamed on him, giving us the word “panic.” In one story Pan pursued the nymph Echo, who rejected him and was torn apart until only her voice remained. In Ovid’s better-known version, shown here, Echo pines for Narcissus, who falls in love with his own reflection. · Pan: goat-legged god of the wild · Echo reduced to a voice · Ovid’s version: Narcissus and his reflection · Pan · Goat-legged son of Hermes and Dryope, god of flocks and wild places, inventor of the panpipes; source of “panic.” · Echo · A nymph whose body faded until only her voice remained. · Silenus · An old, drunken satyr, tutor of Dionysus; in some accounts a son of Pan."},{"id":"h6_marsvenus","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h6_marsvenus.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"Venus and Mars","title":"Venus and Mars","artist":"Sandro Botticelli","thumb":"h6_marsvenus","text":"Venus and Mars · Sandro Botticelli · ca. 1485 · Tempera and oil on panel · National Gallery, London · A long, low panel probably set into the wall paneling or furniture of a Florentine bedroom, possibly for a wedding. · Aphrodite’s husband was Hephaestus, the lame smith whose forge was on the island of Lemnos, but she loved Ares. In the Odyssey, Hephaestus catches the lovers in an invisible net and calls the gods to laugh at them. Botticelli shows a calmer moral: Mars sleeps, exhausted, while Venus stays alert and little satyrs play with his armor. Love conquers war. · Aphrodite married to Hephaestus, loves Ares · Hephaestus’s net (Odyssey 8) · Love tames war · Lemnos · The island where Hephaestus landed when thrown from Olympus and where he kept a forge. · Children of Ares and Aphrodite · Phobos (panic), Deimos (fear), Harmonia (harmony), and in some accounts Eros (desire)."},{"id":"h6_milo","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h6_milo.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"Aphrodite of Melos (Venus de Milo)","title":"Aphrodite of Melos (Venus de Milo)","artist":"","thumb":"h6_milo","text":"Aphrodite of Melos (Venus de Milo) · Hellenistic, ca. 150–125 BCE · Marble · Louvre, Paris · Probably a statue set up in a gymnasium or public building on the island of Melos, where a farmer found it in 1820. · Aphrodite was said to have come ashore on Cyprus, and her great sanctuary stood at Paphos. Her cult has Near Eastern roots in the love-and-war goddesses Inanna, Ishtar, and Astarte, and ancient writers claimed some of her temples practiced sacred prostitution, a claim modern scholars debate. The statue’s lost arms make it impossible to know whether she held an apple, a mirror, or a shield. · Cult centers: Cyprus and Paphos · Roots in Inanna, Ishtar, Astarte · Arms lost; pose uncertain · Paphos · City on Cyprus with Aphrodite’s most famous sanctuary. · Inanna/Ishtar/Astarte · Near Eastern goddesses of love and war (Sumerian, Babylonian, Phoenician) who shaped Aphrodite."},{"id":"h6_sappho","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h6_sappho.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"Sappho and Alcaeus","title":"Sappho and Alcaeus","artist":"Lawrence Alma-Tadema","thumb":"h6_sappho","text":"Sappho and Alcaeus · Lawrence Alma-Tadema · 1881 · Oil on panel · Walters Art Museum, Baltimore · A Victorian history painting imagining ancient Greek life with archaeological detail. · Sappho, who lived on the island of Lesbos around 600 BCE, was the most celebrated female poet of antiquity, and her surviving “Hymn to Aphrodite” calls on the goddess for help in love. Alma-Tadema imagines her listening as the poet Alcaeus plays the lyre. Her poems about desire between women are why “lesbian” comes from Lesbos. · Sappho of Lesbos, ca. 600 BCE · Wrote a hymn to Aphrodite · Archaeologically detailed Victorian scene · Sappho · Poet of Lesbos famous for lyric poems of love, including a prayer to Aphrodite."},{"id":"h6_adonis","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h6_adonis.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"Venus and Adonis","title":"Venus and Adonis","artist":"Titian","thumb":"h6_adonis","text":"Venus and Adonis · Titian · 1550s · Oil on canvas · Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · One of several versions of a composition first painted for Philip II; Titian’s workshop repeated it for other buyers. · Adonis was born from a tree: his mother Myrrha (or Smyrna), after an incestuous passion for her father, King Cinyras of Cyprus, was turned into the myrrh tree. Aphrodite loved the beautiful young hunter and begged him not to hunt, but he was killed by a boar, and anemones sprang from his blood. Titian invents the moment of her clinging plea as he pulls away. Women mourned him each year in the festival of the Adonia. · Adonis born from the myrrh tree · Killed by a boar; anemones from his blood · Yearly mourning festival, the Adonia · Adonis · Beautiful youth loved by Aphrodite, killed by a boar; his cult mourned him as a dying god of vegetation. · Myrrha · Daughter of King Cinyras, turned into the myrrh tree; mother of Adonis."},{"id":"h6_pygmalion","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h6_pygmalion.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"Pygmalion and Galatea","title":"Pygmalion and Galatea","artist":"Jean-Léon Gérôme","thumb":"h6_pygmalion","text":"Pygmalion and Galatea · Jean-Léon Gérôme · ca. 1890 · Oil on canvas · Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A Salon painting; Gérôme, also a sculptor, made a series on this artist-and-model theme. · In Ovid, the Cypriot sculptor Pygmalion carved an ivory woman so beautiful that he fell in love with her, and Aphrodite answered his prayer by bringing the statue to life. Their child was Paphos, ancestor of King Cinyras. The name Galatea for the statue was added only in the 1700s. Gérôme shows the marble turning to flesh from the top down as she bends to kiss him. · Statue brought to life by Aphrodite · Ovid’s Metamorphoses, Book 10 · Name Galatea added much later · Pygmalion · Sculptor of Cyprus whose ivory statue Aphrodite brought to life."},{"id":"h6_hermaph","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h6_hermaph.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"Sleeping Hermaphroditus","title":"Sleeping Hermaphroditus","artist":"","thumb":"h6_hermaph","text":"Sleeping Hermaphroditus · Roman copy, 2nd century CE, of a Hellenistic original · Marble; mattress added by Bernini in 1619 · Louvre, Paris · A Roman garden or villa sculpture designed to surprise: from the back it looks like a sleeping woman, from the front it reveals both sexes. · Hermaphroditus, the son of Hermes and Aphrodite, was so beautiful that the water nymph Salmacis begged the gods to join them forever, and their bodies fused into one with both male and female features. Another son of Aphrodite, Priapus (by Dionysus or Hermes), was a garden god of fertility. Bernini carved the startlingly soft mattress when the statue belonged to Cardinal Scipione Borghese. · Son of Hermes and Aphrodite · Fused with the nymph Salmacis · Bernini’s mattress, 1619 · Hermaphroditus · Child of Hermes and Aphrodite whose body merged with the nymph Salmacis. · Priapus · Rustic god of gardens and fertility, a son of Aphrodite. · Eros (Cupid) · Desire, Aphrodite’s son and companion, who makes gods and humans fall in love with his arrows."},{"id":"h6_anchises","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h6_anchises.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"Venus and Anchises","title":"Venus and Anchises","artist":"Engraving after Annibale Carracci","thumb":"h6_anchises","text":"Venus and Anchises · Engraving after Annibale Carracci · print after a fresco of 1597–1608 · Engraving · Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam; fresco in the Palazzo Farnese, Rome · A print reproducing one scene of Carracci’s ceiling for Cardinal Odoardo Farnese; prints like this spread famous frescoes across Europe. · Zeus made Aphrodite herself fall for a mortal, the Trojan shepherd-prince Anchises. As the Homeric Hymn to Aphrodite tells, she visited him disguised as a girl and later revealed herself, warning him never to boast of it. Their son was Aeneas, the hero who would escape Troy and found the Roman people. Carracci’s fresco shows Anchises removing her sandal. · Aphrodite loves the mortal Anchises · Their son: Aeneas · Shown here in a print after the fresco · Anchises · Trojan prince loved by Aphrodite; father of Aeneas."},{"id":"h6-10","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h6-10.html","h":6,"kind":"theme","label":"Desire as a dangerous power","title":"Desire as a dangerous power","artist":"","thumb":"h6_adonis","text":"Desire as a dangerous power · Aphrodite’s gifts bring joy, ruin, and transformation. · Aphrodite and Eros can overpower anyone, even Zeus. Their stories end in transformation as often as happiness: Myrrha becomes a tree, Adonis a flower, Hermaphroditus a double body, a statue a living woman. For the Greeks, desire was a force of nature like the sea, beautiful and creative but impossible to control. · Even gods fall under Aphrodite’s power · Love stories end in transformation · Desire as an uncontrollable force"},{"id":"h6_ephesus","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h6_ephesus.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"Artemis of Ephesus","title":"Artemis of Ephesus","artist":"","thumb":"h6_ephesus","text":"Artemis of Ephesus · Roman copy of a Hellenistic original of the 2nd century BCE · Marble and bronze · Capitoline Museums, Rome · A copy of the cult statue in Artemis’s great temple at Ephesus, one of the Seven Wonders of the ancient world. · At Ephesus, in Asia Minor, Artemis was worshipped as a mother goddess very unlike the slim huntress of mainland Greece. Her statue is covered with rows of rounded forms, long called breasts but possibly eggs or bulls’ parts, along with animals and bees, all symbols of fertility. The Ephesian Artemis shows how a Greek name could attach to an older local goddess. · Temple at Ephesus: a Wonder of the World · Rows of fertility symbols · Older Anatolian mother goddess · Ephesus · Greek city in Asia Minor with the vast Temple of Artemis."},{"id":"h6_niobe","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h6_niobe.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"Apollo and Diana Punishing Niobe by Killing Her Children","title":"Apollo and Diana Punishing Niobe by Killing Her Children","artist":"Abraham Bloemaert","thumb":"h6_niobe","text":"Apollo and Diana Punishing Niobe by Killing Her Children · Abraham Bloemaert · 1591 · Oil on canvas · Statens Museum for Kunst, Copenhagen · An early showpiece by the Utrecht painter, made to display his skill at twisting nude figures in the elegant Mannerist style. · Queen Niobe of Thebes boasted that her fourteen children made her greater than Leto, who had only two. Leto’s twins, Apollo and Artemis, shot down all of Niobe’s children with arrows. Bloemaert puts the archer gods in the clouds and fills the ground with the bodies of the young; Niobe’s grief turned her to stone on Mount Sipylus, still weeping as a spring. · Niobe boasts over Leto · Apollo and Artemis kill her children · Turned to weeping stone · Niobe · Theban queen whose boast against Leto cost her all her children; turned to stone."},{"id":"h6_actaeon","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h6_actaeon.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"Diana and Actaeon","title":"Diana and Actaeon","artist":"Titian","thumb":"h6_actaeon","text":"Diana and Actaeon · Titian · 1556–59 · Oil on canvas · National Gallery, London, and National Galleries of Scotland, Edinburgh · One of the poesie for Philip II of Spain, now owned jointly by the two museums. · The hunter Actaeon stumbled on Artemis bathing with her nymphs. Furious that a man had seen her naked, she turned him into a stag, and his own hounds tore him apart. Titian shows the instant of discovery: Actaeon pulls back a curtain as the goddess snatches a cloth and glares. The stag skull on the column predicts his fate. · Actaeon sees Artemis bathing · Turned into a stag, killed by his dogs · Stag skull foretells his fate · Actaeon · Hunter turned into a stag by Artemis and killed by his own hounds. · Orion · Giant hunter killed by Artemis (or her scorpion) in several versions and set among the stars."},{"id":"h6_parthenon","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h6_parthenon.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"The Parthenon","title":"The Parthenon","artist":"Iktinos and Kallikrates, with Phidias","thumb":"h6_parthenon","text":"The Parthenon · Iktinos and Kallikrates, with Phidias · 447–432 BCE · Marble · Acropolis, Athens · A temple to Athena Parthenos (“the maiden”) that also served as the treasury of Athens and its empire. · Athens built the Parthenon after the Persian Wars, on the Acropolis the Persians had burned. Its sculptures showed Athena’s birth, her contest with Poseidon for Athens, and battles of order against chaos. Inside stood Phidias’s colossal gold-and-ivory statue of the goddess. The building became the model of Classical architecture for more than two thousand years. · Temple of Athena Parthenos · Built after the Persian Wars · Model of Classical architecture · Parthenon · Temple of Athena on the Athenian Acropolis, 447–432 BCE."},{"id":"h6_varvakeion","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h6_varvakeion.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"Varvakeion Athena","title":"Varvakeion Athena","artist":"After Phidias","thumb":"h6_varvakeion","text":"Varvakeion Athena · After Phidias · Roman copy, ca. 200–250 CE, of the Athena Parthenos (438 BCE) · Marble · National Archaeological Museum, Athens · A small Roman copy of the lost cult statue that stood inside the Parthenon. · Phidias’s Athena Parthenos was about 40 feet tall, built of gold and ivory over a wooden core, and none of it survives. This small copy, found near the Varvakeion school in Athens, preserves its main features: a triple-crested helmet, the aegis with the Gorgon’s head, a figure of Victory in her right hand, and a shield and serpent at her side. · Copy of Phidias’s lost cult statue · Helmet, aegis, Victory, shield, serpent · Original: gold and ivory, ~40 ft"},{"id":"h6_snake","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h6_snake.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"“Snake Goddess”","title":"“Snake Goddess”","artist":"","thumb":"h6_snake","text":"“Snake Goddess” · Minoan, ca. 1600 BCE · Faience · Heraklion Archaeological Museum, Crete · Found in a storage pit at the palace of Knossos, probably a cult figure or offering. · This small figure holds a snake in each hand, with a cat perched on her head. Some scholars see Minoan goddesses like her as distant ancestors of Athena, who also has a sacred snake and a protective role over a city. Arthur Evans’s team restored it heavily, and parts, including the head and cat, may be modern. · Minoan figure holding snakes · Possible ancestor of Athena’s snake · Heavily restored by Evans"},{"id":"h6_spinners","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h6_spinners.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"The Spinners (The Fable of Arachne)","title":"The Spinners (The Fable of Arachne)","artist":"Diego Velázquez","thumb":"h6_spinners","text":"The Spinners (The Fable of Arachne) · Diego Velázquez · ca. 1655–60 · Oil on canvas · Museo del Prado, Madrid · Painted for a private collector in Madrid; it later entered the royal collection. · The Lydian weaver Arachne boasted that she wove better than Athena. In their contest, she wove the gods’ scandalous love affairs so perfectly that Athena struck her, and Arachne was turned into a spider. Velázquez puts working women spinning in the foreground and the myth in the bright room behind, where the tapestry copies Titian’s Rape of Europa. · Arachne challenges Athena · Turned into a spider · Myth hidden behind a workshop scene · Arachne · A weaver turned into a spider by Athena after their contest; source of “arachnid.”"},{"id":"h6-18","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h6-18.html","h":6,"kind":"theme","label":"Goddesses of women’s lives","title":"Goddesses of women’s lives","artist":"","thumb":"h6_ephesus","text":"Goddesses of women’s lives · Each goddess governs a stage or skill of a woman’s life. · Artemis protects girls before marriage, Aphrodite rules the attraction that leads to it, Hera guards marriage itself, Demeter motherhood and the harvest, Hestia the household fire, and Athena the weaving every woman was expected to master. Myths like Arachne’s or Niobe’s warn women against pride, just as Marsyas’s warns men. · Artemis: girlhood; Aphrodite: attraction · Hera and Demeter: marriage and motherhood · Athena and Hestia: weaving and home"},{"id":"h9-0","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/part-9.html","h":9,"kind":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"Demeter and Dionysus","artist":"","thumb":"h9_triptolemos","text":"Demeter and Dionysus · Myths of fertility, mystery, and ecstasy · Demeter and Dionysus are the gods of grain and wine, the two foods that kept the Greek world alive. Their myths are about loss and return: Persephone taken to the underworld and restored each spring, Dionysus born twice and arriving as a stranger. Both gods offered worshippers something the other Olympians did not, secret rites and ecstatic experiences that promised a closer bond with the divine. · Grain and wine: Demeter and Dionysus · Myths of loss, return, rebirth · Mystery rites and ecstatic worship · Kore · “The Maiden,” Persephone’s title; with Demeter she was worshipped as “the Two Goddesses.” · Berlin Painter · Krater with Demeter and Triptolemos · ca. 490 BCE"},{"id":"h9_bernini","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h9_bernini.html","h":9,"kind":"work","label":"The Abduction of Proserpina","title":"The Abduction of Proserpina","artist":"Rembrandt","thumb":"h9_bernini","text":"The Abduction of Proserpina · Rembrandt · ca. 1631 · Oil on panel · Gemäldegalerie, Berlin · Painted in Leiden early in Rembrandt’s career; it was soon in the collection of the Dutch stadholder Frederik Hendrik. · Hades seized Persephone while she gathered flowers and carried her off in his chariot to the underworld, with Zeus’s permission. Rembrandt makes it a struggle: she claws at Hades’s face while her companions cling to her dress, and the chariot plunges toward dark water. The painting is small, but the violence of the moment fills it. · Hades abducts Persephone · Her companions try to hold her back · Chariot plunges toward the underworld · Abduction of Persephone · Hades carries off Demeter’s daughter; her return for part of each year explains the seasons."},{"id":"h9_triptolemos","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h9_triptolemos.html","h":9,"kind":"work","label":"Krater with Demeter and Triptolemos","title":"Krater with Demeter and Triptolemos","artist":"Berlin Painter","thumb":"h9_triptolemos","text":"Krater with Demeter and Triptolemos · Berlin Painter · Athenian, ca. 490 BCE · Red-figure volute krater · Badisches Landesmuseum, Karlsruhe · A large wine-mixing bowl; it was found in Sicily, an island famous for its grain and its worship of Demeter. · While searching for her daughter, Demeter came to Eleusis disguised as an old woman and rested by the Well of the Maiden. The royal family took her in, and in return she taught their son Triptolemus to farm. Here he sits in a winged chariot as Demeter sends him to spread grain across the world. Eleusis became the site of the Mysteries, secret rites that promised initiates a happier afterlife. · Demeter disguised at Eleusis · Triptolemus spreads agriculture · Eleusinian Mysteries: secret initiation · Eleusis · Town near Athens where Demeter was welcomed and where her Mysteries were held. · Well of the Maiden · The well at Eleusis where the grieving Demeter sat in disguise. · Eleusinian Mysteries · Secret rites for Demeter and Persephone; initiates were promised a better lot after death. · Mystery, initiate · A mystery is a secret rite revealed only to initiates (mystai), who swore never to tell. · Triptolemus · Eleusinian prince whom Demeter taught to farm and sent out in a winged chariot. · Mater Dolorosa · “Sorrowful Mother,” the image of the grieving Virgin Mary, often compared to the mourning Demeter."},{"id":"h9-3","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h9-3.html","h":9,"kind":"theme","label":"Grief that makes the seasons","title":"Grief that makes the seasons","artist":"","thumb":"h9_bernini","text":"Grief that makes the seasons · A mother’s grief and a daughter’s return explain the farming year. · When Persephone vanished, Demeter stopped the crops from growing and humanity nearly starved, until Zeus ordered her return. Because Persephone had eaten pomegranate seeds in the underworld, she must go back each year. The myth ties the death and rebirth of the grain to death and hope for human beings, which is why Demeter’s Mysteries offered comfort about the afterlife. · Demeter’s grief stops the harvest · Pomegranate seeds bind Persephone to Hades · Crop cycle linked to hopes after death"},{"id":"h9_cybele","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h9_cybele.html","h":9,"kind":"work","label":"Portrait of a woman as Cybele","title":"Portrait of a woman as Cybele","artist":"","thumb":"h9_cybele","text":"Portrait of a woman as Cybele · Roman, ca. 50 CE · Marble · J. Paul Getty Museum, Los Angeles · A portrait of a Roman woman, possibly a priestess or a member of the imperial family, shown in the guise of the goddess. · Cybele, the Great Mother of Phrygia in Asia Minor, sits enthroned with a lion and a tambourine. Her young consort Attis, born when the nymph Nana conceived from an almond (a fruit of the monster Agdestis), castrated himself in a frenzy and died, and her priests imitated him. Rome officially adopted Cybele in 204 BCE. Her cult, like that of Adonis, centered on a dying young god mourned by a goddess. · Phrygian Great Mother, with lions · Consort Attis dies and is mourned · Adopted by Rome in 204 BCE · Cybele · Great Mother goddess of Phrygia, shown with lions and a turreted crown. · Attis · Cybele’s young consort who castrated himself and died; mourned in her rites. · Agdestis · A double-sexed monster in Attis’s myth whose castration produced the almond tree. · Nana · Daughter of a river god who conceived Attis from an almond. · Cult of Adonis · Women’s yearly mourning for Aphrodite’s dead lover, a vegetation god who dies young."},{"id":"h9_bacchus","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h9_bacchus.html","h":9,"kind":"work","label":"Bacchus","title":"Bacchus","artist":"Caravaggio","thumb":"h9_bacchus","text":"Bacchus · Caravaggio · ca. 1596–98 · Oil on canvas · Uffizi Gallery, Florence · Probably painted for Cardinal Francesco del Monte, Caravaggio’s early patron, and given to the Medici. · Dionysus was the son of Zeus and the Theban princess Semele, who was burned up when Zeus showed himself in full glory; Zeus sewed the unborn child into his thigh and gave birth to him there. Semele, brought up from Hades and made immortal, was renamed Thyone. Caravaggio paints a flushed young model in a toga, offering a glass of wine beside a bowl of overripe fruit. · Twice born: from Semele and from Zeus’s thigh · Semele becomes the goddess Thyone · Caravaggio’s down-to-earth god · Semele (Thyone) · Theban princess, mother of Dionysus, destroyed by Zeus’s lightning and later deified as Thyone. · Ino (Leucothea) and Athamas · Semele’s sister and her husband, who nursed the infant Dionysus; Hera drove them mad, and Ino became the sea goddess Leucothea."},{"id":"h9_exekias","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h9_exekias.html","h":9,"kind":"work","label":"Dionysus Cup","title":"Dionysus Cup","artist":"Exekias","thumb":"h9_exekias","text":"Dionysus Cup · Exekias · Athenian, ca. 530 BCE · Black-figure kylix (interior) · Staatliche Antikensammlungen, Munich · A drinking cup for the symposium; as the wine was drunk, the god appeared sailing on a wine-colored sea. · In a Homeric Hymn, pirates kidnap Dionysus, thinking he is a prince. Vines sprout from the mast, wine floods the ship, and the god becomes a lion; the terrified sailors leap overboard and turn into dolphins. Exekias, the greatest black-figure painter, fills the whole interior with coral-red slip so the scene floats on a glowing sea. · Pirates kidnap Dionysus · Vines from the mast, sailors become dolphins · Coral-red interior turns the cup into a sea · And all at once a vine spread out both ways along the top of the sail with many clusters hanging down from it"},{"id":"h9_maenad","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h9_maenad.html","h":9,"kind":"work","label":"A Bacchanalian Revel before a Term","title":"A Bacchanalian Revel before a Term","artist":"Nicolas Poussin","thumb":"h9_maenad","text":"A Bacchanalian Revel before a Term · Nicolas Poussin · ca. 1632–33 · Oil on canvas · National Gallery, London · Painted in Rome, where Poussin spent most of his career; his bacchanals revived the ancient wine feast in a cool, frieze-like style learned from Roman reliefs. · Maenads (“mad women”), also called Bacchae, were the female followers of Dionysus who roamed the mountains in ecstatic dances. Poussin shows them in a ring dance with satyrs before a garlanded statue of Pan, wine and fruit spilling around them. The rites could end in sparagmos, tearing a living animal apart. · Maenads: Dionysus’s female followers · Dance, wine, and the god Pan · Ecstasy in the countryside · Bacchae, Thyiades, Maenads · Names for the frenzied women who worshipped Dionysus. · Thyrsus · A fennel staff topped with a pinecone and ivy, carried by Dionysus’s followers. · Enthousiasmos · Being filled with the god (“en-theos”), the root of “enthusiasm.” · Ekstasis · “Standing outside oneself,” the ecstatic state Dionysus’s rites produced."},{"id":"h9_midas","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h9_midas.html","h":9,"kind":"work","label":"Midas and Bacchus","title":"Midas and Bacchus","artist":"Nicolas Poussin","thumb":"h9_midas","text":"Midas and Bacchus · Nicolas Poussin · ca. 1624 · Oil on canvas · Alte Pinakothek, Munich · An early painting from Poussin’s first years in Rome; it later belonged to the Bavarian elector Max Emanuel. · King Midas of Phrygia, in some versions a son of Cybele, sheltered Dionysus’s drunken tutor Silenus, and the grateful god granted him a wish. Midas asked that everything he touched turn to gold, then nearly starved. Poussin shows Midas kneeling to thank Bacchus while satyrs and Silenus sleep off the wine; Midas was cured by bathing in the river Pactolus in Lydia, whose sands turned golden. · Midas hosts Silenus · The golden touch · Cured in the river Pactolus · Midas · King of Phrygia granted the golden touch by Dionysus. · Satyrs · Male followers of Dionysus, part man and part horse or goat, lovers of wine and mischief. · Lydia · Kingdom in western Asia Minor, rich in gold from the river Pactolus."},{"id":"h9_pentheus","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h9_pentheus.html","h":9,"kind":"work","label":"The Death of Pentheus","title":"The Death of Pentheus","artist":"","thumb":"h9_pentheus","text":"The Death of Pentheus · Roman, 62–79 CE · Wall painting · House of the Vettii, Pompeii · Part of the painted decoration of a dining room in a wealthy freedmen’s house, where mythological scenes impressed guests. · King Pentheus of Thebes, Dionysus’s cousin, refused to accept the new god and tried to suppress his rites. In Euripides’ tragedy the Bacchae, Dionysus lures him to spy on the maenads, who in their frenzy mistake him for a beast and tear him apart, led by his own mother, Agave. The painting shows the women closing in as Pentheus falls. · Pentheus rejects Dionysus · Torn apart by the maenads · Euripides’ Bacchae · Pentheus · King of Thebes torn apart by maenads, including his mother, for rejecting Dionysus."},{"id":"h9-10","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h9-10.html","h":9,"kind":"theme","label":"The god who breaks boundaries","title":"The god who breaks boundaries","artist":"","thumb":"h9_maenad","text":"The god who breaks boundaries · Dionysus dissolves the lines between human and animal, sane and mad, self and god. · Dionysus arrives as a foreigner and is resisted by kings like Pentheus, who are destroyed for refusing him. His gifts, wine, ecstasy, and theater, let worshippers step outside ordinary life for a time. The myths warn that this power must be honored in its place: welcomed, it brings joy; denied, it explodes. · Foreign god resisted and then accepted · Wine and ecstasy loosen ordinary limits · Denying him brings destruction"},{"id":"h9_andrians","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h9_andrians.html","h":9,"kind":"work","label":"The Bacchanal of the Andrians","title":"The Bacchanal of the Andrians","artist":"Titian","thumb":"h9_andrians","text":"The Bacchanal of the Andrians · Titian · 1523–26 · Oil on canvas · Museo del Prado, Madrid · Painted for Alfonso d’Este, Duke of Ferrara, as a companion to Bacchus and Ariadne in his private study. · Based on an ancient description of a painting by Philostratus, the scene shows the people of the island of Andros, where a river ran with wine thanks to Dionysus. Dancers, drinkers, and a sleeping nude enjoy a festival of the god. A sheet of music in the foreground reads, in French, that whoever drinks and does not drink again does not know what drinking is. · Island of Andros with a river of wine · Based on an ancient text by Philostratus · Painted for the Duke of Ferrara · Bacchanal · A festival or revel in honor of Bacchus; Rome’s Bacchanalia were banned in 186 BCE."},{"id":"h9_epidaurus","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h9_epidaurus.html","h":9,"kind":"work","label":"Theater of Epidaurus","title":"Theater of Epidaurus","artist":"Attributed to Polykleitos the Younger","thumb":"h9_epidaurus","text":"Theater of Epidaurus · Attributed to Polykleitos the Younger · ca. 340–300 BCE · Limestone · Epidaurus, Greece · A theater in the sanctuary of the healing god Asclepius, famous for its acoustics and still used for performances. · Greek drama grew out of festivals for Dionysus, and in Athens tragedies and comedies were performed at his festival, the City Dionysia. A theater had a curved seating area (theatron), a round dancing floor for the chorus (orchestra), and a stage building behind (skene). Epidaurus, the best preserved, holds about 13,000 people. · Drama born in Dionysus’s festivals · Theatron, orchestra, skene · Best-preserved Greek theater · Theatron · “Viewing place,” the curved seating of a Greek theater. · Orchestra · “Dancing place,” the circular floor where the chorus performed. · Cult of Dionysus · Worship through wine, ecstatic dance, sacrifice, and drama."},{"id":"h10-0","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/part-10.html","h":10,"kind":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"Myths of Death","artist":"","thumb":"h10_patinir","text":"Myths of Death · The underworld and its visitors · For most Greeks, the dead became weak, shadowy images of themselves in the gloomy realm of Hades, whatever kind of life they had lived. Only a few great sinners were singled out for punishment and a few favored heroes for paradise. Over the centuries, mystery cults, philosophers, and Roman poets turned this shadowland into a place of judgment and reward, a picture that passed into Christian ideas of hell and heaven. · Hades: shadowy afterlife for most · Punishment for a few, paradise for fewer · Later writers add judgment and reward · Soul (psyche, anima) · The breath or life-spirit that leaves the body at death; psyche in Greek, anima in Latin. · Hades · “The unseen one,” the god and his realm; the Romans called the god Pluto, “the enricher.” · Orcus · A Roman name for the underworld and its god, “the place that confines.” · Eidolon · The image or shade of a dead person, a thin copy of the living. · Anthesteria · Athenian spring festival of new wine when the spirits of the dead were believed to roam, sometimes compared to Halloween. · Joachim Patinir · Charon Crossing the Styx · ca. 1520–24"},{"id":"h10_charon","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h10_charon.html","h":10,"kind":"work","label":"White-ground lekythos with Charon","title":"White-ground lekythos with Charon","artist":"","thumb":"h10_charon","text":"White-ground lekythos with Charon · Athenian, later 5th century BCE · Terracotta · Museum für Kunst und Gewerbe, Hamburg · An oil flask painted for the dead: lekythoi with white grounds were left at graves and buried with the deceased. · Hermes, as guide of souls, led the dead to the river, where the ferryman Charon rowed them across for the price of a coin. Families placed an obol in the mouth of the dead for the fare. White-ground lekythoi, painted in delicate colors on a chalky white surface, show these last journeys and quiet farewells at the tomb. · Hermes leads, Charon ferries · Coin for the fare · Grave offerings for the dead · Psychopompos · “Guide of souls,” Hermes’s role in leading the dead. · Charon · The ferryman who carries the dead across the river of the underworld. · Lekythos · A narrow oil flask; white-ground lekythoi were funeral offerings."},{"id":"h10_patinir","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h10_patinir.html","h":10,"kind":"work","label":"Charon Crossing the Styx","title":"Charon Crossing the Styx","artist":"Joachim Patinir","thumb":"h10_patinir","text":"Charon Crossing the Styx · Joachim Patinir · ca. 1520–24 · Oil on panel · Museo del Prado, Madrid · A landscape painting whose first owner is not recorded; it later belonged to Philip II of Spain. · Patinir places Charon’s boat in the middle of a vast river between paradise on the left, with angels, and hell on the right, guarded by the three-headed dog Cerberus. The small soul in the boat looks toward hell. The painting mixes the classical ferryman with the Christian choice between salvation and damnation. · Charon’s boat between two shores · Paradise vs. hell, guarded by Cerberus · Classical myth meets Christian judgment · Styx and Acheron · Rivers of the underworld; the Styx was the river of oaths, the Acheron the river of woe. · Cerberus · Many-headed hound guarding the gates of Hades."},{"id":"h10_tiresias","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h10_tiresias.html","h":10,"kind":"work","label":"Tiresias Appears to Ulysses during the Sacrifice","title":"Tiresias Appears to Ulysses during the Sacrifice","artist":"Henry Fuseli","thumb":"h10_tiresias","text":"Tiresias Appears to Ulysses during the Sacrifice · Henry Fuseli · 1780–85 · Watercolor · Albertina, Vienna · A large watercolor by the Swiss-born painter, who settled in London and specialized in dramatic scenes from Homer, Shakespeare, and Milton. · In Odyssey 11, the witch Circe sends Odysseus to the edge of the world, the land of the Cimmerians, to consult the dead seer Tiresias. He digs a pit and pours in sacrificial blood, and the shades flock to drink it. Fuseli shows the blind prophet rising in a cloud of shades while Odysseus crouches by the pit, sword drawn. Odysseus’s visit is an act of necromancy, calling up the dead to learn the future. · Circe sends Odysseus to the dead · Blood offering draws the shades · Tiresias foretells his journey home · Tiresias · The blind Theban prophet who keeps his wisdom even in death. · Circe · Witch-goddess who sends Odysseus to consult the dead. · Necromancer · One who calls up the dead to ask about the future. · Cimmerians · A people living in perpetual mist at the edge of the world, near the entrance to Hades."},{"id":"h10-4","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h10-4.html","h":10,"kind":"theme","label":"A gloomy afterlife for almost everyone","title":"A gloomy afterlife for almost everyone","artist":"","thumb":"h10_charon","text":"A gloomy afterlife for almost everyone · Homer’s dead envy the living. · In the Odyssey, the shades Odysseus meets teach hard lessons. Agamemnon, murdered by his wife, warns against trusting women; Achilles says he would rather be a poor laborer on earth than king of all the dead; Ajax, still angry over a lost contest, refuses to speak. King Minos judges disputes, and Orion still hunts. Fame on earth was the only real immortality. · Agamemnon: betrayal · Achilles: life above all · Ajax: anger outlasts death · Lessons of the dead · Agamemnon, Achilles, Ajax, King Minos, and Orion appear in Odyssey 11, each showing what death does to a hero."},{"id":"h10_sisyphus","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h10_sisyphus.html","h":10,"kind":"work","label":"Sisyphus","title":"Sisyphus","artist":"Titian","thumb":"h10_sisyphus","text":"Sisyphus · Titian · 1548–49 · Oil on canvas · Museo del Prado, Madrid · One of four paintings of the great sinners (the “Furies”) made for Mary of Hungary’s palace at Binche, as a warning to rebels. · A few sinners earned eternal punishment in Tartarus. Sisyphus, who cheated death, pushes a boulder uphill forever; Tantalus stands in water that recedes when he bends to drink, beneath fruit he cannot reach; Ixion, who tried to seduce Hera, spins on a fiery wheel; and Tityus has his liver eaten by vultures. Titian’s Sisyphus bends under a rock that seems to crush him. · Sisyphus: endless boulder · Tantalus, Ixion, Tityus · Painted as warnings to rebels · Tartarus · The deepest part of the underworld, where the worst sinners are punished. · Tantalus · Tormented with hunger and thirst, food and water always out of reach; source of “tantalize.” · Sisyphus · King who cheated death and must push a boulder uphill forever. · Ixion · Bound to a spinning wheel for trying to seduce Hera; from his union with a cloud came the Centaurs. · Centaurs · Half-human, half-horse creatures, descendants of Ixion."},{"id":"h10-6","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h10-6.html","h":10,"kind":"theme","label":"Punishments that fit the crime","title":"Punishments that fit the crime","artist":"","thumb":"h10_map","text":"Punishments that fit the crime · The great sinners all tried to take what belonged to the gods. · Each famous sinner offended the gods directly: Tantalus tested them by serving his son as a meal, Ixion lusted after Hera, Sisyphus tricked Death itself. Their punishments are endless repetitions or frustrations, making the crime visible forever. The idea of matching punishment to sin lasted into Dante’s Inferno. · Sinners offended the gods themselves · Endless, repeated torments · Model for Dante’s hell"},{"id":"h10_orpheusrel","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h10_orpheusrel.html","h":10,"kind":"work","label":"Orpheus, Eurydice, and Hermes","title":"Orpheus, Eurydice, and Hermes","artist":"","thumb":"h10_orpheusrel","text":"Orpheus, Eurydice, and Hermes · Roman copy, 1st century BCE, of an Athenian relief of ca. 410 BCE · Marble · Louvre, Paris · A Roman copy of a famous Athenian relief, made for a collector who wanted a celebrated Greek work. · The poet Orpheus, whose music could move stones, went down to Hades to bring back his wife Eurydice, who had died from a snakebite. Charmed by his song, the gods let her go on one condition: he must not look back. He turned at the last moment, and she was lost. The relief shows the farewell, as Hermes gently takes her wrist to lead her back. · Orpheus charms the underworld · He looks back and loses Eurydice · Hermes leads her away · Orpheus and Eurydice · The singer who almost rescued his wife from death but looked back too soon. · Orphism · A religious movement claiming Orpheus as founder, teaching purity and the soul’s rebirth. · Metempsychosis · The transmigration of the soul into a new body after death, taught by Orphics and Pythagoreans."},{"id":"h10_corot","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h10_corot.html","h":10,"kind":"work","label":"Orpheus Leading Eurydice from the Underworld","title":"Orpheus Leading Eurydice from the Underworld","artist":"Jean-Baptiste-Camille Corot","thumb":"h10_corot","text":"Orpheus Leading Eurydice from the Underworld · Jean-Baptiste-Camille Corot · 1861 · Oil on canvas · Museum of Fine Arts, Houston · A large Salon landscape with a mythological subject, made for public exhibition. · Corot imagines the underworld as a misty, silvery grove, closer to the Elysian Fields than to a place of torment. Orpheus raises his lyre and leads Eurydice by the hand, not yet looking back. In the Greek imagination most souls drifted through dim Asphodel Meadows, while a lucky few heroes went to Elysium, a paradise of endless calm. · Underworld as a misty grove · Orpheus not yet looking back · Asphodel Meadows vs. Elysium · Asphodel Meadows · The gray plain where ordinary souls wander. · Elysium (Elysian Fields) · The paradise reserved for heroes and the favored of the gods."},{"id":"h10_map","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h10_map.html","h":10,"kind":"work","label":"The Map of Hell","title":"The Map of Hell","artist":"Sandro Botticelli","thumb":"h10_map","text":"The Map of Hell · Sandro Botticelli · ca. 1480–95 · Ink and color on parchment · Vatican Library · Part of a lavish illustrated manuscript of Dante’s Divine Comedy, probably for Lorenzo di Pierfrancesco de’ Medici. · Plato’s Republic ends with the Myth of Er, a soldier who returns from death to describe souls being judged, rewarded, punished, and choosing new lives. Ideas like these flowed into Christian visions of the afterlife. Dante’s Inferno arranges hell as a funnel of nine circles, staffed by classical figures such as Charon, Minos, and Cerberus, which Botticelli maps from top to bottom. · Plato’s Myth of Er: judgment and rebirth · Dante: hell as nine circles · Classical guardians in a Christian hell · Myth of Er · Story in Plato’s Republic of a man who returns from death to describe judgment and reincarnation. · Inferno, Purgatory, Paradise · The three parts of Dante’s Divine Comedy: hell, purgatory, and heaven."},{"id":"h10_brueghel","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h10_brueghel.html","h":10,"kind":"work","label":"Aeneas and the Sibyl in the Underworld","title":"Aeneas and the Sibyl in the Underworld","artist":"Jan Brueghel the Younger","thumb":"h10_brueghel","text":"Aeneas and the Sibyl in the Underworld · Jan Brueghel the Younger · 1630s · Oil on copper · Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A small, jewel-like painting on copper, a support often used for collectors’ cabinet pictures. · In Book 6 of Virgil’s Aeneid, the Cumaean Sibyl leads Aeneas down through a cave near Lake Avernus to visit his dead father, Anchises. They cross the Acheron in Charon’s boat and pass the places of punishment before reaching Elysium, where Anchises shows his son the souls of future Romans. Brueghel crowds the dark landscape with fires, monsters, and tiny tormented figures. · Aeneid 6: descent at Lake Avernus · Guided by the Cumaean Sibyl · Anchises shows Rome’s future · Lake Avernus · Volcanic lake near Naples believed to be an entrance to the underworld. · Cumaean Sibyl · Prophetess of Apollo at Cumae who guides Aeneas. · Anchises · Aeneas’s father, met in Elysium."},{"id":"h10-11","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h10-11.html","h":10,"kind":"theme","label":"From shadows to judgment","title":"From shadows to judgment","artist":"","thumb":"h10_orpheusrel","text":"From shadows to judgment · The afterlife becomes moral over time. · Homer’s underworld is mostly the same for everyone. Mystery cults promised initiates a better fate, Orphics and Plato added judgment and reincarnation, and Virgil arranged the dead by their deeds and gave Rome a destiny. Dante then built hell, purgatory, and heaven from these classical pieces, and artists kept painting the result for centuries. · Homer: same fate for nearly all · Mysteries, Orphics, Plato: reward and rebirth · Virgil and Dante: an ordered moral world"},{"id":"h11-0","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/part-11.html","h":11,"kind":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"Perseus and the Heroes of Argos","artist":"","thumb":"h11_cellini","text":"Perseus and the Heroes of Argos · The pattern of the hero, Medusa, and Andromeda · Greek heroes are mortal sons of gods whose lives follow a familiar pattern: a strange birth, a dangerous quest, help from the gods, monsters defeated, and a reward at the end. Perseus, the great hero of Argos, fits the pattern almost perfectly. His family tree also links many myths of the Argive plain, from Io and the Danaïds to his great-grandson Heracles. · Heroes follow a shared pattern of folktale motifs · Perseus: hero of Argos and ancestor of Heracles · Argive line: Io → Danaïds → Danaë → Perseus · Folktale motifs in heroic myth · Divine parent, miraculous birth, great strength, a loyal companion, impossible tasks set by an enemy, a broken taboo, a dangerous woman resisted, a quest (often to the underworld), help from gods, return and atonement, a great reward, and a hero’s funeral or deification. · Inachus and Melia · The river god of Argos and an ash-tree nymph, parents of Io in some versions. · Epaphus · Son of Zeus and Io, born in Egypt; ancestor of Danaus, Acrisius, and Perseus. · Acrisius · King of Argos who locked his daughter Danaë away because an oracle said her son would kill him. · Benvenuto Cellini · Perseus with the Head of Medusa · 1545–54"},{"id":"h11-1","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h11-1.html","h":11,"kind":"theme","label":"The pattern of the hero","title":"The pattern of the hero","artist":"","thumb":"h11_rubens","text":"The pattern of the hero · Heroic myths are built from the same parts, rearranged. · Perseus has a god for a father and a miraculous birth, is sent on an impossible quest by a hostile king, receives magical gifts from the gods, kills a monster, rescues a princess, and returns to punish his enemy. Heracles, Theseus, and Jason follow versions of the same plan. Recognizing the pattern helps explain why these stories feel familiar and why they were easy to retell. · Divine birth, quest, monster, reward · Help from gods and magical objects · Same plan behind Heracles, Theseus, Jason"},{"id":"h11_graiae","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h11_graiae.html","h":11,"kind":"work","label":"Perseus and the Graiae","title":"Perseus and the Graiae","artist":"Edward Burne-Jones","thumb":"h11_graiae","text":"Perseus and the Graiae · Edward Burne-Jones · ca. 1875–78 · Gesso relief with gold and silver leaf on oak panel · National Museum Cardiff · One panel of a Perseus cycle commissioned by the politician Arthur Balfour for the music room of his London house. · Danaë and the infant Perseus were set adrift in a chest and washed ashore on the island of Seriphos, where the fisherman Dictys took them in. King Polydectes, wanting Danaë, sent Perseus to fetch the head of the Gorgon Medusa. To find her, Perseus seized the single eye and tooth shared by the Graiae, the Gorgons’ gray-haired sisters, until they gave him directions. Burne-Jones shows him snatching the eye as the three blind sisters grope for it. · Danaë and Perseus washed ashore on Seriphos · Polydectes sends Perseus for Medusa’s head · Perseus steals the Graiae’s shared eye · Dictys and Polydectes · Brothers on Seriphos: the kind fisherman who rescued Danaë, and the king who sent Perseus away so he could force her into marriage. · Graiae · “The gray ones,” three old sisters of the Gorgons who share one eye and one tooth."},{"id":"h11_selinunte","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h11_selinunte.html","h":11,"kind":"work","label":"Metopes from Temple C at Selinus","title":"Metopes from Temple C at Selinus","artist":"","thumb":"h11_selinunte","text":"Metopes from Temple C at Selinus · Greek (Sicily), ca. 540–530 BCE · Limestone · Regional Archaeological Museum, Palermo · Carved panels from the frieze of a temple, probably to Apollo, in the Greek colony of Selinus in Sicily. · The center metope shows Perseus cutting off Medusa’s head while Athena stands by and Medusa clutches the winged horse Pegasus. The Gorgons were three sisters: Stheno (“strength”), Euryale (“far-leaper”), and the mortal Medusa (“ruler”). Perseus used the gifts of the gods, winged sandals, a cap of invisibility, a curved sword, and a bag, and looked only at her reflection to avoid being turned to stone. From her severed neck sprang Pegasus and Chrysaor, her sons by Poseidon. · Perseus beheads Medusa with Athena’s help · Gifts: sandals, cap, sword, bag · Pegasus and Chrysaor spring from her neck · Gorgons · Stheno, Euryale, and Medusa, snake-haired sisters whose gaze turns people to stone; only Medusa was mortal. · Metope · A square panel, often carved, set between the grooved blocks of a Doric temple frieze."},{"id":"h11_caravaggio","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h11_caravaggio.html","h":11,"kind":"work","label":"Medusa","title":"Medusa","artist":"Caravaggio","thumb":"h11_caravaggio","text":"Medusa · Caravaggio · ca. 1597–98 · Oil on canvas mounted on a wooden shield · Uffizi Gallery, Florence · A ceremonial parade shield, given by Cardinal Francesco del Monte to Ferdinando I de’ Medici, Grand Duke of Tuscany. · Even after death, Medusa’s head kept its power, and Perseus gave it to Athena, who set it on her aegis. Images of the Gorgon’s face were placed on shields, armor, and temples to frighten away enemies and evil. Caravaggio paints the head at the instant of beheading, still screaming, on a real convex shield, so the viewer, like Perseus, sees her only as a reflection. · Medusa’s head kept its petrifying power · Gorgon face as a protective device · Painted on a real shield · Gorgoneion · An image of the Gorgon’s face used on shields, armor, and buildings to ward off evil."},{"id":"h11-5","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h11-5.html","h":11,"kind":"theme","label":"The face that protects","title":"The face that protects","artist":"","thumb":"h11_baleful","text":"The face that protects · The monster’s deadly face becomes a weapon against danger. · Medusa’s gaze kills, but once she is defeated her face turns into protection. Greeks painted Gorgon faces on shields and roof tiles, and Athena wears one on her breastplate. Later artists from Cellini to Caravaggio used the severed head as a symbol of power over enemies, and modern writers have read her as an image of fear of female power. · Deadly gaze turned into protection · On shields, armor, temples, and Athena’s aegis · A symbol of power over enemies · Apotropaic · Meant to turn away evil or bad luck, like a Gorgon face on a shield."},{"id":"h11_cellini","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h11_cellini.html","h":11,"kind":"work","label":"Perseus with the Head of Medusa","title":"Perseus with the Head of Medusa","artist":"Benvenuto Cellini","thumb":"h11_cellini","text":"Perseus with the Head of Medusa · Benvenuto Cellini · 1545–54 · Bronze · Loggia dei Lanzi, Florence · Commissioned by Duke Cosimo I de’ Medici for the public square in front of the city hall, as a message about his power. · Perseus stands on Medusa’s body holding up her head, blood pouring from her neck in curling bronze streams. Cellini, a goldsmith, cast the statue in one daring pour that he described in his autobiography. Set near Donatello’s Judith and Michelangelo’s David, both symbols of the Florentine republic, the Perseus told citizens that Medici rule had beheaded its enemies. · Perseus holds up Medusa’s head · Cast in a single difficult pour · Political message for Medici Florence"},{"id":"h11_titian","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h11_titian.html","h":11,"kind":"work","label":"Perseus and Andromeda","title":"Perseus and Andromeda","artist":"Titian","thumb":"h11_titian","text":"Perseus and Andromeda · Titian · 1554–56 · Oil on canvas · Wallace Collection, London · One of the poesie, mythological paintings Titian made for King Philip II of Spain. · On his way home, Perseus found the Ethiopian princess Andromeda chained to a rock by the sea. Her mother, Queen Cassiopeia, had boasted that she was more beautiful than the Nereids, the sea nymphs, and Poseidon sent a sea monster to punish the kingdom; an oracle said only Andromeda’s sacrifice would end it. Titian shows Perseus diving down from the sky while the pale, chained princess twists away from the monster below. · Cassiopeia’s boast offends the Nereids · Andromeda chained as a sacrifice · Perseus swoops down to save her · Cepheus and Cassiopeia · King and queen of Ethiopia, parents of Andromeda; all three became constellations. · Nereids · Sea nymphs, the fifty daughters of Nereus, including Amphitrite and Thetis. · Ceto · A sea monster (or the sea goddess who mothered monsters) sent to attack Ethiopia."},{"id":"h11_rubens","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h11_rubens.html","h":11,"kind":"work","label":"Perseus Freeing Andromeda","title":"Perseus Freeing Andromeda","artist":"Peter Paul Rubens","thumb":"h11_rubens","text":"Perseus Freeing Andromeda · Peter Paul Rubens · ca. 1620–22 · Oil on canvas · Hermitage Museum, St. Petersburg · Rubens probably kept it in his own house in Antwerp until his death; it reached the Hermitage in 1769. · Rubens skips the fight and shows the aftermath: Perseus, crowned by Victory, gently frees Andromeda while cupids lead Pegasus and the dead monster lies below. Perseus later used Medusa’s head to turn Polydectes and his court to stone, returned the gifts of the gods, and, as foretold, accidentally killed his grandfather Acrisius with a discus. He then ruled Tiryns, and his granddaughter Alcmena’s son was Heracles. · Perseus frees Andromeda, crowned by Victory · Petrifies Polydectes; kills Acrisius by accident · Founds the royal line of Heracles · Andromeda · Ethiopian princess rescued by Perseus and later his wife; a constellation."},{"id":"h11-9","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h11-9.html","h":11,"kind":"theme","label":"The girl’s tragedy","title":"The girl’s tragedy","artist":"","thumb":"h11_titian","text":"The girl’s tragedy · Many myths follow a young woman through loss, suffering, and rescue. · Io, Danaë, and Andromeda all follow a pattern scholars call the girl’s tragedy: a princess is separated from her home, loved or threatened by a god or monster, punished or exposed, and finally rescued, often bearing a hero son. The story mirrors a Greek girl’s life, leaving her father’s house for marriage. In art it gave painters a reason to show female suffering and rescue. · Leaving home, threat, punishment, rescue · Mirrors the move from girl to bride · Io, Danaë, Andromeda"},{"id":"h11_baleful","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h11_baleful.html","h":11,"kind":"work","label":"The Baleful Head","title":"The Baleful Head","artist":"Edward Burne-Jones","thumb":"h11_baleful","text":"The Baleful Head · Edward Burne-Jones · 1885 · Gouache · Southampton City Art Gallery · One of the gouache designs for Burne-Jones’s Perseus cycle, commissioned by Arthur Balfour for the music room of his London house; this one plans the cycle’s last scene. · The cycle ends with Perseus showing Andromeda Medusa’s head reflected in the still water of a well, so she can see it safely. Burne-Jones turns the story’s violence into a hushed, dreamy love scene among apple trees. The mirror motif ties the ending back to the shield Perseus used to approach Medusa. · Medusa’s head seen in a well’s reflection · Violence turned into quiet romance · Final scene of the cycle"},{"id":"h12-0","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/part-12.html","h":12,"kind":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"Heracles","artist":"","thumb":"h12_pollaiuolo","text":"Heracles · The greatest hero and his twelve labors · Heracles, “the glory of Hera,” was the strongest of the Greek heroes and the only one worshipped as a god across the Greek world. Son of Zeus and the mortal Alcmena, he was persecuted by Hera from birth, driven mad, and forced to perform twelve impossible labors. His life mixes superhuman deeds with violence, appetite, and suffering, and it ends with his rise to Olympus. · Son of Zeus and Alcmena; enemy of Hera · Twelve labors for King Eurystheus · Mortal hero who becomes a god · Alcmena and Amphitryon · Heracles’ mortal mother and her husband; Amphitryon fathered his twin, Iphicles. · Eurystheus · King of Tiryns and Mycenae, Heracles’ cousin, who set the twelve labors. · Labors, deeds, side-deeds · The labors (athloi) were the twelve tasks set by Eurystheus; deeds (praxeis) were other adventures; side-deeds (parerga) happened along the way. · Antonio del Pollaiuolo · Hercules and the Hydra · ca. 1475"},{"id":"h12_farnese","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h12_farnese.html","h":12,"kind":"work","label":"Farnese Hercules","title":"Farnese Hercules","artist":"After Lysippos","thumb":"h12_farnese","text":"Farnese Hercules · After Lysippos · Roman copy, early 3rd century CE, of a Greek bronze of the 4th century BCE · Marble, signed by Glykon of Athens · National Archaeological Museum, Naples · photograph by Giorgio Sommer, 1860s · A colossal statue made for the Baths of Caracalla in Rome, where bathers would see the hero resting after his labors. · The hero leans exhausted on his club, draped with the skin of the Nemean lion. Behind his back he holds the golden apples of the Hesperides, so this is the end of his labors, not the beginning. Hera sent snakes to kill him in his cradle, but the infant strangled them; later she drove him mad, and he killed his first wife, Megara, and their children. To be cleansed of this blood guilt he served Eurystheus. · Resting hero with club and lion skin · Golden apples hidden behind his back · Labors as penance for killing his family · Megara · Heracles’ first wife, killed with their children when Hera drove him mad. · Miasma · The blood pollution Heracles had to wash away through his labors."},{"id":"h12_crossroads","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h12_crossroads.html","h":12,"kind":"work","label":"The Choice of Hercules","title":"The Choice of Hercules","artist":"Annibale Carracci","thumb":"h12_crossroads","text":"The Choice of Hercules · Annibale Carracci · 1596 · Oil on canvas · Museo di Capodimonte, Naples · The central ceiling painting of the private study (Camerino) of Cardinal Odoardo Farnese in his Roman palace. · In a story told by the Greek writer Prodicus, the young Heracles meets two women at a crossroads: Pleasure offers an easy life, while Virtue points to a steep, rocky path that leads to glory. Heracles chooses Virtue. Carracci set the scene above the desk of a young cardinal, as a reminder to choose duty over pleasure. · Pleasure vs. Virtue at a crossroads · Heracles chooses the hard road · Moral lesson for a young cardinal · Psychomachia · A “battle for the soul” between virtues and vices; Heracles at the crossroads became a favorite image of it."},{"id":"h12_pollaiuolo","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h12_pollaiuolo.html","h":12,"kind":"work","label":"Hercules and the Hydra","title":"Hercules and the Hydra","artist":"Antonio del Pollaiuolo","thumb":"h12_pollaiuolo","text":"Hercules and the Hydra · Antonio del Pollaiuolo · ca. 1475 · Tempera on panel · Uffizi Gallery, Florence · one of a pair with Hercules and Antaeus · One of two small panels repeating large canvases of the labors made for the Medici palace in Florence, where Hercules was a civic hero. · The first labors cleared the land of monsters: the Nemean lion, whose skin Heracles wore, and the nine-headed Hydra of Lerna, which grew two heads for each one cut off until his nephew Iolaus burned the stumps. Then came the Golden Hind of Artemis, the Erymanthian Boar, the filthy stables of King Augeas, and the man-eating Stymphalian Birds. Pollaiuolo, an expert in anatomy, gives the hero taut, straining muscles against a wide Tuscan landscape. · Labors 1–6 in the Peloponnese · Hydra’s heads seared by Iolaus · Pollaiuolo’s study of muscles in action · Labors 1–6 · The Nemean Lion, the Lernaean Hydra, the Golden Hind of Artemis, the Erymanthian Boar, the Augean Stables, and the Stymphalian Birds. · Iolaus · Heracles’ nephew and loyal companion, who helped him defeat the Hydra. · Chiron · Wise centaur accidentally wounded by Heracles during the boar hunt; he gave up his immortality. · Olympic Games · Said to have been founded by Heracles after he cleaned the Augean stables."},{"id":"h12_antaios","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h12_antaios.html","h":12,"kind":"work","label":"Krater with Heracles wrestling Antaeus","title":"Krater with Heracles wrestling Antaeus","artist":"Euphronios","thumb":"h12_antaios","text":"Krater with Heracles wrestling Antaeus · Euphronios · Athenian, ca. 515–510 BCE · Red-figure calyx krater · Louvre, Paris · A large bowl for mixing wine and water at a symposium, by one of the first great red-figure painters. · On his way to the apples of the Hesperides, Heracles fought Antaeus, a giant son of Earth who grew stronger every time he touched the ground. Heracles lifted him into the air and crushed him. Euphronios contrasts the neat, controlled hero with the wild-haired giant whose eyes roll in pain, a picture of civilization defeating savagery. · Antaeus draws strength from Earth · Heracles lifts and crushes him · Early red-figure anatomy · Antaeus · Libyan giant, son of Gaea, invincible while touching the earth."},{"id":"h12_olympia","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h12_olympia.html","h":12,"kind":"work","label":"Metope with Heracles, Atlas, and Athena","title":"Metope with Heracles, Atlas, and Athena","artist":"","thumb":"h12_olympia","text":"Metope with Heracles, Atlas, and Athena · ca. 470–456 BCE · Marble · Archaeological Museum of Olympia · One of twelve metopes of the labors on the Temple of Zeus at Olympia, the sanctuary Heracles was said to have founded. · The later labors took Heracles to the edges of the world: the Cretan Bull, the man-eating Mares of Diomedes, the belt of the Amazon queen Hippolyta, the cattle of the monster Geryon, and the golden apples of the Hesperides. For the apples, Heracles held up the sky while Atlas fetched them. Here Atlas returns with the apples while Athena quietly helps Heracles bear the weight. · Labors 7–11 at the ends of the earth · Heracles holds the sky for Atlas · Athena helps him from behind · Labors 7–11 · The Cretan Bull, the Mares of Diomedes, the Belt of the Amazon Hippolyta, the Cattle of Geryon, and the Apples of the Hesperides. · Pillars of Heracles · The rocks at the Strait of Gibraltar, said to have been set up by Heracles on his way to Geryon. · Alcestis · Queen whom Heracles rescued by wrestling Death, a side-deed on his way to the Mares of Diomedes."},{"id":"h12_andokides","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h12_andokides.html","h":12,"kind":"work","label":"Hercules and Cerberus","title":"Hercules and Cerberus","artist":"Francisco de Zurbarán","thumb":"h12_andokides","text":"Hercules and Cerberus · Francisco de Zurbarán · 1634 · Oil on canvas · Museo del Prado, Madrid · One of ten labors of Hercules painted for the Hall of Realms in Philip IV’s Buen Retiro palace in Madrid; the Spanish Habsburgs claimed Hercules as an ancestor. · The last and hardest labor was to bring Cerberus, the three-headed guard dog of the underworld, up to the living. Zurbarán’s Hercules, a heavy, muscular figure lit against the darkness, swings his club at the snarling dog. When Heracles brought Cerberus to Eurystheus, the frightened king hid in a storage jar. Going down to Hades and returning was the hero’s ultimate victory over death. · Labor 12: capture Cerberus · Painted to honor the Spanish king · A hero who returns from death · Red-figure · Greek vase technique (from ca. 530 BCE) leaving figures in the red clay against a black background, so details could be painted with a brush."},{"id":"h12-7","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h12-7.html","h":12,"kind":"theme","label":"Labors that make the world safe","title":"Labors that make the world safe","artist":"","thumb":"h12_olympia","text":"Labors that make the world safe · Heracles clears the world of monsters so civilization can grow. · Most of the labors pit the hero against wild beasts and savage beings at the edges of the known world. Each victory makes the land usable for farming, travel, and cities. Greeks across the Mediterranean claimed Heracles had visited them, and the Temple of Zeus at Olympia put all twelve labors on display as models of effort rewarded. · Monsters and savages defeated · Hero as founder and civilizer · Displayed at Olympia as a model"},{"id":"h12_nessus","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h12_nessus.html","h":12,"kind":"work","label":"Deianira Abducted by the Centaur Nessus","title":"Deianira Abducted by the Centaur Nessus","artist":"Guido Reni","thumb":"h12_nessus","text":"Deianira Abducted by the Centaur Nessus · Guido Reni · 1620–21 · Oil on canvas · Louvre, Paris · One of four scenes from Heracles’ life painted for Ferdinando Gonzaga, Duke of Mantua, for his villa. · Heracles’ second wife, Deianira, was carried off by the centaur Nessus, who offered to ferry her across a river; Heracles shot him with an arrow dipped in the Hydra’s poison. The dying centaur told Deianira to keep his blood as a love charm. Years later, jealous of a captive princess, she sent Heracles a robe soaked in it, and the poison burned his flesh. In agony he built his own funeral pyre on Mount Oeta. · Nessus seizes Deianira · Poisoned blood as a false love charm · The burning robe kills Heracles · Deianira · Heracles’ second wife, who unknowingly killed him with Nessus’s poisoned robe. · Nessus · Centaur shot by Heracles whose blood became a deadly poison. · Xenia · Guest-friendship; Heracles both honored and violated it in his travels."},{"id":"h12_apotheosis","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h12_apotheosis.html","h":12,"kind":"work","label":"The Apotheosis of Hercules","title":"The Apotheosis of Hercules","artist":"François Lemoyne","thumb":"h12_apotheosis","text":"The Apotheosis of Hercules · François Lemoyne · 1733–36 · Oil on canvas mounted on the ceiling · Salon d’Hercule, Palace of Versailles · A huge ceiling painting for a reception room at Versailles, flattering King Louis XV as a heroic, god-like ruler. · As Heracles’ body burned on the pyre, his mortal part was consumed and his divine part rose to Olympus. There he was reconciled with Hera and married her daughter Hebe, goddess of youth. Lemoyne fills the ceiling with more than a hundred figures welcoming the hero into heaven. Rulers loved the theme because it promised that great labors earn immortality. · Pyre on Mount Oeta · Rises to Olympus; marries Hebe · Royal symbol of earned glory · Apotheosis · The raising of a mortal to the status of a god. · Hebe · Goddess of youth, daughter of Zeus and Hera, Heracles’ wife on Olympus."},{"id":"h12-10","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h12-10.html","h":12,"kind":"theme","label":"The flawed hero","title":"The flawed hero","artist":"","thumb":"h12_nessus","text":"The flawed hero · Heracles’ strength is matched by his excesses and his suffering. · Heracles kills his own family in madness, eats and drinks enormously, rages against gods and men, and dies through a wife’s mistaken jealousy. Greeks laughed at him in comedies and honored him in temples. Later Christians and moralists read his life as a struggle of virtue against appetite that ended in heaven, which is why rulers and churches alike used his image. · Madness, appetite, violence · Comic glutton and revered god · Read later as virtue rewarded"},{"id":"h13-0","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/part-13.html","h":13,"kind":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"Theseus and Crete","artist":"","thumb":"h13_theseusvase","text":"Theseus and Crete · Athens’ national hero, the Minotaur, and the Labyrinth · Athens claimed its people had sprung from its own soil, and its early kings were born from the earth. Its great hero was Theseus, who cleared the road to Athens of bandits, killed the Minotaur in Crete, and was remembered as the king who united Attica. His myths are tied to the island of Crete, where King Minos, the craftsman Daedalus, and the Labyrinth set the stage. · Athenians as “born from the earth” · Theseus: unifier and civic hero · Crete: Minos, Daedalus, the Minotaur · Autochthonous · “Sprung from the land itself,” the Athenian claim about their origins. · Cecrops · Legendary first king of Athens, half man and half snake, who judged the contest between Athena and Poseidon. · Erichthonius · Early Athenian king born from the earth after Hephaestus tried to assault Athena; raised secretly by Athena. · Amphora with Theseus killing the Minotaur · mid-6th century BCE"},{"id":"h13_caryatid","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h13_caryatid.html","h":13,"kind":"work","label":"The Porch of the Caryatids, Erechtheion","title":"The Porch of the Caryatids, Erechtheion","artist":"Carl Friedrich Heinrich Werner","thumb":"h13_caryatid","text":"The Porch of the Caryatids, Erechtheion · Carl Friedrich Heinrich Werner · 1877 · Watercolor · Benaki Museum, Athens · view of the temple on the Athenian Acropolis (built 421–406 BCE) · A travel watercolor for collectors; the temple itself sheltered Athens’ oldest cults, including the tomb of Cecrops. · The Erechtheion on the Acropolis held the sacred spots of Athens’ origin myths: the mark of Poseidon’s trident, Athena’s olive tree, and the tomb of Cecrops. Athena gave the infant Erichthonius in a closed basket to Cecrops’ daughters and forbade them to look; two did, saw the snake-child, and leapt from the Acropolis in madness. Each year young girls called Arrhephoroi carried secret objects in baskets for Athena, recalling the story. · Shrine of Athens’ founding myths · Cecrops’ daughters open the forbidden basket · Ritual of the Arrhephoria · Daughters of Cecrops · Aglauros, Herse, and Pandrosos; those who opened Athena’s basket went mad. · Arrhephoria · An Athenian ritual in which young girls carried secret objects for Athena by night. · Caryatid · A column carved as a standing woman."},{"id":"h13_procris","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h13_procris.html","h":13,"kind":"work","label":"A Satyr Mourning over a Nymph (The Death of Procris)","title":"A Satyr Mourning over a Nymph (The Death of Procris)","artist":"Piero di Cosimo","thumb":"h13_procris","text":"A Satyr Mourning over a Nymph (The Death of Procris) · Piero di Cosimo · ca. 1495 · Oil on panel · National Gallery, London · A long panel probably set into the wall paneling or furniture of a Florentine house. · The Athenian princess Procris married the hunter Cephalus, who was carried off for a time by Eos, the dawn goddess. Their marriage was poisoned by mutual testing and jealousy. Procris gave him a hound, Laelaps, that always caught its prey, and a spear that never missed; spying on him one day, she was killed by the spear when he mistook her for an animal. Piero shows a gentle satyr and the dog grieving over her body. · Cephalus, Procris, and Eos · Jealousy and a spear that never misses · Satyr and hound mourn her · Procris and Cephalus · Athenian couple whose jealousy ended when Cephalus killed Procris by accident. · Laelaps · A hound that always caught its prey; in some versions Minos gave it to Procris. · Eos (Aurora) · Goddess of the dawn, who carried off Cephalus."},{"id":"h13_theseusvase","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h13_theseusvase.html","h":13,"kind":"work","label":"Amphora with Theseus killing the Minotaur","title":"Amphora with Theseus killing the Minotaur","artist":"","thumb":"h13_theseusvase","text":"Amphora with Theseus killing the Minotaur · Athenian, mid-6th century BCE · Black-figure terracotta · Louvre, Paris · A storage jar painted with Athens’ favorite hero, popular as the city promoted Theseus in the 500s BCE. · Theseus was the son of King Aegeus of Athens (or of Poseidon) and Aethra of Troezen. Growing up, he lifted a boulder to find his father’s sword and sandals and walked to Athens, killing bandits on the way: the clubber Periphetes, Sinis the pine-bender, the sow of Crommyon, Sciron, the wrestler Cercyon, and Procrustes, who stretched or cut guests to fit his bed. At Athens Medea tried to poison him before Aegeus recognized the sword. · Son of Aegeus (or Poseidon) and Aethra · Six bandits on the road, then the Minotaur · Recognized by his father’s sword · Seven labors of Theseus · Periphetes, Sinis, the Crommyonian Sow, Sciron, Cercyon, Procrustes, and the Minotaur. · Medea · Sorceress from Colchis, then wife of Aegeus, who tried to poison Theseus. · Procrustes · Bandit who forced travelers to fit his bed by stretching or cutting them."},{"id":"h13_pasiphae","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h13_pasiphae.html","h":13,"kind":"work","label":"Daedalus presenting the wooden cow to Pasiphaë","title":"Daedalus presenting the wooden cow to Pasiphaë","artist":"","thumb":"h13_pasiphae","text":"Daedalus presenting the wooden cow to Pasiphaë · Roman, 62–79 CE · Wall painting · House of the Vettii, Pompeii · Part of the painted decoration of a reception room in a wealthy Pompeian house, paired with other myths of tragic love. · Minos, son of Zeus and Europa, became king of Crete. When he refused to sacrifice a magnificent bull to Poseidon, the god made his wife Pasiphaë desire it. The craftsman Daedalus built her a hollow wooden cow, and she bore the Minotaur, half man and half bull, which Minos shut inside the Labyrinth that Daedalus designed. Athens had to send seven youths and seven maidens to feed it. · Pasiphaë cursed with desire for a bull · Daedalus builds the wooden cow · Minotaur kept in the Labyrinth · Minos and Pasiphaë · King and queen of Crete; their children include Ariadne, Phaedra, and Androgeus. · Daedalus · Athenian master craftsman who built the cow, the Labyrinth, and wings. · Talos · A bronze giant who guarded Crete by circling it three times a day. · Androgeus · Son of Minos killed in Attica; his death led Minos to demand the human tribute from Athens."},{"id":"h13_canova","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h13_canova.html","h":13,"kind":"work","label":"Theseus and the Minotaur","title":"Theseus and the Minotaur","artist":"Master of the Campana Cassoni","thumb":"h13_canova","text":"Theseus and the Minotaur · Master of the Campana Cassoni · ca. 1510–20 · Paint on wood · Musée du Petit Palais, Avignon · The front panel of a cassone, a painted chest made for a bride’s trousseau; the painter is known only from a group of such panels in the Campana collection. · Theseus volunteered to go to Crete with the tribute. Ariadne, Minos’s daughter, fell in love with him and gave him a ball of thread to find his way out of the Labyrinth. The panel tells the whole story at once: Theseus lands and meets Ariadne and her sister Phaedra, kills the Minotaur at the center of the maze, and sails away with the sisters under the black sail he forgot to change. · Ariadne’s thread leads him out · Minotaur killed in the Labyrinth · Several moments in one panel · Labyrinth · The maze built by Daedalus to contain the Minotaur. · Minotaur · Bull-headed son of Pasiphaë, killed by Theseus."},{"id":"h13_ariadne","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h13_ariadne.html","h":13,"kind":"work","label":"Ariadne","title":"Ariadne","artist":"John William Waterhouse","thumb":"h13_ariadne","text":"Ariadne · John William Waterhouse · 1898 · Oil on canvas · private collection · Shown at the Royal Academy in London; Waterhouse painted many sleeping or dreaming women from classical myth. · On the way home Theseus abandoned Ariadne asleep on the island of Naxos, where Dionysus found and married her. Waterhouse shows her asleep by the sea while Dionysus’s leopards approach and Theseus’s ship sails off in the distance. Theseus forgot to change his black sail to white, the sign of success, and his father Aegeus, seeing it, threw himself into the sea, now called the Aegean. · Ariadne left asleep on Naxos · Black sail: Aegeus’s death, the Aegean · Dionysus marries Ariadne · Ariadne · Minos’s daughter who helped Theseus, was abandoned on Naxos, and married Dionysus. · Cocalus · King in Sicily who sheltered Daedalus; his daughters killed Minos. · Scylla (of Megara) · A princess who betrayed her father Nisus for love of Minos and was turned into a bird."},{"id":"h13_icarus","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h13_icarus.html","h":13,"kind":"work","label":"Landscape with the Fall of Icarus","title":"Landscape with the Fall of Icarus","artist":"After Pieter Bruegel the Elder","thumb":"h13_icarus","text":"Landscape with the Fall of Icarus · After Pieter Bruegel the Elder · ca. 1560s · Oil on canvas · Royal Museums of Fine Arts of Belgium, Brussels · A landscape for a collector, probably an early copy of a lost Bruegel painting. · Daedalus made wings of feathers and wax for himself and his son Icarus and warned him not to fly too high. Icarus flew toward the sun, the wax melted, and he fell into the sea. Bruegel shrinks the tragedy to a pair of legs splashing in the corner while a plowman, a shepherd, and a fisherman go on with their work, a reminder that the world takes little notice of one person’s fall. · Wings of feathers and wax · Icarus flies too high and falls · Tiny tragedy in a busy world · Icarus · Son of Daedalus who flew too near the sun and fell into the sea."},{"id":"h13-8","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h13-8.html","h":13,"kind":"theme","label":"The hero of Athens","title":"The hero of Athens","artist":"","thumb":"h13_canova","text":"The hero of Athens · Athens built Theseus into its own Heracles. · As Athens grew powerful in the 500s and 400s BCE, it promoted Theseus as a civic hero who cleared the roads, freed the city from Cretan tribute, and united Attica under one government. His cycle of labors was arranged to match Heracles’, and his image spread on vases and temples. Myth gave the young democracy a founder it could be proud of. · Labors designed to rival Heracles’ · Freed Athens from Cretan tribute · Founder figure for the democracy"},{"id":"h13_amazon","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h13_amazon.html","h":13,"kind":"work","label":"The Battle of the Amazons","title":"The Battle of the Amazons","artist":"Peter Paul Rubens","thumb":"h13_amazon","text":"The Battle of the Amazons · Peter Paul Rubens · ca. 1615–18 · Oil on panel · Alte Pinakothek, Munich · Painted for the Antwerp merchant and collector Cornelis van der Geest; it was soon famous through engravings. · Theseus joined Heracles against the Amazons, warrior women from the edge of the world, and carried off their queen Antiope (sometimes called Hippolyta). The Amazons then invaded Athens and were defeated. Rubens packs the battle onto a bridge, with horses and riders tumbling into the river below. Greek artists loved the Amazonomachy as a battle of Greek men against a reversed world where women fight. · Theseus abducts the Amazon queen · Amazons invade and are beaten · Greeks vs. the “other” · Amazonomachy · The battle between Greeks and Amazons, a favorite subject of temple and tomb sculpture. · Antiope (Hippolyta) · Amazon queen taken by Theseus; mother of Hippolytus."},{"id":"h13_metope","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h13_metope.html","h":13,"kind":"work","label":"Parthenon metope with a Lapith and a centaur","title":"Parthenon metope with a Lapith and a centaur","artist":"","thumb":"h13_metope","text":"Parthenon metope with a Lapith and a centaur · 447–438 BCE · Marble, South Metope 31 · British Museum, London · One of the carved panels on the south side of the Parthenon, the temple of Athena on the Acropolis. · At the wedding of Theseus’s friend Pirithous, king of the Lapiths, and Hippodamia, the drunken centaurs tried to carry off the bride and the women, and a great fight followed. Theseus and Pirithous later tried to kidnap brides of their own, Helen of Sparta and even Persephone; Helen’s brothers, Castor and Pollux, rescued her, and the two heroes were trapped in the underworld until Heracles freed Theseus. · Centaurs attack the Lapith wedding · Theseus and Pirithous seek divine brides · Trapped in Hades; Heracles rescues Theseus · Centauromachy · The battle of the Lapiths and centaurs, an image of reason against animal passion. · Pirithous and Hippodamia · King of the Lapiths and his bride, whose wedding the centaurs disrupted. · Castor and Pollux · Twin brothers of Helen (the Dioscuri) who rescued her from Theseus."},{"id":"h13_phaedra","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h13_phaedra.html","h":13,"kind":"work","label":"Phèdre","title":"Phèdre","artist":"Alexandre Cabanel","thumb":"h13_phaedra","text":"Phèdre · Alexandre Cabanel · 1880 · Oil on canvas · Musée Fabre, Montpellier · A Salon painting based on Racine’s tragedy Phèdre of 1677, itself drawn from Euripides. · Theseus later married Phaedra, Ariadne’s sister, who fell in love with her stepson Hippolytus. When he rejected her, she killed herself and left a letter accusing him. Theseus cursed his son, and Poseidon sent a bull from the sea that terrified Hippolytus’s horses and dragged him to his death. Cabanel shows Phaedra wasting away on her bed, consumed by forbidden love. · Phaedra loves her stepson Hippolytus · False accusation; Theseus’s curse · Euripides, then Racine · Phaedra · Daughter of Minos, wife of Theseus, who falsely accused her stepson. · Hippolytus · Son of Theseus and the Amazon queen, devoted to Artemis, killed by his father’s curse."},{"id":"h14-0","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/part-14.html","h":14,"kind":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"Thebes, Oedipus, and Jason","artist":"","thumb":"h14_ingres","text":"Thebes, Oedipus, and Jason · A cursed city, a tragic king, and the voyage of the Argo · Thebes was a city founded in blood and haunted by curses, and its royal family produced the most famous tragedy of all: Oedipus, who killed his father and married his mother without knowing it. Jason’s quest for the Golden Fleece, by contrast, is an adventure story, though it too ends in disaster when he betrays the sorceress Medea. Athenian playwrights turned both legends into tragedies still performed today. · Thebes: founded by Cadmus, cursed for generations · Oedipus: fate, guilt, and knowledge · Jason and Medea: quest and betrayal · Sophocles, Oedipus the King · Tragedy (ca. 429 BCE) in which Oedipus discovers his own crimes; Aristotle’s model of tragic plot. · Aeschylus, Seven against Thebes · Tragedy (467 BCE) about the war between Oedipus’s sons. · Sophocles, Antigone · Tragedy in which Antigone buries her brother against the king’s order. · Apollonius of Rhodes, Argonautica · Hellenistic epic (3rd century BCE) of Jason’s voyage. · Euripides, Medea · Tragedy (431 BCE) in which Medea takes revenge on Jason. · Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres · Oedipus and the Sphinx · 1808"},{"id":"h14_cadmus","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h14_cadmus.html","h":14,"kind":"work","label":"Two Followers of Cadmus Devoured by a Dragon","title":"Two Followers of Cadmus Devoured by a Dragon","artist":"Cornelis van Haarlem","thumb":"h14_cadmus","text":"Two Followers of Cadmus Devoured by a Dragon · Cornelis van Haarlem · 1588 · Oil on canvas · National Gallery, London · A dramatic showpiece of Dutch Mannerism; Hendrick Goltzius made a famous engraving after it the same year, which spread the design. · Cadmus, searching for his sister Europa, was told by Delphi to found a city where a cow lay down. When his men went for water, a dragon sacred to Ares killed them; Cadmus slew it and, on Athena’s advice, sowed its teeth, from which armed men sprang up and fought until five remained, the Spartoi, ancestors of Thebes’ nobles. Van Haarlem shows the dragon tearing into the companions in the foreground while Cadmus fights in the distance. · Cadmus follows a cow to found Thebes · Dragon of Ares kills his men · Sown teeth become the Spartoi · Cadmus · Phoenician prince who founded Thebes; he married Harmonia, daughter of Ares and Aphrodite. · Spartoi · “Sown men,” warriors grown from the dragon’s teeth. · Harmonia · Daughter of Ares and Aphrodite, wife of Cadmus; her cursed necklace brought ruin to their descendants. · Amphion and Zethus · Twin sons of Zeus and Antiope who built Thebes’ walls; Amphion married Niobe. · Dirce · Queen who mistreated Antiope and was tied to a bull by the twins."},{"id":"h14_kylix","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h14_kylix.html","h":14,"kind":"work","label":"Cup with Oedipus and the Sphinx","title":"Cup with Oedipus and the Sphinx","artist":"Oedipus Painter","thumb":"h14_kylix","text":"Cup with Oedipus and the Sphinx · Oedipus Painter · Athenian, ca. 470 BCE · Red-figure kylix · Vatican Museums · A drinking cup for the symposium, with the picture inside the bowl. · An oracle warned King Laius of Thebes that his son would kill him, so he and Queen Jocasta exposed their baby with his ankles pinned, giving him the name Oedipus, “swollen foot.” A shepherd saved him, and he was raised by King Polybus and Queen Merope of Corinth. Fleeing a prophecy that he would kill his father, he killed a stranger at a crossroads: Laius. At Thebes he answered the Sphinx’s riddle, which this cup shows him pondering. · Exposed as a baby, raised in Corinth · Kills Laius at a crossroads · Ponders the Sphinx’s riddle · Laius and Jocasta · King and queen of Thebes, Oedipus’s parents. · Polybus and Merope · King and queen of Corinth who raised Oedipus as their son. · Sphinx · A winged lion with a woman’s head who killed all who failed her riddle. · Riddle of the Sphinx · What walks on four legs in the morning, two at noon, and three in the evening? A human: crawling, walking, then with a cane."},{"id":"h14_ingres","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h14_ingres.html","h":14,"kind":"work","label":"Oedipus and the Sphinx","title":"Oedipus and the Sphinx","artist":"Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres","thumb":"h14_ingres","text":"Oedipus and the Sphinx · Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres · 1808 (enlarged 1827) · Oil on canvas · Louvre, Paris · A required study sent from Rome to the French Academy, later enlarged and sold; it made the nude hero a Neoclassical ideal. · Oedipus solved the riddle, the Sphinx killed herself, and grateful Thebes made him king and gave him the widowed queen Jocasta. They had four children: Antigone, Ismene, Polynices, and Eteocles. When plague struck, Oedipus vowed to find Laius’s killer and slowly discovered that it was himself, and that his wife was his mother. Jocasta hanged herself and Oedipus blinded himself. Ingres shows the calm, confident hero before he knows the truth. · Answers the riddle, becomes king · Discovers he killed his father, wed his mother · Blinds himself; Jocasta dies · Anagnorisis · Recognition: the moment a tragic character discovers the truth. · Peripeteia · Reversal: the sudden turn from good fortune to bad. · Antigone, Ismene, Polynices, Eteocles · The children of Oedipus and Jocasta."},{"id":"h14-4","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h14-4.html","h":14,"kind":"theme","label":"Tragedy: knowledge that destroys","title":"Tragedy: knowledge that destroys","artist":"","thumb":"h14_kylix","text":"Tragedy: knowledge that destroys · Oedipus’s search for truth is what ruins him. · Aristotle praised Oedipus the King because its recognition and reversal happen at the same moment: the more Oedipus learns, the further he falls. The play raises questions with no easy answer. Was Oedipus guilty if he acted in ignorance? Can anyone escape a prophecy? Tragedy let Athenian audiences feel pity and fear safely, and these legends of Thebes became its richest source. · Recognition and reversal together · Guilt without intent; fate vs. choice · Pity and fear, felt safely in the theater"},{"id":"h14_antigone","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h14_antigone.html","h":14,"kind":"work","label":"Antigone Gives Token Burial to the Body of Her Brother Polynices","title":"Antigone Gives Token Burial to the Body of Her Brother Polynices","artist":"Jules-Eugène Lenepveu","thumb":"h14_antigone","text":"Antigone Gives Token Burial to the Body of Her Brother Polynices · Jules-Eugène Lenepveu · 1835–98 · Watercolor, pen and ink over black chalk · Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A drawing by a French academic history painter; the museum does not record what it was made for. · After Oedipus left Thebes, his sons agreed to share the throne, but Eteocles refused to give it up. Polynices raised an army, the Seven against Thebes, and the brothers killed each other. Their uncle Creon, now king, forbade burial of the traitor Polynices. Their sister Antigone buried him anyway, choosing the laws of the gods over the king’s command, and was sentenced to be walled up alive. · Brothers kill each other: the Seven against Thebes · Creon forbids burial · Antigone obeys the gods, not the king · Seven against Thebes · The seven champions who attacked Thebes on behalf of Polynices. · Creon · Jocasta’s brother, king of Thebes after the war, who condemned Antigone."},{"id":"h14_jason","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h14_jason.html","h":14,"kind":"work","label":"Jason","title":"Jason","artist":"Gustave Moreau","thumb":"h14_jason","text":"Jason · Gustave Moreau · 1865 · Oil on canvas · Musée d’Orsay, Paris · Shown at the Paris Salon of 1865, where Moreau’s jewel-like, dreamlike myths set him apart from the realists of his day. · The Golden Fleece came from a flying ram sent to rescue Phrixus and Helle, children of King Athamas, from their stepmother Ino; Phrixus reached Colchis, where King Aeetes hung the fleece in a grove guarded by a dragon. Jason, whose uncle Pelias had taken the throne of Iolcus, was sent to fetch it. Moreau shows him standing over the dead dragon, fleece in hand, while Medea, whose magic made it possible, stands close behind. · Ram rescues Phrixus and Helle · Pelias sends Jason for the fleece · Medea’s magic wins the fleece · Phrixus and Helle · Children of Athamas and Nephele saved by the golden ram; Helle fell into the Hellespont. · Aeetes · King of Colchis, son of Helios, keeper of the fleece and father of Medea. · Pelias · Jason’s uncle, who seized the throne of Iolcus. · Argo and Argonauts · The ship built by Argus, and the heroes who sailed in her, including Heracles, Orpheus, Castor, and Pollux."},{"id":"h14_medea","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h14_medea.html","h":14,"kind":"work","label":"Medea about to Kill Her Children","title":"Medea about to Kill Her Children","artist":"Eugène Delacroix","thumb":"h14_medea","text":"Medea about to Kill Her Children · Eugène Delacroix · 1838 · Oil on canvas · Palais des Beaux-Arts, Lille · A Salon painting bought by the French state, a showpiece of Romantic drama. · On the voyage the Argonauts met the women of Lemnos, King Cyzicus, the boxer Amycus, and the blind prophet Phineus, whom the winged brothers Zetes and Calais freed from the Harpies; Heracles was left behind searching for his beloved Hylas. In Colchis, Aeetes’ daughter Medea used her magic to help Jason yoke fire-breathing bulls and take the fleece, and on the way home she brought down the bronze giant Talos. Years later Jason abandoned her for a Corinthian princess, Glauce, and Medea killed the bride and her own children. Delacroix shows her hiding in a cave, dagger in hand, as her pursuers approach. · Adventures of the Argo · Medea’s magic wins the fleece · Abandoned, she kills her children · Medea · Colchian princess and sorceress who helped Jason and took revenge when he left her. · Phineus and the Harpies · Blind prophet tormented by Harpies who snatched his food until the Argonauts drove them off. · Hylas · Heracles’ young companion, pulled into a spring by nymphs. · Talos and ichor · Bronze giant of Crete whose single vein of ichor, the gods’ blood, Medea drained. · Glauce · Corinthian princess Jason planned to marry, killed by Medea’s poisoned robe."},{"id":"h14-8","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h14-8.html","h":14,"kind":"theme","label":"The foreign woman","title":"The foreign woman","artist":"","thumb":"h14_jason","text":"The foreign woman · Medea is both the hero’s helper and his ruin. · Like Ariadne for Theseus, Medea betrays her own family to help a Greek hero, and like Ariadne she is abandoned. But Medea fights back. Euripides makes her the most frightening woman on the Greek stage, a foreigner with magic powers who refuses to be discarded. Audiences were invited both to fear her and to feel the injustice done to her. · Helper who betrays her family · Abandoned by the hero · Feared and pitied: Euripides’ Medea"},{"id":"h14_meleager","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h14_meleager.html","h":14,"kind":"work","label":"The Calydonian Boar Hunt","title":"The Calydonian Boar Hunt","artist":"Peter Paul Rubens","thumb":"h14_meleager","text":"The Calydonian Boar Hunt · Peter Paul Rubens · ca. 1611–12 · Oil on panel · J. Paul Getty Museum, Los Angeles · Painted soon after Rubens’s return from Italy; the crowded hunt borrows from the Roman sarcophagi of this myth he had studied in Rome. · Artemis sent a monstrous boar to ravage Calydon, and Prince Meleager gathered heroes, among them the huntress Atalanta, to kill it. Rubens shows Meleager driving his spear into the boar while Atalanta, who drew first blood, stands behind with her bow. Meleager gave her the prize, and in the quarrel that followed he killed his uncles; his mother, Althaea, then burned the log on which his life depended. · Artemis’s boar ravages Calydon · Atalanta draws first blood · Meleager’s life tied to a burning log · Meleager · Calydonian prince who led the boar hunt and died when his mother burned his life-log. · Calydonian Boar Hunt · The great hunt that gathered heroes from all over Greece."},{"id":"h14_atalanta","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h14_atalanta.html","h":14,"kind":"work","label":"Atalanta and Hippomenes","title":"Atalanta and Hippomenes","artist":"Guido Reni","thumb":"h14_atalanta","text":"Atalanta and Hippomenes · Guido Reni · ca. 1620–25 · Oil on canvas · Museo di Capodimonte, Naples · A large mythological painting; Reni painted two versions, the other now in the Prado, Madrid. · Atalanta, a swift huntress who wished to stay unmarried, raced every suitor and killed those she beat. Hippomenes (or Melanion) prayed to Aphrodite, who gave him three golden apples, from the Garden of the Hesperides or, in Ovid, from her own sacred grove on Cyprus. During the race he dropped them one by one, and Atalanta stopped to pick them up and lost. Reni turns the race into a slow, elegant dance of two pale bodies. · Atalanta races her suitors · Aphrodite’s golden apples · She stops to pick them up and loses · Atalanta · Virgin huntress who joined the boar hunt and was won in a footrace. · Hippomenes · Suitor who beat Atalanta with Aphrodite’s golden apples."},{"id":"h15-0","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/part-15.html","h":15,"kind":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"The Trojan War","artist":"","thumb":"h15_paris","text":"The Trojan War · The House of Atreus, Achilles, and the fall of Troy · The Trojan War was the defining legend of the Greek heroic age. It began with a golden apple and a beauty contest, drew every great Greek hero to the walls of Troy for ten years, and ended with the city’s destruction and the long, deadly homecomings of the victors. Homer’s Iliad tells only a few weeks of the tenth year, centered on the anger of Achilles. · Cause: the Judgment of Paris and Helen · Ten-year siege; Iliad covers weeks of year ten · Fall of Troy and the troubled homecomings · Homer, Iliad · Epic poem of the quarrel between Achilles and Agamemnon and its deadly results. · House of Atreus · The cursed family of Tantalus, Pelops, Atreus, Agamemnon, and Orestes. · Tantalus and Pelops · Tantalus served his son Pelops to the gods; restored to life, Pelops gave his name to the Peloponnese. · Oenomaus, Hippodamia, Myrtilos · Pelops won Hippodamia by bribing the charioteer Myrtilos to sabotage her father’s chariot, then killed him, bringing a curse on his line. · Atreus and Thyestes · Brothers whose feud led Atreus to serve Thyestes his own sons; Thyestes’ son Aegisthus later took revenge. · Peter Paul Rubens · The Judgement of Paris · ca. 1632–35"},{"id":"h15_francois","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h15_francois.html","h":15,"kind":"work","label":"The François Vase","title":"The François Vase","artist":"Kleitias (painter) and Ergotimos (potter)","thumb":"h15_francois","text":"The François Vase · Kleitias (painter) and Ergotimos (potter) · ca. 570–565 BCE · Black-figure volute krater · National Archaeological Museum, Florence · shown in an early-20th-century drawing · A great wine-mixing bowl found in an Etruscan tomb at Chiusi; its bands of about 270 figures, many labeled with their names, make it a picture book of myth. · The main band shows the gods arriving at the wedding of the mortal Peleus and the sea goddess Thetis, whose son would be Achilles. At that wedding Eris, goddess of strife, who had not been invited, threw in a golden apple marked “for the fairest.” Other bands show Achilles’ later deeds and Ajax carrying his body. Because so many figures are labeled, the vase is a key document of how Greeks told these stories. · Wedding of Peleus and Thetis · About 270 figures, many labeled · A pictorial encyclopedia of myth · Peleus and Thetis · Mortal hero and sea goddess, parents of Achilles. · Eris · Goddess of strife who threw the golden apple. · Tyndareus and Leda · King and queen of Sparta; by Zeus, Leda bore Helen and Pollux, by Tyndareus, Clytemnestra and Castor."},{"id":"h15_paris","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h15_paris.html","h":15,"kind":"work","label":"The Judgement of Paris","title":"The Judgement of Paris","artist":"Peter Paul Rubens","thumb":"h15_paris","text":"The Judgement of Paris · Peter Paul Rubens · ca. 1632–35 · Oil on panel · National Gallery, London · A large late painting by Rubens of a favorite subject for showing three ideal female nudes. · Zeus refused to decide which goddess deserved Eris’s apple and sent Hermes to ask the Trojan prince Paris, a son of King Priam and Hecuba, who was tending sheep on Mount Ida. Hera offered power, Athena victory in war, and Aphrodite the most beautiful woman in the world. Paris chose Aphrodite, and his prize was Helen, the wife of Menelaus of Sparta. Rubens shows Paris holding the apple as the three goddesses pose before him. · Hera, Athena, Aphrodite compete · Paris chooses Aphrodite and Helen · The cause of the Trojan War · Judgment of Paris · Paris’s choice of Aphrodite as fairest, which led to the abduction of Helen. · Priam and Hecuba · King and queen of Troy, parents of Hector, Paris, Cassandra, and Polyxena. · Oath of Tyndareus · Helen’s suitors swore to defend whoever married her, which bound them to fight at Troy."},{"id":"h15-3","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h15-3.html","h":15,"kind":"theme","label":"A war that began with a choice","title":"A war that began with a choice","artist":"","thumb":"h15_francois","text":"A war that began with a choice · One moment of vanity and desire sets the whole heroic world at war. · The golden apple, the beauty contest, and Helen’s departure are a chain of choices that commit gods and men to ten years of slaughter. The oath Helen’s suitors swore turns a private wrong into a war of all Greece. Later artists painted the Judgment of Paris more than almost any other myth, often as an excuse to show three nudes. · Eris’s apple, Paris’s choice · Suitors’ oath makes it a Greek war · Favorite subject of later painters"},{"id":"h15_iphigenia","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h15_iphigenia.html","h":15,"kind":"work","label":"The Sacrifice of Iphigenia","title":"The Sacrifice of Iphigenia","artist":"","thumb":"h15_iphigenia","text":"The Sacrifice of Iphigenia · Roman, 1st century CE · Wall painting from the House of the Tragic Poet, Pompeii · National Archaeological Museum, Naples · Part of the decoration of a Pompeian house named for its many scenes of drama and poetry. · The Greek fleet gathered at Aulis, but Artemis held back the winds. The seer Calchas declared that Agamemnon, the commander, must sacrifice his daughter Iphigenia. In the painting she is carried to the altar while Calchas raises a knife and Agamemnon, at left, covers his face; above, Artemis appears with the deer that, in some versions, replaced the girl at the last moment. Clytemnestra never forgave her husband. · Fleet stalled at Aulis · Agamemnon sacrifices Iphigenia · A deer substituted in some versions · Iphigenia · Daughter of Agamemnon and Clytemnestra, sacrificed (or rescued by Artemis) at Aulis. · Calchas · The Greek army’s seer. · Palamedes · Clever Greek hero who exposed Odysseus’s feigned madness and was later framed by him."},{"id":"h15_exekias","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h15_exekias.html","h":15,"kind":"work","label":"Amphora with Achilles and Ajax playing a game","title":"Amphora with Achilles and Ajax playing a game","artist":"Exekias","thumb":"h15_exekias","text":"Amphora with Achilles and Ajax playing a game · Exekias · Athenian, ca. 540–530 BCE · Black-figure terracotta · Vatican Museums · A storage jar signed by Exekias as both potter and painter, found in an Etruscan tomb at Vulci. · Achilles, raised by the wise centaur Chiron, was the greatest Greek warrior; his cousin Ajax was the next. Exekias imagines them in a quiet moment, leaning over a board game during a lull in the fighting, spears on their shoulders. Inscriptions give their throws, and Achilles, still wearing his helmet, is winning. The calm makes the violence everyone knows is coming more powerful. · Achilles and Ajax in a quiet moment · Board game during the siege · Exekias at the peak of black-figure · Achilles · Son of Peleus and Thetis, greatest Greek hero, invulnerable except at his heel in later versions. · Ajax · Huge, steadfast Greek warrior, second only to Achilles. · Chiron · Wise centaur who trained Achilles and other heroes."},{"id":"h15_briseis","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h15_briseis.html","h":15,"kind":"work","label":"Briseis Led Away from Achilles","title":"Briseis Led Away from Achilles","artist":"","thumb":"h15_briseis","text":"Briseis Led Away from Achilles · Roman, 1st century CE · Wall painting from the House of the Tragic Poet, Pompeii · National Archaeological Museum, Naples · Another painting from the same Pompeian house, probably copying a famous lost Greek painting. · The Iliad opens in the tenth year. Agamemnon had taken Chryseis, daughter of Apollo’s priest Chryses, and when Apollo sent a plague he had to return her. To make up his loss he took Achilles’ captive Briseis. Achilles, insulted, withdrew from the fighting and asked his mother Thetis to make the Greeks suffer. Here Patroclus leads Briseis away while Achilles sits in angry dignity. · Chryseis returned; Briseis taken · Achilles withdraws in anger · Patroclus hands Briseis over · Chryses and Chryseis · Priest of Apollo and his daughter, whose capture by Agamemnon brought a plague. · Briseis · Achilles’ captive, taken by Agamemnon. · Patroclus · Achilles’ closest companion."},{"id":"h15_sarpedon","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h15_sarpedon.html","h":15,"kind":"work","label":"Krater with the Death of Sarpedon","title":"Krater with the Death of Sarpedon","artist":"Euphronios","thumb":"h15_sarpedon","text":"Krater with the Death of Sarpedon · Euphronios · Athenian, ca. 515 BCE · Red-figure calyx krater · National Archaeological Museum of Cerveteri (formerly at the Metropolitan Museum) · A wine-mixing bowl looted from an Etruscan tomb, bought by the Metropolitan Museum in 1972 and returned to Italy in 2008. · With Achilles out of the fight, the Trojans drove the Greeks back to their ships. Patroclus put on Achilles’ armor and led a counterattack, killing Sarpedon, a son of Zeus who fought for Troy. Zeus allowed his son’s death but sent Sleep and Death to carry his body home. Euphronios shows the two winged brothers lifting the bleeding hero while Hermes watches. · Patroclus kills Sarpedon, son of Zeus · Sleep and Death carry him home · A famous case of looting and return · Sarpedon · Lycian prince, son of Zeus, ally of Troy, killed by Patroclus. · Hypnos and Thanatos · Sleep and Death, twin brothers."},{"id":"h15_andromache","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h15_andromache.html","h":15,"kind":"work","label":"Andromache Mourning Hector","title":"Andromache Mourning Hector","artist":"Jacques-Louis David","thumb":"h15_andromache","text":"Andromache Mourning Hector · Jacques-Louis David · 1783 · Oil on canvas · École des Beaux-Arts, Paris, on deposit at the Louvre · The painting David submitted to win admission to the French Royal Academy, shown at the Salon of 1783. · Hector, Troy’s greatest defender, killed Patroclus. Grief-stricken, Achilles returned to battle in new armor made by Hephaestus, killed Hector, and dragged his body behind his chariot. Hector’s wife Andromache and their little son Astyanax lost everything. David shows Andromache beside Hector’s body, one hand reaching toward him and the other holding her child. · Hector kills Patroclus · Achilles kills and drags Hector · Andromache and Astyanax mourn · Hector · Eldest son of Priam, Troy’s chief defender. · Andromache and Astyanax · Hector’s wife and infant son."},{"id":"h15_priam","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h15_priam.html","h":15,"kind":"work","label":"Priam Begging Achilles for the Body of Hector","title":"Priam Begging Achilles for the Body of Hector","artist":"Alexander Ivanov","thumb":"h15_priam","text":"Priam Begging Achilles for the Body of Hector · Alexander Ivanov · 1824 · Oil on canvas · Tretyakov Gallery, Moscow · A student painting at the St. Petersburg Academy of Arts, which awarded it a gold medal. · In the Iliad’s final book, old King Priam, guided by Hermes, crosses the Greek camp at night to beg Achilles for his son’s body. He kisses the hands that killed Hector and asks Achilles to remember his own father. The two enemies weep together, and Achilles returns the body. The poem ends with Hector’s funeral, not with the fall of Troy. · Priam goes to Achilles by night · Shared grief between enemies · Iliad ends with Hector’s funeral"},{"id":"h15-10","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h15-10.html","h":15,"kind":"theme","label":"Glory and its cost","title":"Glory and its cost","artist":"","thumb":"h15_exekias","text":"Glory and its cost · Achilles chooses a short, glorious life over a long, forgotten one. · Achilles knew he could live long in obscurity or die young with undying fame, and he chose fame. The Iliad honors that choice but never hides its price: the deaths of Patroclus, Sarpedon, and Hector, and the grief of fathers, wives, and children. Its last scene, Priam and Achilles weeping together, makes shared mortality the poem’s real subject. · Kleos (glory) vs. a long life · War’s cost to families · Shared grief ends the Iliad · Kleos · Glory or fame won by great deeds, the only immortality a Homeric hero could hope for."},{"id":"h15_ajax1","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h15_ajax1.html","h":15,"kind":"work","label":"Krater with the Suicide of Ajax","title":"Krater with the Suicide of Ajax","artist":"","thumb":"h15_ajax1","text":"Krater with the Suicide of Ajax · Etruscan, ca. 400–350 BCE · Red-figure calyx krater, said to be from Vulci · British Museum, London · A wine-mixing bowl made in Etruria, showing how Greek myths spread to Italy. · After Achilles was killed by an arrow from Paris, guided by Apollo, the Greeks awarded his armor to Odysseus instead of Ajax. Humiliated, Ajax went mad, then killed himself by falling on his sword. Earlier, Achilles had killed the Amazon queen Penthesilea and fallen in love with her as she died, and mocked by the soldier Thersites, killed him too. The painting shows Ajax kneeling over the sword fixed in the ground. · Death of Achilles · Armor given to Odysseus · Ajax falls on his sword · Penthesilea · Amazon queen and ally of Troy, killed by Achilles. · Thersites · Common soldier who mocked the leaders and was struck down by Achilles. · Armor of Achilles · The prize whose award to Odysseus drove Ajax to suicide."},{"id":"h15_laocoon","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h15_laocoon.html","h":15,"kind":"work","label":"Laocoön","title":"Laocoön","artist":"El Greco","thumb":"h15_laocoon","text":"Laocoön · El Greco · ca. 1610–14 · Oil on canvas · National Gallery of Art, Washington · El Greco’s only surviving painting of a classical myth, made late in his life in Toledo, Spain. · To take Troy the Greeks needed Achilles’ son Neoptolemus, the bow of Heracles, and the stolen statue of Athena called the Palladium. Then they built a wooden horse, hid soldiers inside, and pretended to sail away. The Trojan priest Laocoön warned against it; two giant serpents came from the sea and crushed him and his sons. El Greco sets the scene before a view of Toledo, with the horse heading for the city gate. · Neoptolemus, Heracles’ bow, the Palladium · Laocoön warns against the horse · Serpents crush him and his sons · Laocoön · Trojan priest killed by sea serpents after warning against the wooden horse. · Neoptolemus · Achilles’ son, brought to Troy after his father’s death. · Palladium · The ancient statue of Athena that protected Troy, stolen by Odysseus and Diomedes."},{"id":"h15_horse","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h15_horse.html","h":15,"kind":"work","label":"The Procession of the Trojan Horse into Troy","title":"The Procession of the Trojan Horse into Troy","artist":"Giovanni Domenico Tiepolo","thumb":"h15_horse","text":"The Procession of the Trojan Horse into Troy · Giovanni Domenico Tiepolo · ca. 1760 · Oil on canvas · National Gallery, London · A small painting, probably a study for a larger work, by the son of Giambattista Tiepolo. · The Trojans pulled the horse into the city and celebrated. That night the Greeks climbed out, opened the gates, and Troy was sacked. Priam was killed at an altar, Astyanax thrown from the walls, Polyxena sacrificed at Achilles’ tomb, and Cassandra, Apollo’s disbelieved prophetess, dragged from Athena’s shrine. Euripides’ Trojan Women shows the captured queens waiting to be shipped away as slaves. · Horse hauled into the city · Night sack of Troy · Fate of the Trojan women · Trojan Horse · The hollow wooden horse that carried Greek soldiers into Troy. · Cassandra and Polyxena · Daughters of Priam: Cassandra, cursed never to be believed, and Polyxena, sacrificed on Achilles’ tomb. · Euripides, The Trojan Women · Tragedy (415 BCE) about the captive women after Troy’s fall."},{"id":"h15_orestes","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h15_orestes.html","h":15,"kind":"work","label":"Orestes Pursued by the Furies","title":"Orestes Pursued by the Furies","artist":"William-Adolphe Bouguereau","thumb":"h15_orestes","text":"Orestes Pursued by the Furies · William-Adolphe Bouguereau · 1862 · Oil on canvas · Chrysler Museum of Art, Norfolk, Virginia · A large Salon painting, the kind of dramatic classical scene that made academic painters’ reputations. · The returns home, or nostoi, were often deadly. Agamemnon came back to Mycenae with Cassandra and was murdered in his bath by Clytemnestra and her lover Aegisthus. Years later his son Orestes, urged by his sister Electra and Apollo, killed his mother and Aegisthus. The Furies, avengers of family blood, hounded him until Athena’s court in Athens acquitted him. Bouguereau shows Orestes covering his ears as the Furies point at his dead mother. · Agamemnon murdered on his return · Orestes avenges him by killing his mother · Furies pursue him; Athens acquits him · Nostoi · “Returns,” the stories of the Greek heroes’ voyages home after Troy. · Aeschylus, Oresteia · Trilogy (458 BCE): Agamemnon, The Libation Bearers, and The Eumenides. · Clytemnestra and Aegisthus · Agamemnon’s wife and her lover, who killed him. · Orestes and Electra · Agamemnon’s children who avenged him; Orestes later married Hermione, daughter of Helen and Menelaus."},{"id":"h16-0","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/part-16.html","h":16,"kind":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"The Odyssey","artist":"","thumb":"h16_odland","text":"The Odyssey · Odysseus’s ten-year journey home · Homer’s Odyssey follows Odysseus, king of Ithaca, on his ten-year voyage home after Troy, and his wife Penelope and son Telemachus, who wait for him while suitors take over his palace. Where the Iliad celebrates strength, the Odyssey celebrates intelligence, endurance, and the right treatment of guests. Odysseus survives monsters, witches, and goddesses by his wits, then wins back his home in disguise. · Odysseus’s journey home from Troy · Cunning and endurance over strength · Guest-friendship tested at every stop · Homer, Odyssey · Epic poem of Odysseus’s return to Ithaca. · Xenia · Guest-friendship: the sacred duty of host and guest, honored or broken in nearly every episode. · The Odyssey Landscapes: the Laestrygonians · ca. 60–40 BCE"},{"id":"h16_turner","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h16_turner.html","h":16,"kind":"work","label":"Ulysses Deriding Polyphemus","title":"Ulysses Deriding Polyphemus","artist":"J. M. W. Turner","thumb":"h16_turner","text":"Ulysses Deriding Polyphemus · J. M. W. Turner · 1829 · Oil on canvas · National Gallery, London · A Royal Academy exhibition painting, one of Turner’s grandest treatments of classical myth. · After sacking the Cicones in Thrace and escaping the Lotus-Eaters, whose fruit made men forget home, Odysseus and his men were trapped in the cave of the Cyclops Polyphemus, who ate them two at a time. Odysseus got him drunk, told him his name was “Nobody,” blinded him with a stake, and escaped under the bellies of his sheep. As he sailed off he shouted his real name, and Polyphemus’s father Poseidon cursed his voyage. Turner shows the ship sailing into a blazing sunrise while the giant writhes on the cliff. · Cicones and Lotus-Eaters · “Nobody” blinds Polyphemus · Poseidon’s curse · Lotus-Eaters · People whose fruit made travelers forget their homes. · Polyphemus · Cyclops son of Poseidon blinded by Odysseus. · Curse of Poseidon · The god’s anger that kept Odysseus from home for ten years."},{"id":"h16_odland","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h16_odland.html","h":16,"kind":"work","label":"The Odyssey Landscapes: the Laestrygonians","title":"The Odyssey Landscapes: the Laestrygonians","artist":"","thumb":"h16_odland","text":"The Odyssey Landscapes: the Laestrygonians · Roman, ca. 60–40 BCE · Wall painting from a house on the Esquiline Hill, Rome · Vatican Library · Part of a continuous painted frieze of Odysseus’s adventures in a Roman house, found in 1848. · The wind-king Aeolus gave Odysseus a bag holding all the adverse winds, but within sight of Ithaca his men opened it, hoping for treasure, and were blown back. Next the cannibal Laestrygonians destroyed every ship but Odysseus’s own. The painter sets the tiny figures of giants hurling rocks in a vast, airy landscape, one of the earliest surviving landscape paintings in Western art. · Aeolus’s bag of winds opened · Laestrygonians sink the fleet · Early landscape painting · Aeolus · King of the winds who tried to help Odysseus home. · Laestrygonians · Cannibal giants who destroyed most of Odysseus’s ships."},{"id":"h16_circe","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h16_circe.html","h":16,"kind":"work","label":"Circe Offering the Cup to Ulysses","title":"Circe Offering the Cup to Ulysses","artist":"John William Waterhouse","thumb":"h16_circe","text":"Circe Offering the Cup to Ulysses · John William Waterhouse · 1891 · Oil on canvas · Gallery Oldham, England · A Royal Academy painting; Waterhouse returned to enchantresses from myth throughout his career. · On the island of Aeaea the witch Circe turned Odysseus’s men into pigs. Hermes gave Odysseus a magic herb, moly, that protected him, and he forced Circe to restore his men; he then stayed with her for a year. Waterhouse puts the viewer in Odysseus’s place: Circe holds out the cup and wand, and Odysseus appears only as a reflection in the mirror behind her throne. · Circe turns men into pigs · Hermes’s herb, moly, protects Odysseus · Viewer stands in Odysseus’s place · Circe · Witch-goddess of Aeaea, daughter of Helios. · Moly · Magic herb from Hermes that protected Odysseus from Circe’s drugs."},{"id":"h16_sirensvase","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h16_sirensvase.html","h":16,"kind":"work","label":"Stamnos with Odysseus and the Sirens","title":"Stamnos with Odysseus and the Sirens","artist":"Siren Painter","thumb":"h16_sirensvase","text":"Stamnos with Odysseus and the Sirens · Siren Painter · Athenian, ca. 480–470 BCE · Red-figure terracotta · British Museum, London · A jar for mixing or storing wine, giving its name to the painter who decorated it. · After visiting the underworld, Odysseus sailed past the Sirens, whose song lured sailors to their deaths. He plugged his crew’s ears with wax and had himself tied to the mast so he could hear the song safely. The painter shows the Sirens as birds with women’s heads, one plunging toward the sea, perhaps in despair at her failure. · Sirens’ deadly song · Crew’s ears waxed; Odysseus tied to mast · Sirens as bird-women · Sirens · Bird-women whose irresistible song lured sailors onto the rocks."},{"id":"h16_sirenswh","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h16_sirenswh.html","h":16,"kind":"work","label":"Ulysses and the Sirens","title":"Ulysses and the Sirens","artist":"John William Waterhouse","thumb":"h16_sirenswh","text":"Ulysses and the Sirens · John William Waterhouse · 1891 · Oil on canvas · National Gallery of Victoria, Melbourne · A Royal Academy painting that deliberately revived the ancient bird-woman form of the Sirens, rather than the mermaids of later art. · Waterhouse copies the Greek vase type, filling the sky with bird-bodied Sirens swooping around the ship. Beyond them lay the narrow strait between Scylla, a six-headed monster who snatched six of the crew, and Charybdis, a whirlpool. On the island of Thrinacia the starving crew ate the sacred cattle of Helios, and Zeus destroyed the ship, leaving Odysseus alone. · Bird-Sirens circle the ship · Scylla and Charybdis · Cattle of Helios eaten; crew destroyed · Scylla and Charybdis · A six-headed monster and a whirlpool on either side of a narrow strait; “between Scylla and Charybdis” means between two dangers. · Cattle of Helios · Sacred cattle on Thrinacia; Eurylochus led the crew in eating them, and Zeus destroyed the ship."},{"id":"h16_calypso","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h16_calypso.html","h":16,"kind":"work","label":"Odysseus and Calypso","title":"Odysseus and Calypso","artist":"Arnold Böcklin","thumb":"h16_calypso","text":"Odysseus and Calypso · Arnold Böcklin · 1882–83 · Tempera on mahogany panel · Kunstmuseum Basel · A Symbolist painting for collectors, one of Böcklin’s moody mythological landscapes. · Odysseus was washed ashore on Ogygia, island of the nymph Calypso, who kept him for seven years and offered to make him immortal. He refused, longing for home and his mortal wife. Böcklin shows him as a dark, faceless figure staring out to sea from a rock while Calypso sits apart in red. The sea goddess Leucothea later saved him from a storm on his raft. · Seven years with Calypso · Refuses immortality for home · Saved at sea by Leucothea · Calypso · Nymph of Ogygia who kept Odysseus for seven years. · Leucothea · The former Theban princess Ino, now a sea goddess, who gave Odysseus her veil to save him."},{"id":"h16_nausicaa","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h16_nausicaa.html","h":16,"kind":"work","label":"Odysseus and Nausicaa","title":"Odysseus and Nausicaa","artist":"Pieter Lastman","thumb":"h16_nausicaa","text":"Odysseus and Nausicaa · Pieter Lastman · 1619 · Oil on panel · Alte Pinakothek, Munich · A history painting by Rembrandt’s teacher; its first owner is not recorded. · Shipwrecked and naked, Odysseus appeared before Princess Nausicaa and her maids as they washed clothes by the shore. Nausicaa kept calm and led him to her father’s palace, where the seafaring Phaeacians welcomed him, heard his story, and carried him home to Ithaca. Lastman shows the maids scattering in fright while the princess holds her ground. · Odysseus surprises Nausicaa · Phaeacians hear his tale · Their ship brings him home · Nausicaa · Phaeacian princess who helped Odysseus. · Phaeacians · Hospitable sea people who carried Odysseus to Ithaca."},{"id":"h16-8","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h16-8.html","h":16,"kind":"theme","label":"Wits over strength","title":"Wits over strength","artist":"","thumb":"h16_turner","text":"Wits over strength · Odysseus survives by cleverness, patience, and disguise. · Odysseus is “much-turning,” a hero of tricks and lies as well as courage. He wins with false names, wax, ropes, and patience, and he loses men whenever they lack self-control. The poem also judges every host: monsters like Polyphemus eat their guests, while the good Phaeacians give gifts. The rules of guest-friendship are the moral backbone of the Odyssey. · Tricks: “Nobody,” the mast, disguises · Self-control saves, appetite destroys · Hosts judged by how they treat guests · Metis · Cunning intelligence, Odysseus’s great gift (also the name of Athena’s mother)."},{"id":"h16_penelope","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h16_penelope.html","h":16,"kind":"work","label":"Penelope with the Suitors","title":"Penelope with the Suitors","artist":"Pinturicchio","thumb":"h16_penelope","text":"Penelope with the Suitors · Pinturicchio · ca. 1509 · Fresco, detached · National Gallery, London · Painted for a room in the Siena palace of Pandolfo Petrucci, ruler of the city, celebrating faithful wives. · At home, more than a hundred suitors had moved into Odysseus’s palace, eating his wealth and pressing Penelope to remarry. She promised to choose once she finished weaving a shroud for her father-in-law Laertes, but unraveled the work every night. Odysseus, disguised as a beggar and helped by his loyal swineherd Eumaeus and his son Telemachus, strung his great bow, which no suitor could bend, and killed them all. Pinturicchio shows Penelope at her loom as the suitors and the disguised Odysseus enter. · Suitors consume Odysseus’s house · Penelope’s weaving trick · Odysseus returns in disguise and takes revenge · Penelope · Odysseus’s faithful and clever wife. · Telemachus · Son of Odysseus and Penelope. · Eumaeus · Odysseus’s loyal swineherd. · Laertes · Odysseus’s aged father. · Suitors of Penelope · Young nobles who occupied the palace and were killed by Odysseus."},{"id":"h16-10","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h16-10.html","h":16,"kind":"theme","label":"The homecoming","title":"The homecoming","artist":"","thumb":"h16_calypso","text":"The homecoming · The Odyssey ends not in battle but in recognition. · Odysseus must be recognized by his old dog, his nurse, his son, his wife, and finally his father before he is truly home. Penelope tests him with the secret of their bed, built around a living olive tree. Her loyalty and cleverness match his, and later writers treated her as the model of a faithful wife, in contrast to Clytemnestra. · Recognitions, one by one · Penelope tests him with the bed · Faithful Penelope vs. Clytemnestra"},{"id":"h17-0","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/part-17.html","h":17,"kind":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"Aeneas and the Founding of Rome","artist":"","thumb":"h17_bernini","text":"Aeneas and the Founding of Rome · Virgil’s epic and Rome’s own legends · The Romans adopted the Greek gods but told their own foundation stories, and those stories were about duty, family, and the state. Virgil’s Aeneid, written for the emperor Augustus, makes the Trojan Aeneas the ancestor of Rome and of Augustus’s own family. Roman historians such as Livy then told of Romulus and Remus, the Sabine women, and the heroes of the early Republic. · Roman religion: spirits, duty, family · Aeneid: Troy to Italy, written for Augustus · Romulus, the Sabines, and the Republic · Numen (pl. numina) · Divine power or presence, often felt in places and objects. · Sacrificium · A sacred offering, the center of Roman worship. · Lares · Protective spirits of the household and crossroads. · Penates · Gods of the family storeroom (penus), carried from Troy by Aeneas. · Gens and familia · The clan sharing a family name, and the household under one father. · Paterfamilias and patria potestas · The male head of the household and his legal power over its members. · Genius and pietas · The life-spirit of a man or family, and the duty owed to gods, family, and country. · Dis · A Roman name for the god of the underworld. · Federico Barocci · Aeneas Fleeing Troy · 1598"},{"id":"h17_lararium","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h17_lararium.html","h":17,"kind":"work","label":"The Lararium of the House of the Vettii, Pompeii","title":"The Lararium of the House of the Vettii, Pompeii","artist":"Luigi Bazzani","thumb":"h17_lararium","text":"The Lararium of the House of the Vettii, Pompeii · Luigi Bazzani · 1895 · Watercolor of a household shrine of ca. 62–79 CE · A record painted soon after excavation; the shrine itself was where the household offered daily worship to its protective spirits. · Roman religion centered on the home. In this shrine the household Lares dance on either side of the Genius, the spirit of the head of the family, while a serpent below protects the house. Families offered food, wine, and incense here daily. Romans renamed Greek gods for their own: Asclepius became Aesculapius, Persephone Proserpina, Odysseus Ulysses, and Hades Dis or Pluto. · Lares flank the family Genius · Daily offerings at home · Greek gods given Roman names · Lararium · A household shrine for the Lares. · Aesculapius · The Roman name for Asclepius, god of medicine, brought to Rome in 291 BCE."},{"id":"h17_herculesvictor","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h17_herculesvictor.html","h":17,"kind":"work","label":"Temple of Hercules Victor","title":"Temple of Hercules Victor","artist":"","thumb":"h17_herculesvictor","text":"Temple of Hercules Victor · Late 2nd century BCE · Marble · Forum Boarium, Rome · A round temple in the cattle market beside the Tiber, near the Great Altar where Romans honored Hercules. · Romans told that Hercules, driving Geryon’s cattle back from Spain, stopped at the site of Rome, where the monster Cacus stole some of the herd. Hercules killed him, and King Evander set up the Ara Maxima, the Great Altar, in his honor. The cattle market, the Forum Boarium, grew up around it. This marble temple is one of the oldest surviving in Rome. · Hercules kills Cacus at Rome · Ara Maxima founded by Evander · Oldest surviving marble temple in Rome · Forum Boarium · Rome’s cattle market by the Tiber. · Ara Maxima · The Great Altar of Hercules in the Forum Boarium."},{"id":"h17_bernini","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h17_bernini.html","h":17,"kind":"work","label":"Aeneas Fleeing Troy","title":"Aeneas Fleeing Troy","artist":"Federico Barocci","thumb":"h17_bernini","text":"Aeneas Fleeing Troy · Federico Barocci · 1598 · Oil on canvas · Galleria Borghese, Rome · A second version of a painting Barocci first made for the emperor Rudolf II; the young Bernini knew it, and it shaped his own marble group of the subject. · Aeneas, son of Venus and the Trojan Anchises, escapes burning Troy carrying his old father, who holds the household gods, and leading his small son Ascanius, also called Iulus. Virgil wrote the Aeneid under Augustus, whose family, the Julii, claimed descent from Iulus. Barocci sets the three generations against the flames, with Aeneas’s wife Creusa following behind; she will be lost in the escape. The group is the image of Roman pietas. · Aeneas carries Anchises, leads Ascanius · Iulus: ancestor of the Julian family · Three generations = pietas · Aeneas · Trojan hero, son of Venus and Anchises, ancestor of the Romans. · Ascanius (Iulus) · Aeneas’s son, claimed ancestor of Julius Caesar and Augustus. · Augustus, Virgil, Livy, Ovid · Rome’s first emperor and the writers of his age who shaped Rome’s myths."},{"id":"h17_guerin","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h17_guerin.html","h":17,"kind":"work","label":"Aeneas Telling Dido of the Misfortunes of Troy","title":"Aeneas Telling Dido of the Misfortunes of Troy","artist":"Pierre-Narcisse Guérin","thumb":"h17_guerin","text":"Aeneas Telling Dido of the Misfortunes of Troy · Pierre-Narcisse Guérin · 1815 · Oil on canvas · Louvre, Paris · A Neoclassical history painting, staged like a scene from a play, that was a great success at the Paris Salon of 1817. · Aeneas wandered for years: to Thrace, where a bleeding bush warned him away; past the Harpies; to Epirus, where the seer Helenus guided him; and to Sicily, where he saw the blinded Polyphemus. Storms drove him to Carthage, newly founded by Queen Dido. As he told her the story of Troy, she fell in love with him, encouraged by Venus and Cupid, shown here as Ascanius on her lap. · Wanderings: Thrace, Harpies, Epirus, Sicily · Arrival at Carthage · Dido falls in love as he tells his story · Dido · Phoenician queen and founder of Carthage. · Helenus · Trojan prophet, son of Priam, ruling in Epirus."},{"id":"h17_guercino","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h17_guercino.html","h":17,"kind":"work","label":"The Death of Dido","title":"The Death of Dido","artist":"Guercino","thumb":"h17_guercino","text":"The Death of Dido · Guercino · 1631 · Oil on canvas · Galleria Spada, Rome · Commissioned by Cardinal Bernardino Spada as a gift for Maria de’ Medici, queen of France; when that fell through he bought it himself in 1631, and it remains in his family’s palace collection. · Jupiter sent Mercury to remind Aeneas that his destiny lay in Italy, and he left Carthage in secret. Dido built a pyre, cursed the Trojans, and stabbed herself with his sword. Her curse explained the later Punic Wars between Rome and Carthage. Guercino shows her collapsed on the pyre as her sister and attendants rush to her, while Aeneas’s ships sail away in the distance. · Aeneas leaves at Jupiter’s command · Dido’s curse and suicide · Origin story of the Punic Wars · Punic Wars · Three wars (264–146 BCE) between Rome and Carthage, which Dido’s curse was said to foreshadow."},{"id":"h17-6","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h17-6.html","h":17,"kind":"theme","label":"Duty before desire","title":"Duty before desire","artist":"","thumb":"h17_primaporta","text":"Duty before desire · Aeneas’s greatness lies in giving up what he wants for what he must do. · Unlike Achilles or Odysseus, Aeneas does not fight for personal glory or for his own home. He carries his father and his gods, abandons Dido, and fights a war in Italy because fate and the future Rome require it. Virgil makes that pietas the Roman virtue, and Augustus presented himself as Aeneas’s heir. The poem also mourns the people, like Dido, crushed along the way. · Pietas: duty to gods, family, country · Personal desire sacrificed to destiny · Augustus as Aeneas’s heir"},{"id":"h17_arapacis","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h17_arapacis.html","h":17,"kind":"work","label":"Ara Pacis Augustae: the sacrifice relief","title":"Ara Pacis Augustae: the sacrifice relief","artist":"","thumb":"h17_arapacis","text":"Ara Pacis Augustae: the sacrifice relief · 13–9 BCE · Marble · Museum of the Ara Pacis, Rome · historical photograph · A panel of the Altar of Augustan Peace, voted by the Senate to celebrate Augustus’s return from Spain and Gaul. · In Italy Aeneas, guided by the Cumaean Sibyl, visited the underworld, passing the Mourning Fields of tragic lovers and reaching Elysium, where Anchises showed him the future heroes of Rome. Landing in Latium, he found the sign of a white sow, allied with King Evander, and won Lavinia, daughter of King Latinus, by defeating the Rutulian prince Turnus. This relief shows a veiled man sacrificing a sow, traditionally read as Aeneas, though some scholars argue it is Rome’s second king, Numa. · Underworld: Mourning Fields to Elysium · White sow, Evander, Latinus, Lavinia · Aeneas defeats Turnus · Latinus and Lavinia · King of Latium and his daughter, whom Aeneas married. · Turnus · Rutulian prince and Aeneas’s rival, killed at the end of the Aeneid. · Lavinium and Alba Longa · The town Aeneas founded and the city founded by Ascanius."},{"id":"h17_primaporta","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h17_primaporta.html","h":17,"kind":"work","label":"Augustus of Prima Porta","title":"Augustus of Prima Porta","artist":"","thumb":"h17_primaporta","text":"Augustus of Prima Porta · Early 1st century CE, after a bronze of ca. 20 BCE · Marble · Vatican Museums · photograph ca. 1875–1900 · A statue found in 1863 at the villa of Augustus’s wife Livia, presenting the emperor as a commander and a near-divine leader. · Augustus stands like a Greek statue, addressing his troops, his breastplate showing the return of Roman standards from Parthia. At his feet a small Cupid rides a dolphin, a reminder that the Julian family descended from Venus through Aeneas. The statue puts Virgil’s myth into stone: the emperor as the destined heir of Troy. · Emperor as general and orator · Cupid on a dolphin: descent from Venus · Myth used as imperial propaganda"},{"id":"h17_wolf","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h17_wolf.html","h":17,"kind":"work","label":"Capitoline Wolf","title":"Capitoline Wolf","artist":"","thumb":"h17_wolf","text":"Capitoline Wolf · Date debated (traditionally 5th century BCE; twins added ca. 1471–1509) · Bronze · Capitoline Museums, Rome · The symbol of Rome; the twins were added in the Renaissance when the wolf was given to the people of Rome. · In Livy’s history, King Numitor of Alba Longa was overthrown by his brother Amulius, who forced Numitor’s daughter Rhea Silvia to become a Vestal Virgin. Mars fathered twins with her anyway, and Amulius had them thrown into the Tiber. A she-wolf nursed them until the shepherd Faustulus and his wife Larentia raised them; lupa, “wolf,” was also slang for a prostitute, which may explain the story. The wolf’s date is now disputed, with some tests suggesting it was cast in the Middle Ages. · Romulus and Remus, sons of Mars and Rhea Silvia · Nursed by a she-wolf · The wolf’s date is debated · Livy, Ab urbe condita · Livy’s history of Rome “from the founding of the city.” · Numitor and Amulius · Rival brothers, kings of Alba Longa; Numitor was the twins’ grandfather. · Faustulus and Larentia · The shepherd and his wife who raised the twins."},{"id":"h17_romulus","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h17_romulus.html","h":17,"kind":"work","label":"Romulus and Remus","title":"Romulus and Remus","artist":"Peter Paul Rubens","thumb":"h17_romulus","text":"Romulus and Remus · Peter Paul Rubens · ca. 1615–16 · Oil on canvas · Capitoline Museums, Rome · Its first owner is not recorded; it is now in the museum on the Capitoline Hill, the heart of ancient Rome. · Rubens paints the she-wolf licking one twin while the river god Tiber reclines nearby and the shepherd Faustulus arrives. Grown up, the twins restored their grandfather and founded a new city, but in a quarrel Romulus killed Remus and named Rome for himself. After his death he was said to have been taken up to heaven and worshipped as the god Quirinus. · She-wolf, Tiber, and Faustulus · Romulus kills Remus, founds Rome · Romulus deified as Quirinus · Quirinus · The god Romulus became after his death. · Apotheosis · Deification; Roman emperors were declared divus (divine) after death. · Triumph · A victory parade through Rome in which the triumphant general dressed as Jupiter."},{"id":"h17_sabine","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h17_sabine.html","h":17,"kind":"work","label":"The Abduction of the Sabine Women","title":"The Abduction of the Sabine Women","artist":"Nicolas Poussin","thumb":"h17_sabine","text":"The Abduction of the Sabine Women · Nicolas Poussin · ca. 1633–34 · Oil on canvas · Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Owned in the 1630s by the French ambassador to Rome and later by Cardinal Richelieu; it is staged like an ancient relief among classical buildings. · Rome had too few women, so Romulus invited the neighboring Sabines to a festival and, at his signal, the Romans seized their daughters. Later the Sabine king Tatius attacked, helped by the traitor Tarpeia, who opened the citadel and was crushed under the Sabines’ shields. The Sabine women, led by Romulus’s wife Hersilia, ran between the armies and made peace, and the two peoples merged. Poussin shows Romulus giving the signal from the steps at left. · Romans seize the Sabine women · Tarpeia’s betrayal · The women make peace · Sabine women · Women seized by the first Romans who later reconciled Rome and the Sabines. · Tarpeia · Roman girl who betrayed the citadel and was killed; traitors were thrown from the Tarpeian Rock. · Hersilia and Tatius · Romulus’s Sabine wife, and the Sabine king who later shared rule with him."},{"id":"h17_horatii","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h17_horatii.html","h":17,"kind":"work","label":"The Oath of the Horatii","title":"The Oath of the Horatii","artist":"Jacques-Louis David","thumb":"h17_horatii","text":"The Oath of the Horatii · Jacques-Louis David · 1784–85 · Oil on canvas · Louvre, Paris · Commissioned by the French crown; its message of patriotic sacrifice was later read as a call to revolution. · In the war between Rome and Alba Longa, the two cities let three brothers from each side, the Roman Horatii and the Alban Curiatii, fight for them. David shows the Horatii swearing on swords held by their father, while the women, one engaged to a Curiatius, collapse in grief. Only one Horatius survived; when his sister mourned her dead fiancé, he killed her too. · Three brothers fight for Rome · Oath on their father’s swords · Patriotism above family · Horatii and Curiatii · Roman and Alban triplets whose combat decided the war."},{"id":"h17_lucretia","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h17_lucretia.html","h":17,"kind":"work","label":"Lucretia","title":"Lucretia","artist":"Rembrandt","thumb":"h17_lucretia","text":"Lucretia · Rembrandt · 1664 · Oil on canvas · National Gallery of Art, Washington · A late painting focused on a single, tragic figure; its first owner is not recorded. · Sextus Tarquinius, son of Rome’s last king, raped the virtuous Lucretia, wife of Tarquinius Collatinus. She told her husband and father what had happened and stabbed herself. Lucius Junius Brutus swore on her blood to drive out the kings, and in 509 BCE the Romans founded the Republic. Rembrandt shows her in the moment before the blow, dagger raised, face full of sorrow. · Lucretia’s rape and suicide · Brutus’s oath · Kings expelled; Republic founded · Lucretia · Roman wife whose death led to the overthrow of the monarchy. · Brutus and Collatinus · Leaders of the revolt and the first consuls of the Republic."},{"id":"h17-14","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h17-14.html","h":17,"kind":"theme","label":"Myth as Roman history","title":"Myth as Roman history","artist":"","thumb":"h17_horatii","text":"Myth as Roman history · Rome’s legends taught citizens what Rome expected of them. · Roman stories are set in Rome itself and told as history. They praise self-sacrifice for the state, punish betrayal, and often turn on the bodies of women, the Sabines, Tarpeia, Lucretia, whose suffering changes the course of politics. Later revolutions, from France to America, borrowed these legends as models of civic virtue. · Told as history, set in Rome · Duty and sacrifice for the state · Women’s fates drive political change"},{"id":"h18-0","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/part-18.html","h":18,"kind":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"Interpreting Myth","artist":"","thumb":"h18_primavera","text":"Interpreting Myth · How people have tried to explain what myths mean · Almost as soon as myths were written down, people began asking what they really meant. Greek philosophers read them as hidden science or moral lessons, Christians as pagan echoes of the Bible, and modern scholars as ritual, language, psychology, social structure, or politics. Each theory reveals something, and each age’s favorite theory says as much about that age as about the myths. · Ancient: allegory and history · Christian and Renaissance: moral and spiritual meanings · Modern: ritual, language, mind, structure, context · Allegory · Reading a story as a coded message about something else. · Symbolism · Treating figures and objects in a myth as signs for ideas. · Sandro Botticelli · Primavera · ca. 1480"},{"id":"h18-1","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h18-1.html","h":18,"kind":"text","label":"Greek theories","title":"Greek theories","artist":"","thumb":null,"text":"Greek theories · Greek thinkers were the first critics of myth. Some philosophers, offended by stories of gods behaving badly, read the myths as hidden truths about nature or ethics. Others treated them as distorted history. · Myth as science, history, and morality · Myths contain hidden philosophical or historical truth · Gods as forces of nature or virtues · Gods as deified ancient kings · Philosophical or historical truth · The view that myths preserve real knowledge or real events in disguised form. · Physical allegory · Reading gods as natural forces: Zeus as sky, Hera as air, Hephaestus as fire. · Etymology · Explaining a god’s nature from his or her name, a favorite method of Stoic writers. · Euhemerism · The theory of Euhemerus (ca. 300 BCE) that gods were great human kings later worshipped. · Moral allegory · Reading myths as lessons in virtue and vice, such as Heracles at the crossroads."},{"id":"h18_ovide","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h18_ovide.html","h":18,"kind":"work","label":"Ovide moralisé en prose","title":"Ovide moralisé en prose","artist":"","thumb":"h18_ovide","text":"Ovide moralisé en prose · 15th century · Illuminated manuscript · Bibliothèque nationale de France, Paris · A French prose version of a moralized Ovid, made for an aristocratic reader who wanted the stories with their Christian meaning. · Medieval Christians kept reading Ovid by treating his myths as allegories of Christian truth. The long French poem called the Ovide moralisé (about 1300) retells the Metamorphoses and then explains each story: Phaethon’s fall stands for pride, Orpheus can stand for Christ. Prose versions like this one carried the method into the 1400s, and it shaped how Renaissance artists understood classical subjects. · Ovid retold with Christian morals · About 1300; prose versions later · Kept myth alive in the Middle Ages · Ovide moralisé · French medieval retelling of Ovid with Christian allegorical explanations. · Alchemy · Medieval and Renaissance search for transforming metals, which read myths as coded recipes."},{"id":"h18_primavera","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h18_primavera.html","h":18,"kind":"work","label":"Primavera","title":"Primavera","artist":"Sandro Botticelli","thumb":"h18_primavera","text":"Primavera · Sandro Botticelli · ca. 1480 · Tempera on panel · Uffizi Gallery, Florence · Made for the Medici family, probably for a room in the Florence palace of Lorenzo di Pierfrancesco de’ Medici, possibly for a wedding. · Venus stands in her garden with Cupid above, flanked by the Three Graces, Mercury, Flora, and the west wind Zephyr seizing the nymph Chloris. Many scholars read it through the Neoplatonism of the Florentine philosopher Marsilio Ficino, who taught that earthly beauty and love lead the soul upward toward the divine. Whatever its exact meaning, it shows how Renaissance thinkers treated myth as philosophy. · Venus, Graces, Mercury, Flora, Zephyr · Neoplatonic reading: love leads upward · Myth as philosophy in Medici Florence · Neoplatonism · A philosophy, revived in Renaissance Florence, that saw earthly beauty as a reflection of divine beauty."},{"id":"h18-4","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h18-4.html","h":18,"kind":"text","label":"From the Enlightenment to anthropology","title":"From the Enlightenment to anthropology","artist":"","thumb":null,"text":"From the Enlightenment to anthropology · Enlightenment thinkers saw myths as the mistakes of primitive minds, while Romantics prized them as the poetry of nations. In the 1800s and 1900s, scholars compared myths around the world and linked them to rituals and social customs. · Myth as superstition, poetry, and ritual · Enlightenment: myths as early, mistaken science · Romantics: myths as national poetry and imagination · Anthropology: myths tied to ritual and society · Enlightenment theories · 18th-century views of myth as superstition from humanity’s childhood. · Romantic theories · 19th-century views of myth as the creative expression of a people’s spirit. · Ritual theory · The idea that myths grew out of rituals and explain them (James Frazer, Jane Harrison). · Charter theory · Bronisław Malinowski’s idea that myths justify a society’s customs and institutions."},{"id":"h18_helios","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h18_helios.html","h":18,"kind":"work","label":"Aurora","title":"Aurora","artist":"Guido Reni","thumb":"h18_helios","text":"Aurora · Guido Reni · 1612–14 · Fresco · Casino dell’Aurora, Palazzo Pallavicini-Rospigliosi, Rome · A ceiling fresco for the garden pavilion of Cardinal Scipione Borghese’s palace in Rome. · The sun god drives his chariot across the sky, led by Aurora, the dawn, scattering flowers, and circled by the dancing Hours. In the 1800s the philologist Max Müller argued that most myths began as poetic descriptions of the sun, dawn, and sky, misunderstood as language changed, so that a story like Daphne fleeing Apollo meant the dawn fleeing the sun. Solar mythology faded, but comparing myths across Indo-European languages remained influential, notably in Georges Dumézil’s theory of three social functions. · Max Müller: myths as misunderstood sun poetry · Comparing Indo-European myths · Dumézil: priests, warriors, producers · Solar mythology · Max Müller’s theory that myths began as descriptions of the sun and sky. · Indo-European comparative mythology · Comparing myths of peoples with related languages to reconstruct a shared ancestor. · Linguistic theories · Explanations of myth from the history of words and language."},{"id":"h18_moreau","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h18_moreau.html","h":18,"kind":"work","label":"Oedipus and the Sphinx","title":"Oedipus and the Sphinx","artist":"Gustave Moreau","thumb":"h18_moreau","text":"Oedipus and the Sphinx · Gustave Moreau · 1864 · Oil on canvas · Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The painting that made Moreau famous at the Paris Salon of 1864, an early landmark of Symbolism. · Moreau’s Sphinx clings to Oedipus’s chest, a beautiful and deadly woman locked in a staring match with the hero, above the remains of earlier victims. Within a few decades Sigmund Freud would read the Oedipus myth as a key to the mind, and Carl Jung would see myths as expressions of a shared unconscious. Psychology gave myth a new meaning: stories about the hidden self. · Symbolist femme fatale and hero · Freud: the Oedipus complex · Jung: archetypes and the collective unconscious · Psychological allegory · Reading myth as a picture of inner, mental conflict. · Condensation and displacement · Freud’s terms for how dreams, and myths, compress and shift meaning. · Oedipus complex · Freud’s theory of a child’s desire for one parent and rivalry with the other. · Electra complex · A term (from Jung) for the female version of the Oedipus complex. · Collective unconscious and archetypes · Jung’s idea of a shared layer of the mind containing universal patterns, such as the hero or the great mother."},{"id":"h18-7","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h18-7.html","h":18,"kind":"text","label":"Structure and context","title":"Structure and context","artist":"","thumb":null,"text":"Structure and context · In the 20th century, structuralists looked for the hidden logic that organizes myths, while other scholars insisted on reading each myth in its historical and social setting, including how it treats women. · Myth as a system of signs, and as a product of its time · Structuralism: myths as systems of opposites · Paris School: myth within Greek society and ritual · Contextual and feminist readings · Structuralism · Claude Lévi-Strauss’s method of reading myths as patterns of opposites (nature/culture, raw/cooked) that a culture tries to resolve. · Semiotics: signifier and signified · The study of signs: the signifier is the word or image, the signified the idea it stands for. · Paris School of myth criticism · Jean-Pierre Vernant, Marcel Detienne, and Pierre Vidal-Naquet, who read Greek myth through Greek society, politics, and sacrifice. · Programs of action · The idea that myths model how people should act in their society. · Feminist criticism · Reading myths for how they define and control women, and recovering women’s perspectives."},{"id":"h18-8","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/h18-8.html","h":18,"kind":"theme","label":"Why myths last","title":"Why myths last","artist":"","thumb":"h18_moreau","text":"Why myths last · Myths survive because every age can find itself in them. · No single theory explains all myths. A story like Oedipus can be ritual, history, psychology, and politics at once, and artists from Athenian vase painters to Moreau have each found something new in it. That openness is why these ancient stories keep returning in art, literature, and film. · No single key to all myths · Each age reads itself into them · Endless reinterpretation in art"},{"id":"sources","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/sources.html","h":99,"kind":"sources","label":"Sources","title":"Sources","artist":"","thumb":null,"text":"Sources"},{"id":"credits","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-proto/credits.html","h":99,"kind":"credits","label":"Image credits","title":"Image credits","artist":"","thumb":null,"text":"Image credits"}]};
